"use strict";(self.webpackChunkcasdoor_website=self.webpackChunkcasdoor_website||[]).push([["41741"],{96484(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"captcha-for-login-pages","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/captcha-for-login-pages","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-captcha-for-login-pages.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-captcha-for-login-pages.mdx","title":"CAPTCHA-Anbieter f\xfcr Login-Seiten im Vergleich: reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest und Alibaba Cloud in Casdoor","description":"Vergleich von reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Cloudflare Turnstile, GeeTest, Alibaba Cloud Captcha und dem integrierten CAPTCHA f\xfcr Login- und Registrierungsseiten sowie deren Verwendung in Casdoor.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CAPTCHA","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"reCAPTCHA","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/re-captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"hCaptcha","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/h-captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"Turnstile","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/turnstile"},{"inline":true,"label":"GeeTest","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/gee-test"},{"inline":true,"label":"Alibaba Cloud","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/alibaba-cloud"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":8.05,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"captcha-for-login-pages","title":"CAPTCHA-Anbieter f\xfcr Login-Seiten im Vergleich: reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest und Alibaba Cloud in Casdoor","description":"Vergleich von reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Cloudflare Turnstile, GeeTest, Alibaba Cloud Captcha und dem integrierten CAPTCHA f\xfcr Login- und Registrierungsseiten sowie deren Verwendung in Casdoor.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CAPTCHA","reCAPTCHA","hCaptcha","Turnstile","GeeTest","Alibaba Cloud","Security"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Customize the Login Page in Casdoor: Custom Styling of the Authentication UI Without Forking","permalink":"/de/blog/customize-login-page"}},"content":"F\xfcr die meisten Anmelde- und Registrierungssseiten im globalen Internet ist Cloudflare Turnstile die einfachste Wahl: Der kostenlose Plan hat keine Obergrenze f\xfcr Challenges, und der Standardmodus zeigt nur risikoreichen Besuchern ein Checkbox an. Wenn sich viele Ihrer Nutzer in Festlandchina befinden, verwenden Sie stattdessen Alibaba Cloud Captcha oder GeeTest, da Cloudflare angibt, dass Turnstile dort nicht unterst\xfctzt wird. Wenn das CAPTCHA ohne einen externen Dienst funktionieren muss, verwenden Sie das integrierte Bild-CAPTCHA von Casdoor. Casdoor unterst\xfctzt alle diese Anbieter sowie Google reCAPTCHA (v2 und v3) und hCaptcha als [CAPTCHA-Anbieter](/docs/provider/captcha/overview), und Sie legen fest, wann das CAPTCHA pro Anwendung angezeigt wird.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Der Vergleich {#the-comparison}\\n\\nDie unten genannten Preise und Verf\xfcgbarkeiten wurden am 2026-11-02 anhand der Websites der jeweiligen Anbieter \xfcberpr\xfcft. Wo ein Anbieter keine Zahl ver\xf6ffentlicht, weist die Tabelle darauf hin, statt zu raten.\\n\\n| Anbieter | Was der Benutzer sieht | Unsichtbarer Modus | Daten und Datenschutz | Kostenlose Stufe und Preise | Festlandchina | Casdoor-Typ |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Casdoor Standard | F\xfcnf Ziffern aus einem Bild eingeben | Nein | Generiert und gepr\xfcft durch Ihren Casdoor-Server; kein Drittanbieter | Kostenlos, Teil von Casdoor | Funktioniert, kein externer Aufruf | `Default` |\\n| Google reCAPTCHA v2 | Checkbox; einige Benutzer erhalten eine Bild-Challenge | v2 hat auch eine unsichtbare Variante, die nur verd\xe4chtigen Traffic challenge-t | Google; setzt den `_GRECAPTCHA`-Cookie zur Risikoanalyse | Essentials-Stufe kostenlos bis zu 10.000 Bewertungen pro Monat pro Organisation; Premium kostet pauschal $8 bis zu 100.000, danach $1 pro 1.000 | Google empfiehlt die Verwendung von `www.recaptcha.net`, wenn `www.google.com` nicht erreichbar ist | `reCAPTCHA`, `reCAPTCHA v2` (Checkbox) |\\n| Google reCAPTCHA v3 | Nichts; gibt einen Score zur\xfcck | Ja | Wie bei v2 | Wie bei v2 | Wie bei v2 | `reCAPTCHA v3` |\\n| hCaptcha | Checkbox mit Bild-Challenges im kostenlosen Plan | Passiver Modus bei Pro (\\"99,9 % passiv\\") und ein No-CAPTCHA-Modus bei Enterprise | Gibt an, dass der kostenlose Plan mit GDPR, CCPA, LGPD und PIPL konform ist; zertifiziert nach dem EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework | Basic ist kostenlos; Pro kostet $139 pro Monat (oder $99 pro Monat bei j\xe4hrlicher Abrechnung) mit 100.000 Bewertungen, danach $0,99 pro 1.000; Enterprise \xfcber den Vertrieb | Nicht dokumentiert von hCaptcha | `hCaptcha` |\\n| Cloudflare Turnstile | Managed-Modus zeigt eine Checkbox nur bei Bedarf | Non-interaktive und Invisible-Widget-Modi | Cloudflare gibt an, dass nur streng notwendige Daten verarbeitet werden und Formulareintr\xe4ge nicht gelesen werden; kein Cloudflare-Proxy erforderlich | Free-Plan: unbegrenzte Challenges, bis zu 20 Widgets; Enterprise \xfcber den Vertrieb | Cloudflare: \\"nicht unterst\xfctzt in Festlandchina\\" | `Cloudflare Turnstile` |\\n| GeeTest CAPTCHA v4 | Ein-Klick, Schiebepuzzle, Textklick, Match-Three | Nicht auf der v4-Produktseite aufgelistet | Chinesischer Anbieter; produktspezifische Datenschutzerkl\xe4rung | Keine \xf6ffentliche Preisliste; 30-t\xe4gige kostenlose Testphase, danach Vertrieb | Chinesischer Anbieter | `GEETEST` |\\n| Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 | Ein-Klick, Slider, Puzzle, Bildwiederherstellung | Traceless-Modus in den Editionen Standard und Enterprise | L\xe4uft auf Alibaba Cloud | Pay-as-you-go: 0,005 CNY pro Aufruf in Festlandchina, 0,007 CNY au\xdferhalb; Abonnement-Editionen und Ressourcenpakete ebenfalls verf\xfcgbar | Ja; Casdoor verifiziert gegen den Endpunkt der Shanghai-Region | `Aliyun Captcha` |\\n\\nZwei Hinweise zu den reCAPTCHA-Zeilen. Google hat reCAPTCHA in Google Cloud Fraud Defense integriert, mit Essentials-, Premium- und Enterprise-Stufen, und das kostenlose Kontingent von 10.000 Bewertungen wird \xfcber die gesamte Google-Cloud-Organisation hinweg geb\xfcndelt. Casdoor kommuniziert mit reCAPTCHA \xfcber einen Site Key und Secret Key \xfcber den Standard-`siteverify`-Endpunkt und l\xe4dt das Skript von `recaptcha.net` anstelle von `google.com`, sowohl f\xfcr das Browser-Skript als auch f\xfcr die serverseitige Verifizierung.\\n\\n## Welchen w\xe4hlen? {#which-one-to-pick}\\n\\n### Globales SaaS {#global-saas}\\n\\nBeginnen Sie mit [Cloudflare Turnstile](/docs/provider/captcha/cloudflareTurnstile). Der kostenlose Plan misst keine Challenges, der Managed-Modus verhindert, dass die meisten Benutzer klicken m\xfcssen, und Sie m\xfcssen Ihre Website nicht \xfcber Cloudflare leiten. Wenn Sie bereits auf Google Cloud laufen und die Risikosignale von reCAPTCHA nutzen m\xf6chten, ist [reCAPTCHA](/docs/provider/captcha/recaptcha) in Ordnung, aber beachten Sie die 10.000 kostenlosen Bewertungen: Eine stark frequentierte Anmeldeseite oder ein SMS-Pumping-Angriff kann dieses Kontingent an einem Tag aussch\xf6pfen. [hCaptcha](/docs/provider/captcha/hcaptcha) ist die andere \xfcbliche Wahl; sein kostenloser Plan zeigt immer eine Challenge an, und die passiven Modi sind kostenpflichtig.\\n\\n### Eine App mit Nutzern in Festlandchina {#an-app-with-users-in-mainland-china}\\n\\nTurnstile f\xe4llt aus, laut Cloudflares eigener Dokumentation. F\xfcr reCAPTCHA dokumentiert Google `www.recaptcha.net` als die Domain, die verwendet werden soll, wenn `www.google.com` nicht erreichbar ist, und Casdoor nutzt dies bereits, aber Sie sollten dennoch von den Netzwerken Ihrer Benutzer aus testen, bevor Sie sich darauf verlassen. hCaptcha ver\xf6ffentlicht keine Aussage \xfcber China, daher gilt derselbe Rat.\\n\\nDie zwei sicheren Optionen sind [Alibaba Cloud Captcha](/docs/provider/captcha/aliyunCaptcha) und [GeeTest](/docs/provider/captcha/geetest). Alibaba Cloud ver\xf6ffentlicht Preise pro Aufruf und erm\xf6glicht den Start mit Pay-as-you-go; sein Traceless-Modus (in Casdoor verwenden Sie daf\xfcr den `Popup`-Subtyp) kommt dem unsichtbaren Erlebnis von Turnstile nahe. GeeTest verkauft \xfcber sein Vertriebsteam nach einer 30-t\xe4gigen Testphase. Wenn Sie sowohl China als auch den Rest der Welt von einer Casdoor-Instanz bedienen, denken Sie daran, dass eine Anwendung nur einen CAPTCHA-Anbieter hat, also teilen Sie die Zielgruppen in zwei Anwendungen auf, wenn sie unterschiedliche CAPTCHAs ben\xf6tigen.\\n\\n### Datenschutzbedenkliche Nutzer in der EU {#privacy-sensitive-users-in-the-eu}\\n\\nJeder gehostete Captcha-Dienst sendet einige Daten \xfcber den Browser des Besuchers an den Anbieter. Wenn Ihr Rechtsbereich keine Drittanbieter im Anmeldeprozess zulassen m\xf6chte, ist das Casdoor-[Standard-Captcha](/docs/provider/captcha/default) die einzige Option in der Tabelle, bei der alles auf Ihrem eigenen Server bleibt. Unter den gehosteten Diensten beschreiben sowohl Turnstile als auch hCaptcha Datenschutz als Teil des Produkts (Daten beschr\xe4nkt auf das, was f\xfcr die \xdcberpr\xfcfung erforderlich ist; jeweils DSGVO bzw. EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework). reCAPTCHA setzt ein `_GRECAPTCHA`-Cookie; Google weist darauf hin, dass die Auslieferung von `www.recaptcha.net` andere Cookies vermeidet, die `www.google.com` m\xf6glicherweise setzt. Welchen Dienst Sie auch w\xe4hlen, f\xfchren Sie ihn in Ihrer Datenschutzerkl\xe4rung auf.\\n\\n### Selbst gehostet, Intranet oder offline {#self-hosted-intranet-or-offline}\\n\\nVerwenden Sie das Standard-Captcha. Es ben\xf6tigt keine Schl\xfcssel und keinen ausgehenden Netzwerkzugriff, funktioniert also auch in Air-Gapped-Installationen. Kombinieren Sie es mit der Regel `Internet-Only` (unten), damit Personen im B\xfcro-Netzwerk es nie sehen, w\xe4hrend Anfragen aus dem \xf6ffentlichen Internet weiterhin betroffen sind.\\n\\nDas Standard-Captcha ist in dieser Gruppe am schw\xe4chsten gegen automatisierte Solver, da es ein einfaches verzerrtes Ziffernbild ist. F\xfcr eine \xf6ffentliche Anmelde-Seite mit hohem Traffic kombinieren Sie es mit [Multi-Faktor-Authentifizierung](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/mfa-methods-compared) oder wechseln Sie Benutzer zu [Passwortloser Anmeldung](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/passwordless-authentication), was das Raten von Passw\xf6rtern eliminiert, gegen das Captchas entwickelt wurden.\\n\\n## Wie CAPTCHA in Casdoor funktioniert {#how-captcha-works-in-casdoor}\\n\\nSie f\xfcgen einen Provider hinzu, dessen **Category** auf `Captcha` gesetzt ist, und w\xe4hlen einen dieser Typen: `Default`, `reCAPTCHA`, `reCAPTCHA v2`, `reCAPTCHA v3`, `hCaptcha`, `Cloudflare Turnstile`, `GEETEST` oder `Aliyun Captcha`. Dann f\xfcgen Sie ihn einer Anwendung im Tab **Providers** hinzu. Eine Anwendung kann nur einen Captcha-Provider enthalten; Casdoor verweigert das Speichern eines zweiten. Einrichtungsschritte f\xfcr jeden Anbieter finden Sie in der [Dokumentation der Captcha-Provider](/docs/provider/captcha/overview).\\n\\n### Wo Casdoor danach fragt {#where-casdoor-asks-for-it}\\n\\nDerselbe Provider sch\xfctzt mehrere Aktionen, und Casdoor pr\xfcft das Token serverseitig, nicht nur im Browser:\\n\\n- Anmeldung mit Passwort.\\n- Absenden des Registrierungsformulars.\\n- Senden eines Verifizierungscodes per E-Mail oder SMS, was den Code-Schritt bei der Registrierung, die Seite zum Zur\xfccksetzen des Passworts und die anmeldungsbasierte Code-Eingabe abdeckt. Dies ist der Schutz gegen Bots, die Ihr SMS-Budget verbrauchen.\\n\\n### Wann Casdoor danach fragt {#when-casdoor-asks-for-it}\\n\\nDie **Rule** des Captcha-Providers in der Anwendung entscheidet, wann er erscheint:\\n\\n| Rule         | Behavior                                                               |\\n|---|---|\\n| `None` | Nie; das Captcha ist deaktiviert |\\n| `Dynamic` | Erst nachdem die fehlgeschlagenen Anmeldungen des Benutzers das **Failed signin limit** der Anwendung erreicht haben |\\n| `Always` | Jedes Mal |\\n| `Internet-Only` | F\xfcr Anfragen von \xf6ffentlichen IP-Adressen; nicht f\xfcr private, Loopback- oder andere nicht-\xf6ffentliche Adressen |\\n\\n`Dynamic` ist die schonendste Einstellung f\xfcr die Anmeldung: Ehrliche Benutzer sehen nie eine Challenge, und jemand, der Passw\xf6rter r\xe4t, trifft nach einigen Fehlversuchen auf eine. Sie gilt jedoch nur f\xfcr Konten, die bereits existieren. Eine Registrierung oder ein Code, der an eine Adresse ohne Konto gesendet wird, l\xf6st sie nicht aus. Wenn Missbrauch von SMS oder E-Mails bei der Registrierung Ihr Anliegen ist, verwenden Sie `Always` oder `Internet-Only`.\\n\\n### Wo es auf der Seite erscheint {#where-it-appears-on-the-page}\\n\\nBei den Anmeldeelementen der Anwendung hat das Element **Captcha** seine eigene Regel: `Pop up` zeigt das Captcha in einem Dialog an, wenn es ben\xf6tigt wird, und `Inline` platziert es direkt auf der Seite. Siehe [\xc4ndern des Captchas](/docs/application/signin-items-table#captcha-rules). Alibaba Cloud Captcha hat einen zus\xe4tzlichen **Sub type**: `Embed` zeigt es innerhalb des Casdoor-Dialogs an, und `Popup` \xfcbergibt die Kontrolle an den eigenen Popup-Dialog von Alibaba Cloud, was f\xfcr traceless Szenarien erforderlich ist.\\n\\nVerwenden Sie die Schaltfl\xe4che **Preview** auf der Provider-Seite, um die Schl\xfcssel zu \xfcberpr\xfcfen, bevor Sie die Regel aktivieren.\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- Google reCAPTCHA Versionen: https://developers.google.com/recaptcha/docs/versions\\n- Google reCAPTCHA FAQ (`recaptcha.net`, `_GRECAPTCHA` Cookie): https://developers.google.com/recaptcha/docs/faq\\n- reCAPTCHA Abrechnung in Google Cloud Fraud Defense (gepr\xfcft am 2026-11-02): https://docs.cloud.google.com/recaptcha/docs/billing-information\\n- hCaptcha Preise (gepr\xfcft am 2026-11-02): https://www.hcaptcha.com/pricing\\n- hCaptcha Datenschutzerkl\xe4rung: https://www.hcaptcha.com/privacy\\n- Cloudflare Turnstile \xdcbersicht: https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/\\n- Cloudflare Turnstile Pl\xe4ne (gepr\xfcft am 2026-11-02): https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/plans/\\n- Cloudflare China Network FAQ (Turnstile in Festlandchina): https://developers.cloudflare.com/china-network/faq/\\n- GeeTest CAPTCHA v4 (gepr\xfcft am 2026-11-02): https://www.geetest.com/adaptive-captcha\\n- Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 \xdcbersicht: https://help.aliyun.com/zh/captcha/captcha2-0/product-overview/what-is-alibaba-cloud-captcha-2\\n- Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 Editionen und Preise (gepr\xfcft am 2026-11-02): https://help.aliyun.com/zh/captcha/captcha2-0/product-overview/version-description"},{"id":"customize-login-page","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/customize-login-page","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-customize-login-page.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-customize-login-page.mdx","title":"Customize the Login Page in Casdoor: Custom Styling of the Authentication UI Without Forking","description":"Style Casdoor\'s hosted login and sign-up pages without forking: themes, CSS, HTML, logo, background, form position, sign-in fields and organization choice.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Login Page","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/login-page"},{"inline":true,"label":"UI Customization","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/ui-customization"},{"inline":true,"label":"CSS","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/css"},{"inline":true,"label":"Themes","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/themes"},{"inline":true,"label":"Branding","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/branding"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":9.27,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"customize-login-page","title":"Customize the Login Page in Casdoor: Custom Styling of the Authentication UI Without Forking","description":"Style Casdoor\'s hosted login and sign-up pages without forking: themes, CSS, HTML, logo, background, form position, sign-in fields and organization choice.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Login Page","UI Customization","CSS","Themes","Branding","IAM","SSO"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"CAPTCHA-Anbieter f\xfcr Login-Seiten im Vergleich: reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest und Alibaba Cloud in Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/captcha-for-login-pages"},"nextItem":{"title":"Jenkins OIDC, Gitea OIDC and Grafana SSO with Casdoor: One Login for a Self-Hosted DevOps Stack","permalink":"/de/blog/devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana"}},"content":"Yes, Casdoor supports custom styling of its authentication UI, and you don\'t need to fork the frontend to get it. Every application has a **UI Customization** tab where you set the logo, favicon, background image, custom CSS for desktop and mobile, the position of the form, a side panel, footer HTML and a theme. Two tables, **Signin items** and **Signup items**, decide which fields the login and sign-up pages show, in which order, with which labels and placeholders. All of it is stored per application in the database, so one Casdoor instance can serve many differently branded login pages, and organization admins can edit their own pages within safe limits.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What to change and where\\n\\n| What you want to change | Where in Casdoor | Docs |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Primary color, corner radius, dark or compact style | **Theme** of the organization, or of the application with **Follow organization theme** off | [Customize the theme](/docs/organization/customize-theme) |\\n| A complete ready-made look | **Identity \u2192 Marketplace**, sign-in themes | [Marketplace](/docs/marketplace/overview) |\\n| Logo, dark logo, favicon, page title | Application edit page | [Application settings](/docs/application/terminology) |\\n| Background image (desktop and phone) | **Background URL**, **Background URL Mobile** | [UI customization](/docs/application/ui-customization#1-background-image) |\\n| Look of the form | **Custom CSS**, **Custom CSS Mobile** | [UI customization](/docs/application/ui-customization#2-login-panel-style) |\\n| Form on the left, center or right, or next to a panel | **Form position**, **Side panel HTML** | [UI customization](/docs/application/ui-customization#3-panel-position) |\\n| Text below the form | **Footer HTML** | [Application settings](/docs/application/terminology#custom-html) |\\n| Which fields the login page shows, order, placeholders | **Signin items** | [Sign-in items table](/docs/application/signin-items-table) |\\n| Password, code, magic link, WebAuthn, LDAP and their order | **Signin methods** | [Sign-in methods](/docs/application/signin-methods) |\\n| Sign-up fields, required flags, labels, regex | **Signup items** | [Sign-up items table](/docs/application/signup-items-table) |\\n| Language selector | **Languages** sign-in item, **Languages** of the organization | [Sign-in items table](/docs/application/signin-items-table#language-selector-rules) |\\n| Let users pick or type their organization | **Org choice mode** | [Specify login organization](/docs/application/specify-login-organization) |\\n\\nIn the Chinese console the tab is called \u754C\u9762\u5B9A\u5236 and the sign-in table \u767B\u5F55\u9879, so if you are looking for how to change the \u767B\u5F55\u754C\u9762 of an application, start there.\\n\\n## Themes: organization, application and Marketplace\\n\\nThe quickest change is the theme. A theme in Casdoor is a small object with a style (Default, Dark, Document or Blossom in the editor), a primary color, a border radius and a compact switch. You can set it on the organization, where it applies to the console for its members and to the entry pages of every application that follows the organization theme, or on a single application by turning off **Follow organization theme**. The application edit page has a live preview, so you can see the result before you save.\\n\\nIf you want more than colors, the [Casdoor Marketplace](/docs/marketplace/overview) has sign-in themes. Installing one writes the theme data, form position, form CSS for desktop and mobile, side panel HTML, footer HTML and background URLs of one application. The casdoor-integrations repository currently holds 24 themes, from Midnight and Split to Terminal, Nord and Solarized. Uninstalling a theme restores what the application had before, so trying one costs nothing. Browse them at [marketplace.casdoor.com](https://marketplace.casdoor.com).\\n\\n## Branding: logo, favicon and background\\n\\nEach application has its own **Logo**, **Favicon** and **Title**, separate from the organization. One organization can run several products that share users but show different logos. There is also an optional **Logo dark**, shown when the page is in dark mode; without it, Casdoor keeps using the normal logo.\\n\\n**Background URL** sets the image behind the form. Set **Background URL Mobile** too if the desktop image crops badly on phones. The preview updates as soon as the URL loads.\\n\\n## Custom CSS and form layout\\n\\n**Custom CSS** takes plain CSS rules; Casdoor wraps them in a style element itself, so don\'t add one of your own. The main containers to target are `.login-panel` and `.login-form`:\\n\\n```css\\n.login-panel {\\n  border-radius: 12px;\\n  background-color: #ffffff;\\n  box-shadow: 0 0 30px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Custom CSS Mobile** holds rules for phones, so you don\'t need to write media queries for the common case.\\n\\n**Form position** places the form on the left, center or right. The fourth option, **Enable side panel**, centers the form and puts a panel next to it whose content comes from **Side panel HTML**. That field takes HTML, so a style element inside it is fine.\\n\\n**Footer HTML** is rendered below the sign-in and sign-up forms. If the application is the default application of its organization, its footer also replaces \\"Powered by Casdoor\\" in the admin console for that organization\'s users.\\n\\nWhen you are happy with a design on staging, **Export JSON** at the bottom of the application edit page copies the look (logo, favicon, backgrounds, CSS, form position, side panel, theme and the custom HTML fields) to the clipboard, and **Import JSON** on the same application on another instance applies it. The export contains no secrets or providers.\\n\\n## Fields, labels and order\\n\\nThe **Signin items** table lists every element of the login page: the logo, the username and password fields, the forgot-password link, the agreement checkbox, the language selector, the captcha, provider buttons and so on. For each row you can set **Visible**, a custom **Placeholder**, per-item **Custom CSS** and a rule, and move it up or down. You can also add custom items whose label is HTML.\\n\\nA few rules worth knowing:\\n\\n- Hiding **Forgot password?** turns off password reset in the API too, not just the link.\\n- The **Captcha** rule switches between a pop-up and an inline captcha. If you are choosing a captcha provider, see [CAPTCHA providers compared for login pages](/blog/captcha-for-login-pages).\\n- The **Languages** rule switches between a globe icon and a labeled selector. The list itself is limited to the **Languages** of the organization, so an organization that only serves English and Japanese users shows only those two.\\n\\nThe sign-in methods (password, verification code, magic link, WebAuthn, LDAP, Face ID and others) are a separate table, **Signin methods**, with its own order and display names.\\n\\nThe **Signup items** table does the same for registration, with **Required**, **Regex** validation, custom **Label** and rules such as \\"No verification\\" for email. `POST /api/signup` also clears every field whose item is hidden, so API clients can\'t set fields your form doesn\'t show. **Prompted** asks users for missing fields, such as a phone number, after they sign in through a provider that didn\'t supply one.\\n\\n## Specify the login organization\\n\\nIf several organizations share one Casdoor, users need a way to reach the right one. **Org choice mode** on the default application of an organization (or on `app-built-in`) adds an organization step to `/login`: **Input** shows a text field, **Select** shows a dropdown. After the user picks, Casdoor sends them to `/login/<organization>`, which shows that organization\'s own styled page. The browser remembers the last organization in `localStorage`, so a user whose console session expired lands on their own login page again.\\n\\n## What tenant admins can safely change\\n\\nLetting every customer style their own login page raises an obvious question: what stops an organization admin from adding a script that copies passwords, or a CSS selector that leaks each typed character through background image requests? Casdoor checks this on the server. When an admin who is not a global admin adds or saves an application, every custom HTML and CSS field that changed is validated, and the save is rejected if any of them breaks the rules:\\n\\n- HTML may only use layout and text elements such as `div`, `span`, `p`, headings, lists, links, images, tables, `style` and basic SVG shapes. `script`, `form`, `input` and `iframe` are refused, as are `on...` event handlers, `srcset`, `formaction`, `srcdoc` and similar attributes. `href` is allowed only on links with relative, `http`, `https`, `mailto` or `tel` URLs.\\n- CSS may not contain backslashes, `<`, `image(`, `image-set(` and a few other functions. Every `url()` must be relative, a `data:` URL, or start with an allowed prefix: Google Fonts, Fontsource on jsDelivr, the Casdoor CDNs, this Casdoor\'s own origin, the domains of the global storage providers, or an entry of `formCssAllowedUrls`.\\n- `@import` is allowed only from Google Fonts and Fontsource, because an imported stylesheet isn\'t checked itself.\\n\\nTo let tenants load images from your own host, add it to `conf/app.conf` (see [Configuration](/docs/basic/configuration#backend-options)):\\n\\n```ini\\nformCssAllowedUrls = \\"https://img.example.com/\\"\\n```\\n\\nOn top of the save-time check, the sign-in pages sanitize custom HTML before rendering it, unless the application belongs to the `built-in` organization or a global admin has turned on **Enable custom scripts** for it. Only a global admin can turn that switch on, and while it is on only a global admin can change that application\'s HTML. The same rules apply when a tenant installs a Marketplace theme: a theme that breaks them can only be installed by a global admin.\\n\\n**Signin HTML** and **Signup HTML** deserve a separate note: they replace the whole built-in page, form included. Since tenants can\'t add forms or scripts, these two fields are mainly for global admins; for branding, CSS, the side panel and the footer are the better tools.\\n\\n## Hosted login page or your own UI?\\n\\nYou can build your own login screen on Casdoor\'s API, but the hosted page is usually the better choice. With OAuth 2.0 or OpenID Connect, users type their password only on the Casdoor origin and your application receives an authorization code, never the credentials. Captcha, MFA, social and enterprise providers, forgot-password, sign-up validation and the organization picker keep working without code on your side, and they keep getting fixes when you upgrade Casdoor. A custom UI has to reimplement each of those flows and puts passwords through your own servers. Guides for wiring the hosted page into an app: [Spring Boot SSO with OIDC](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc) and [Nginx SSO authentication](/blog/nginx-sso-authentication).\\n\\nOther hosted providers make the same trade. Idura Verify, for example, lets you add a stylesheet URL and custom HTML around its login box from its dashboard, under a strict Content Security Policy with an explicit list of style origins for fonts and images. Casdoor\'s `formCssAllowedUrls` plays the same role, with the difference that Casdoor is open source and self-hosted, so you control the list.\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- [Customize the look of the sign-in page](/docs/application/ui-customization)\\n- [Theme anpassen](/docs/organization/customize-theme)\\n- [Sign-in items table](/docs/application/signin-items-table) and [Sign-up items table](/docs/application/signup-items-table)\\n- [Specify login organization](/docs/application/specify-login-organization)\\n- [Marketplace](/docs/marketplace/overview) and [casdoor/casdoor-integrations](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-integrations)\\n- Casdoor source: [`object/application_util.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/application_util.go) and [`object/application_html.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/application_html.go)\\n- Idura Verify: [Custom styling](https://docs.idura.app/verify/guides/custom-styling/)"},{"id":"devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana.mdx","title":"Jenkins OIDC, Gitea OIDC and Grafana SSO with Casdoor: One Login for a Self-Hosted DevOps Stack","description":"Use Casdoor as the OIDC provider for Gitea, Jenkins, Grafana, Argo CD, MinIO, Portainer, Harbor and GitLab: callback URLs, config keys and admin group mapping.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Jenkins","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/jenkins"},{"inline":true,"label":"Gitea","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/gitea"},{"inline":true,"label":"Grafana","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/grafana"},{"inline":true,"label":"Argo CD","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/argo-cd"},{"inline":true,"label":"MinIO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/min-io"},{"inline":true,"label":"DevOps","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/dev-ops"}],"readingTime":9.14,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana","title":"Jenkins OIDC, Gitea OIDC and Grafana SSO with Casdoor: One Login for a Self-Hosted DevOps Stack","description":"Use Casdoor as the OIDC provider for Gitea, Jenkins, Grafana, Argo CD, MinIO, Portainer, Harbor and GitLab: callback URLs, config keys and admin group mapping.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","OIDC","SSO","Jenkins","Gitea","Grafana","Argo CD","MinIO","DevOps"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Customize the Login Page in Casdoor: Custom Styling of the Authentication UI Without Forking","permalink":"/de/blog/customize-login-page"},"nextItem":{"title":"DingTalk, WeCom, Lark, Infoflow and WeChat OAuth Login with Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/dingtalk-wecom-lark-login"}},"content":"Every tool in a typical self-hosted DevOps stack can act as an OpenID Connect client: Gitea and Argo CD speak OIDC natively, Jenkins does through the `oic-auth` plugin, Grafana and Portainer through generic OAuth 2.0, and MinIO, Harbor and GitLab through their OIDC settings. Point all of them at one Casdoor instance, give each tool its own Casdoor application, and put your people in Casdoor groups. Each tool then reads the `groups` claim and decides who is an administrator. This post lists the callback URL, the config keys and the group mapping for each tool, then covers the parts they share.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The stack at a glance\\n\\nAll paths below are relative to the tool\'s own public URL. Register them in **Redirect URLs** of the Casdoor application for that tool.\\n\\n| App | Protocol | Callback URL | Group/role to admin mapping | Casdoor doc |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Gitea | OIDC (OAuth2 auth source) | `/user/oauth2/<source-name>/callback` | Yes: admin group, restricted group, team map | [Gitea](/docs/integration/go/gitea) |\\n| Jenkins | OIDC (`oic-auth` plugin) | `/securityRealm/finishLogin`, logout `/OicLogout` | Yes: groups claim plus Matrix-based security | [Jenkins (OIDC)](/docs/integration/java/jenkins-oidc) |\\n| Grafana | Generic OAuth 2.0 | `/login/generic_oauth` | Yes: `role_attribute_path` | [Grafana](/docs/integration/go/grafana) |\\n| Argo CD | OIDC | `/auth/callback`, CLI `http://localhost:8085/auth/callback` | Yes: `argocd-rbac-cm` | [Argo CD](/docs/integration/go/argocd) |\\n| MinIO | OIDC | Console `/oauth_callback` | Policies from one claim (`claim_name`) | [MinIO](/docs/integration/go/minio) |\\n| Portainer | OAuth 2.0 (Custom) | The Portainer base URL | Business Edition only | [Portainer](/docs/integration/go/portainer) |\\n| Harbor | OIDC | `/c/oidc/callback` | Yes: OIDC Admin Group | none yet |\\n| GitLab | OIDC (OmniAuth) | `/users/auth/openid_connect/callback` | Premium and Ultimate: `admin_groups` | [GitLab](/docs/integration/ruby/gitlab) |\\n\\nThe discovery document every OIDC client needs is `https://<casdoor-host>/.well-known/openid-configuration`. If you have not connected an OIDC client to Casdoor before, [Connect a standard OIDC client](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) explains the endpoints.\\n\\n## Prepare Casdoor once\\n\\nBefore touching the tools, set up three things in Casdoor.\\n\\n**Groups.** Create groups such as `devops-admins` and `devops-users` in the organization tree and add users to them. See [Groups](/docs/organization/organization-tree).\\n\\n**Token group format.** By default the `groups` claim names each group as `<organization>/<group>`. Most tools compare the claim with a plain string you type, so set **Token group format** on the **OIDC/OAuth** tab of each application to `Name (group)`. The claim then carries `devops-admins`. The options are described under [Group format](/docs/token/overview#token-group-format).\\n\\n**Token format.** Keep **Token format** at `JWT`, the default. In that format the ID token carries `groups`, `roles` and `tag`. `JWT-Empty` and `JWT-Standard` leave all three out of the token, and `JWT-Custom` carries them only if you select them in **Token fields**. The `/api/userinfo` response returns `groups` and the role names when the `profile` scope is granted, so tools that read userinfo still see them.\\n\\nRoles work too. In the `JWT` format the `roles` claim is an array of role objects, so match on `roles[*].name`. In `/api/userinfo` it is a plain array of names. See [User roles](/docs/user/roles).\\n\\n## Gitea\\n\\nIn **Site Administration**, add an authentication source of type **OAuth2** with provider **OpenID Connect**, the client ID and secret, and the Casdoor discovery URL. The name you give the source is part of the callback URL: a source named `casdoor` uses `https://gitea.example.com/user/oauth2/casdoor/callback`.\\n\\nFor group mapping, fill in **Claim name providing group names** with `groups`, then **Group Claim value for administrator users** with `devops-admins`. Gitea also has **Group Claim value for restricted users** and **Map claimed groups to Organization teams**. The same source from the CLI:\\n\\n```bash\\ngitea admin auth add-oauth \\\\\\n  --name casdoor \\\\\\n  --provider openidConnect \\\\\\n  --key <client-id> \\\\\\n  --secret <client-secret> \\\\\\n  --auto-discover-url https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration \\\\\\n  --group-claim-name groups \\\\\\n  --admin-group devops-admins\\n```\\n\\n## Jenkins\\n\\nJenkins has no OIDC support of its own. Install the [OpenId Connect Authentication](https://plugins.jenkins.io/oic-auth/) plugin, choose it as the security realm, and use the well-known configuration. The login callback is `/securityRealm/finishLogin` and the logout callback is `/OicLogout`; add both to Casdoor. In JCasC:\\n\\n```yaml\\njenkins:\\n  securityRealm:\\n    oic:\\n      serverConfiguration:\\n        wellKnown:\\n          wellKnownOpenIDConfigurationUrl: https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration\\n      clientId: <client-id>\\n      clientSecret: <client-secret>\\n      userNameField: name\\n      fullNameFieldName: displayName\\n      emailFieldName: email\\n      groupsFieldName: groups\\n      logoutFromOpenidProvider: true\\n      properties:\\n      - \\"pkce\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe plugin only passes the groups to Jenkins. Rights come from the authorization strategy: with Matrix-based security, each row of the table is a user or a group, so add a row for `devops-admins` and give it Overall/Administer. Choosing the plugin as the security realm turns off other login methods, so configure the plugin\'s `escapeHatch` property (a fallback username and secret) before you save. Casdoor also has its own Jenkins plugin, described in [Jenkins plugin](/docs/integration/java/jenkins-plugin), but the generic OIDC plugin is the one this post assumes.\\n\\n## Grafana\\n\\nGrafana uses generic OAuth. Its callback is `/login/generic_oauth`. Grafana\'s default `scopes` is `user:email`, so set the OIDC scopes explicitly:\\n\\n```ini\\n[auth.generic_oauth]\\nenabled = true\\nname = Casdoor\\nclient_id = <client-id>\\nclient_secret = <client-secret>\\nscopes = openid profile email\\nauth_url = https://door.example.com/login/oauth/authorize\\ntoken_url = https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token\\napi_url = https://door.example.com/api/userinfo\\nuse_pkce = true\\ngroups_attribute_path = groups\\nrole_attribute_path = contains(groups[*], \'devops-admins\') && \'Admin\' || contains(groups[*], \'devops-users\') && \'Editor\' || \'Viewer\'\\nrole_attribute_strict = true\\n```\\n\\nGrafana evaluates `role_attribute_path` against the ID token first, then the userinfo response, then the access token. Valid results are `None`, `Viewer`, `Editor`, `Admin` and `GrafanaAdmin`; the last one needs `allow_assign_grafana_admin = true`. `allowed_groups` limits login to listed groups and requires `groups_attribute_path`. Team sync is a Grafana Enterprise and Grafana Cloud feature.\\n\\n## Argo CD\\n\\nArgo CD talks to Casdoor directly, without its bundled Dex. The browser callback is `/auth/callback`. `argocd login --sso` starts a local server on port 8085 by default and uses `http://localhost:8085/auth/callback`, so register that as well.\\n\\nSet `url` and `oidc.config` (with `name`, `issuer`, `clientID`, `clientSecret`, `requestedScopes`) in `argocd-cm`. Argo CD\'s default `requestedScopes` adds a `groups` scope. Casdoor does not need it, because the `JWT` ID token always carries `groups`, so our guide requests `[\\"openid\\", \\"profile\\", \\"email\\"]`. Groups are mapped in `argocd-rbac-cm`, where `scopes` defaults to `[groups]`:\\n\\n```yaml\\ndata:\\n  policy.default: role:readonly\\n  policy.csv: |\\n    g, devops-admins, role:admin\\n```\\n\\nThe full walkthrough, including the client secret in `argocd-secret`, is in the [Argo CD](/docs/integration/go/argocd) guide.\\n\\n## MinIO\\n\\nMinIO reads policies from a single claim, set with `claim_name` (default `policy`). It does not map groups. MinIO\'s own Casdoor quickstart and our guide use Casdoor\'s `tag` claim: put a MinIO policy name such as `readwrite` in the user\'s **Tag**, then:\\n\\n```bash\\nmc admin config set myminio identity_openid \\\\\\n  config_url=\\"https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration\\" \\\\\\n  client_id=<client-id> client_secret=<client-secret> claim_name=\\"tag\\"\\n```\\n\\n`role_policy` is the alternative: every user who signs in through this provider gets the listed policies, and it cannot be combined with `claim_name`. The Console callback is `<console-url>/oauth_callback`. Behind a proxy, set `MINIO_BROWSER_REDIRECT_URL`; the old `redirect_uri` key is deprecated. Note that the `minio/minio` GitHub repository has been archived and its README says it is no longer maintained, so check which MinIO build you run before relying on these settings.\\n\\n## Portainer\\n\\nPortainer uses a Custom OAuth provider. Fill in **Authorization URL** (`/login/oauth/authorize`), **Access token URL** (`/api/login/oauth/access_token`) and **Resource URL** (`/api/userinfo`) on your Casdoor host, and set **Redirect URL** to the Portainer base URL. There is also a **Logout URL** field. In Portainer CE, **Automatic team membership** is marked as a Business Edition feature, and the setting that assigns admin rights to groups is only in Business Edition. On CE, users created by automatic provisioning get the standard user role and you promote admins by hand.\\n\\n## Harbor and GitLab\\n\\nHarbor shows its redirect URI at the bottom of the OIDC settings page: `<harbor-url>/c/oidc/callback`. Set **OIDC Provider Endpoint** to the Casdoor URL, **Group Claim Name** to `groups`, and **OIDC Admin Group** to `devops-admins`. Harbor reads the group claim from the ID token, which the `JWT` format provides. You can only switch Harbor from database to OIDC authentication before any local users exist.\\n\\nGitLab\'s OIDC docs have a Casdoor section and require Casdoor to be served over HTTPS. The callback is `/users/auth/openid_connect/callback`. Group-based roles (`groups_attribute`, `admin_groups`, `required_groups`, `external_groups`) need GitLab Premium or Ultimate.\\n\\n## Tools without OIDC\\n\\nFor internal dashboards with no login of their own, put them behind Apache APISIX\'s `openid-connect` plugin (`discovery`, `client_id`, `client_secret`, `redirect_uri`, `bearer_only`), as in the [APISIX guide](/docs/integration/lua/apisix), or behind nginx as described in [Nginx SSO authentication](/blog/nginx-sso-authentication).\\n\\n## One application per tool or a shared one\\n\\nA single Casdoor application with every tool\'s callback in its Redirect URLs works, but one application per tool is easier to run:\\n\\n- Each tool gets its own client secret, so a leaked Jenkins secret does not expose Grafana.\\n- **Token group format**, **Token format** and token expiry are per application, so you can give Argo CD `Name (group)` without changing another tool\'s claims.\\n- Casdoor checks `post_logout_redirect_uri` against the Redirect URLs of the application, so each list stays short and specific.\\n\\nUsers still sign in once: after the first login, Casdoor\'s session cookie lets the next tool\'s redirect complete without a password prompt.\\n\\n## Abmeldung {#logout}\\n\\nCasdoor\'s end-session endpoint is `/api/logout`, listed as `end_session_endpoint` in the discovery document. It accepts `id_token_hint`, `post_logout_redirect_uri`, `client_id` and `state`, and only redirects to a URL listed in the application\'s Redirect URLs. Signing out of Casdoor ends the Casdoor session and the tokens of that session, but each tool also keeps its own session cookie. Wire up logout in each tool:\\n\\n- Jenkins: `logoutFromOpenidProvider`, with `/OicLogout` registered.\\n- Grafana: `signout_redirect_url`.\\n- Argo CD: `logoutURL` in `oidc.config`, for example `https://door.example.com/api/logout?id_token_hint={{token}}&post_logout_redirect_uri={{logoutRedirectURL}}`.\\n- Harbor: **OIDC Session Logout**.\\n- Portainer: **Logout URL**.\\n\\nThe details of the endpoint are in [RP-initiated logout](/docs/session/rp-initiated-logout).\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- Gitea: [Authentication](https://docs.gitea.com/usage/authentication), [`admin auth add-oauth`](https://docs.gitea.com/administration/command-line)\\n- Jenkins: [OpenId Connect Authentication plugin](https://plugins.jenkins.io/oic-auth/), [configuration reference](https://github.com/jenkinsci/oic-auth-plugin/blob/master/docs/configuration/README.md), [Managing security](https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/security/managing-security/)\\n- Grafana: [Configure generic OAuth2 authentication](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-access/configure-authentication/generic-oauth/)\\n- Argo CD: [User management](https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/user-management/), [RBAC](https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/rbac/), [`argocd login`](https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/commands/argocd_login/)\\n- MinIO: [Casdoor quickstart in the MinIO repository](https://github.com/minio/minio/blob/master/docs/sts/casdoor.md)\\n- Portainer: [OAuth authentication](https://docs.portainer.io/admin/settings/authentication/oauth)\\n- Harbor: [Configure OIDC provider authentication](https://goharbor.io/docs/main/administration/configure-authentication/oidc-auth/)\\n- GitLab: [Use OpenID Connect as an authentication provider](https://docs.gitlab.com/administration/auth/oidc/)\\n- Apache APISIX: [openid-connect plugin](https://apisix.apache.org/docs/apisix/plugins/openid-connect/)"},{"id":"dingtalk-wecom-lark-login","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/dingtalk-wecom-lark-login","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-dingtalk-wecom-lark-login.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-dingtalk-wecom-lark-login.mdx","title":"DingTalk, WeCom, Lark, Infoflow and WeChat OAuth Login with Casdoor","description":"Sign in with DingTalk, WeCom, Lark/Feishu, Baidu Infoflow and WeChat through Casdoor: app types, QR login, user IDs, email access, corporate tokens, sync.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"DingTalk","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/ding-talk"},{"inline":true,"label":"WeCom","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/we-com"},{"inline":true,"label":"Lark","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/lark"},{"inline":true,"label":"Feishu","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/feishu"},{"inline":true,"label":"WeChat","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/we-chat"},{"inline":true,"label":"Infoflow","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/infoflow"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":9.95,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"dingtalk-wecom-lark-login","title":"DingTalk, WeCom, Lark, Infoflow and WeChat OAuth Login with Casdoor","description":"Sign in with DingTalk, WeCom, Lark/Feishu, Baidu Infoflow and WeChat through Casdoor: app types, QR login, user IDs, email access, corporate tokens, sync.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","DingTalk","WeCom","Lark","Feishu","WeChat","Infoflow","OAuth","SSO"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Jenkins OIDC, Gitea OIDC and Grafana SSO with Casdoor: One Login for a Self-Hosted DevOps Stack","permalink":"/de/blog/devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana"},"nextItem":{"title":"Email Provider for Casdoor Verification Codes: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, ACS Email and SES","permalink":"/de/blog/email-providers-for-verification-codes"}},"content":"Casdoor has built-in OAuth providers for DingTalk, WeCom (WeChat Work), Lark (Feishu), Baidu Infoflow and WeChat. You create an app in each vendor\'s developer console, paste its credentials into a Casdoor provider, and add the provider to your application. Casdoor runs the vendor flow (QR scan on a desktop, or sign-in inside the vendor\'s own client), fetches the corporate access token where the vendor needs one, and maps the vendor\'s user ID to a Casdoor user. Your application only talks OIDC to Casdoor and never sees a DingTalk header, a WeCom `user_ticket` or a WeChat `unionid`. For DingTalk, WeCom and Lark, a syncer can also import the whole org directory ahead of the first sign-in.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Auf einen Blick {#at-a-glance}\\n\\n| Provider | App you create | Where users sign in | ID that links the Casdoor user | Email and phone | Directory syncer |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| DingTalk | Internal app (AppKey, AppSecret) | DingTalk sign-in page, `login.dingtalk.com` | `openId`; `unionid` becomes the username | From the profile, replaced by the corporate directory; treated as verified | Yes |\\n| WeCom | Self-built app (CorpID, Secret, AgentId), or third-party | Web login (QR, or quick login from WeCom desktop) or silent sign-in inside WeCom | `userid` (internal), `open_userid` (third-party) | Email only with `snsapi_privateinfo`; phone not read | Yes |\\n| Lark / Feishu | Custom app (App ID, App Secret) | Lark sign-in page, Feishu or Lark global | `open_id`; username is `user_id`, else `union_id`, else `open_id` | Yes, if the app has the contact permissions | Yes |\\n| Baidu Infoflow | Internal app (CorpID, Secret, AgentID) | Infoflow authorize page | `userid`, also the username | Email | No |\\n| WeChat | Website app on the Open Platform, plus an Official Account | QR scan on a PC; inside the WeChat browser through the Official Account | `unionid`, else `openid` | Neither | No |\\n\\nThe setup steps for each are in the docs: [DingTalk](/docs/provider/oauth/DingTalk), [WeCom](/docs/provider/oauth/weCom), [Lark](/docs/provider/oauth/lark), [Infoflow](/docs/provider/oauth/infoflow) and [WeChat](/docs/provider/oauth/Wechat). This post explains what happens behind each one.\\n\\n## What your application sees\\n\\nEvery provider in the table ends the same way. Casdoor exchanges the vendor\'s code, finds or creates the user in the organization, and then issues its own authorization code to your app. Your app redeems it at Casdoor\'s token endpoint and gets a Casdoor ID token and access token, signed with the application\'s certificate. The claims come from the Casdoor user record: name, display name, email, phone, avatar, groups.\\n\\nYour backend code is the same for all five. Any OIDC library works; see the [OAuth 2.0 and OIDC reference](/docs/how-to-connect/oauth) and, for a full walkthrough, [Spring Boot SSO with OIDC](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc). If you do need the vendor\'s own API later, Casdoor stores the provider\'s access token on the user, as described in [OAuth providers](/docs/provider/oauth/overview#using-the-providers-access-token).\\n\\n## DingTalk\\n\\nCreate an internal app on the DingTalk Open Platform and copy its AppKey and AppSecret into the Client ID and Client secret of the provider. The sign-in page sends the user to `https://login.dingtalk.com/oauth2/auth` with `scope=openid` and `prompt=login consent`. After the user confirms, Casdoor makes four calls:\\n\\n1. `POST https://api.dingtalk.com/v1.0/oauth2/userAccessToken` turns the code into a user access token.\\n2. `GET https://api.dingtalk.com/v1.0/contact/users/me` returns the user\'s `openId`, `unionId`, nick, avatar, email and mobile. This needs the **Contact.User.Read** permission on the app.\\n3. `POST https://api.dingtalk.com/v1.0/oauth2/accessToken` with `appKey` and `appSecret` returns the corporate (app) access token.\\n4. With that token, `topapi/user/getbyunionid` maps the `unionid` to the corporate `userid`, and `topapi/v2/user/get` reads the directory record.\\n\\nIf the user is not a member of the organization that owns the app, step 4 fails with DingTalk error 60121 and the sign-in stops. If the directory lookup succeeds, the directory email and mobile replace the personal ones, `unionid` becomes the username, and the job title is kept as an extra claim.\\n\\n### The corporate access token and its header\\n\\nDingTalk has two API generations that take the same token in different places:\\n\\n```http\\nGET https://api.dingtalk.com/v1.0/contact/users/me\\nx-acs-dingtalk-access-token: <token>\\n```\\n\\n```http\\nPOST https://oapi.dingtalk.com/topapi/v2/user/get?access_token=<token>\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n\\n{\\"userid\\": \\"<userid>\\"}\\n```\\n\\nThe new API on `api.dingtalk.com` reads the token only from the `x-acs-dingtalk-access-token` header. Call it without that header (or with `Authorization: Bearer`) and it answers `AuthenticationFailed.MissingParameter`, naming the header. The older `oapi.dingtalk.com` endpoints still take an `access_token` query parameter. Casdoor uses both: the header for `users/me`, the query parameter for the two `topapi` calls, with the corporate token from `v1.0/oauth2/accessToken` in both places. DingTalk documents that token as valid for 7,200 seconds and asks callers to cache it per app. DingTalk also marks the old `gettoken` endpoint as legacy for new apps.\\n\\nCasdoor counts the email and phone that DingTalk returns as verified, so the default binding rule (email, then phone) can attach a DingTalk sign-in to an existing user. See [how Casdoor links accounts](/docs/provider/oauth/overview#automatic-account-linking).\\n\\n## WeCom (WeChat Work)\\n\\nA WeCom provider has a **Sub type** and a **Method**:\\n\\n- **Internal** with **Normal** sends the user to the WeCom web login, `login.work.weixin.qq.com/wwlogin/sso/login` with `login_type=CorpApp`. WeCom shows a QR code, or a one-click quick login when WeCom desktop is running and signed in.\\n- **Internal** with **Silent** uses `open.weixin.qq.com/connect/oauth2/authorize` with the app\'s `agentid`. This is the one to use for pages opened inside the WeCom client.\\n- **Third-party** uses `login_type=ServiceApp` and the service provider\'s secret (`get_provider_token`), then `get_login_info`, and links the user by `open_userid`.\\n\\nFor internal apps, Casdoor gets the corporate token from `cgi-bin/gettoken` with the CorpID and the app Secret, then exchanges the code for the member\'s `userid`. A visitor who is not a member comes back with an `openid` instead, and Casdoor rejects the sign-in. The token is a query parameter on every WeCom call; WeCom says it normally lasts 7,200 seconds and that `gettoken` is rate limited.\\n\\nThe email address is the part to plan for. WeCom stopped returning avatar, gender, mobile and email from the member read API (`user/get`) for self-built apps created after 20:00 on 2022-06-20. To get the email, set the provider\'s scope to `snsapi_privateinfo`. WeCom then returns a `user_ticket`, which Casdoor redeems at `getuserdetail`. WeCom requires the admin to pick the sensitive fields in the app settings and the member to consent, and it never gives these fields to third-party apps. Casdoor does not read the phone number from WeCom.\\n\\n## Lark and Feishu\\n\\nCreate a custom app on the [Feishu Open Platform](https://open.feishu.cn/) or the Lark global platform and copy the App ID and App Secret. Turn on **Use global endpoint** in the provider for Lark (`open.larksuite.com`); leave it off for Feishu (`open.feishu.cn`).\\n\\nCasdoor first gets a `tenant_access_token` from `auth/v3/tenant_access_token/internal`, then posts the code to `authen/v1/access_token` with that token in an `Authorization: Bearer` header. The response carries `open_id`, `union_id`, `user_id`, email, enterprise email and mobile. Feishu returns the sensitive ones only when the app holds the matching permission:\\n\\n| Field | Feishu permission |\\n|---|---|\\n| `user_id` | `contact:user.employee_id:readonly` |\\n| `email` | `contact:user.email:readonly` |\\n| `enterprise_email` | `contact:user.employee:readonly` |\\n| `mobile` | `contact:user.phone:readonly` |\\n\\nWithout them, sign-in still works: the username falls back from `user_id` to `union_id` to `open_id`. Casdoor uses `enterprise_email` when `email` is empty and treats both email and phone as verified. Feishu now lists the v1 `access_token` endpoint as a historical version, but it is still served.\\n\\n## Baidu Infoflow\\n\\nInfoflow (\u5982\u6D41) is Baidu\'s workplace app. Register an app in the Infoflow admin, give it address book permissions, and copy the CorpID, Secret and AgentID into the provider with the **Internal** sub type. The sign-in page goes to `https://xpc.im.baidu.com/oauth2/authorize`. Casdoor then calls `qy.im.baidu.com/api/gettoken` with the CorpID and Secret, exchanges the code and AgentID for the member\'s `userid` at `api/user/getuserinfo`, and reads name, avatar and email from `api/user/get`. The `userid` becomes both the linked ID and the username, and the email counts as verified. There is no Infoflow syncer, so users are created on first sign-in.\\n\\n## WeChat-Anmeldung {#wechat}\\n\\nWeChat login has two halves, and you usually need both:\\n\\n- **Web** sub type: a website app on the WeChat Open Platform. The PC sign-in page shows a QR code from `open.weixin.qq.com/connect/qrconnect` with `scope=snsapi_login`; the user scans it with the WeChat app.\\n- **Mobile** sub type, or the second credential pair of the same provider: an Official Account. Inside the WeChat browser, a QR code is useless, so Casdoor switches to `connect/oauth2/authorize` with `snsapi_userinfo` and the Official Account\'s AppID. If no Official Account is configured, Casdoor tells the user to open the page in an external browser.\\n\\nWeChat returns no email or phone, only `openid`, `unionid`, nickname and avatar. Casdoor links the user by `unionid` when WeChat sends one, falling back to `openid`, and also stores the per-app `openid`. WeChat gives a user the same `unionid` across all apps and Official Accounts under one Open Platform account, so bind your website app and your Official Account to the same Open Platform account. Otherwise a user who scans on a PC and later signs in on a phone becomes two Casdoor users.\\n\\n## Importing the directory first\\n\\nFor DingTalk, WeCom and Lark, a [syncer](/docs/syncer/overview) can pull users and departments in on a schedule, so accounts exist before anyone signs in and departments become Casdoor groups. Each one uses a corporate token from the vendor\'s directory API: the [DingTalk syncer](/docs/syncer/DingTalk) walks departments recursively, the [WeCom syncer](/docs/syncer/WeCom) falls back to `user/list_id` and `user/get` for apps created after 2022-08-15, and the [Lark syncer](/docs/syncer/Lark) has a **Lark platform** setting to choose Feishu or Lark global.\\n\\nThe WeCom syncer writes the `userid` that the WeCom provider links by, so synced users sign in directly. The DingTalk and Lark syncers store the directory `userid` / `user_id`, while sign-in links by `openId` / `open_id`. Their first sign-in matches through the binding rule on email or phone instead, which works because both providers report those as verified.\\n\\n## QR login and account recovery\\n\\nQR login moves the password problem to the phone: if a user loses their WeChat or DingTalk account, they cannot scan. Because each of these identities is linked to a Casdoor user rather than being the user, the user can still sign in with a verification code or reset the password through the [forgot-password flow](/docs/application/signin-items-table#forgot-password-flow), as long as the application has an email or SMS provider and the user has an address on file. DingTalk and Lark fill that in from the directory; for WeChat, ask for a phone number at sign-up. To show more than one of these options on the page, see [sign-in methods](/docs/application/signin-methods).\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- DingTalk: get a user token (`userAccessToken`, `expireIn` 7200): https://open.dingtalk.com/document/isvapp-server/obtain-user-token\\n- DingTalk: get the access token of an internal app: https://open.dingtalk.com/document/development/obtain-orgapp-token\\n- DingTalk: obtain user personal information: https://open.dingtalk.com/document/orgapp-server/tutorial-obtaining-user-personal-information\\n- WeCom: get access_token: https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/91039\\n- WeCom: web login component (`wwlogin/sso/login`): https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/98152\\n- WeCom: build the OAuth link and scopes: https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/91022\\n- WeCom: get sensitive member info (`getuserdetail`): https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/95833\\n- WeCom: read member (2022-06-20 field change): https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/90196\\n- Feishu: get user_access_token (v1) and field permissions: https://open.feishu.cn/document/server-docs/authentication-management/access-token/create-2\\n- WeChat: website app login: https://developers.weixin.qq.com/doc/oplatform/Website_App/WeChat_Login/Wechat_Login.html\\n- WeChat: UnionID: https://developers.weixin.qq.com/doc/oplatform/Website_App/WeChat_Login/Authorized_Interface_Calling_UnionID.html\\n- Casdoor providers: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/idp"},{"id":"email-providers-for-verification-codes","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/email-providers-for-verification-codes","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-email-providers-for-verification-codes.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-email-providers-for-verification-codes.mdx","title":"Email Provider for Casdoor Verification Codes: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, ACS Email and SES","description":"Pick an email provider for Casdoor sign-up codes, password resets and magic links: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, Azure ACS Email, Amazon SES and plain SMTP.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Email","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/email"},{"inline":true,"label":"SMTP","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/smtp"},{"inline":true,"label":"Brevo","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/brevo"},{"inline":true,"label":"Resend","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/resend"},{"inline":true,"label":"SendGrid","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/send-grid"},{"inline":true,"label":"Azure","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/azure"},{"inline":true,"label":"Deliverability","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/deliverability"}],"readingTime":9.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"email-providers-for-verification-codes","title":"Email Provider for Casdoor Verification Codes: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, ACS Email and SES","description":"Pick an email provider for Casdoor sign-up codes, password resets and magic links: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, Azure ACS Email, Amazon SES and plain SMTP.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Email","SMTP","Brevo","Resend","SendGrid","Azure","Deliverability"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"DingTalk, WeCom, Lark, Infoflow and WeChat OAuth Login with Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/dingtalk-wecom-lark-login"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to Host Static Files on a CDN with Casdoor: S3, MinIO, Azure Blob and Other Storage Providers","permalink":"/de/blog/file-storage-and-cdn"}},"content":"Casdoor sends sign-up verification codes, password reset links and magic links through an Email provider. It has native API types for **Resend**, **SendGrid** and **Azure Communication Services (ACS) Email**, a `Default` SMTP type that works with any SMTP relay (your own server, Google Workspace, Microsoft 365, **Brevo**, **Amazon SES**), and a `Custom HTTP Email` type for any other HTTP API. If you want a free tier and a short setup, start with Resend or Brevo. If you already run on AWS or Azure, SES or ACS keeps billing in one place. Whichever you pick, the deliverability work is the same: a sender domain with SPF and DKIM that align with the From address.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Die Optionen auf einen Blick {#the-options-at-a-glance}\\n\\nFree tiers and prices were checked on 2026-11-02 on each vendor\'s own pricing page.\\n\\n| Provider | API or SMTP | Free tier (checked 2026-11-02) | Sender domain setup | Casdoor type |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Your own mail server | SMTP | You run it | You publish SPF, DKIM and DMARC, and need forward and reverse DNS for the IP | `Default` |\\n| Google Workspace (`smtp.gmail.com`) | SMTP with an app password | Part of the Workspace subscription, 2,000 messages per day | Your Workspace domain | `Default` |\\n| Microsoft 365 (`smtp.office365.com`) | SMTP AUTH | Part of the subscription | Your Microsoft 365 domain | `Default` |\\n| Brevo | SMTP relay (`smtp-relay.brevo.com`) | 300 emails per day | Brevo code (TXT), DKIM and DMARC records | `Default` |\\n| Resend | API | 3,000 emails per month, 100 per day | SPF and DKIM records generated by Resend, then DMARC | `Resend` |\\n| SendGrid | API | 60-day trial, 100 emails per day | Domain authentication: CNAME records for SPF and DKIM, TXT for DMARC | `SendGrid` |\\n| Azure Communication Services Email | API | None listed; $0.00025 per email plus $0.00012 per MB | Azure managed subdomain, or a custom domain with SPF and DKIM | `Azure ACS` |\\n| Amazon SES | SMTP interface | No SES-only quota; new AWS accounts get Free Tier credits that SES can use | Verified identity for every From address | `Default` |\\n| Any other HTTP API | HTTP | Depends on the service | Depends on the service | `Custom HTTP Email` |\\n\\nThe [Email providers overview](/docs/provider/email/overview) in the docs lists the same types and the fields each one uses.\\n\\n## SMTP: your own server, Google Workspace, Microsoft 365\\n\\nThe `Default` type takes a host, port, username and password. **SSL mode** `Auto` uses implicit TLS on port 465 and STARTTLS on other ports, which covers almost every relay below.\\n\\nWith your own server, SPF, DKIM signing, reverse DNS and IP reputation are all yours. Google requires valid forward and reverse DNS (PTR) records for the sending IP.\\n\\nGoogle Workspace documents `smtp.gmail.com` on port 465 or 587, signed in with the full Workspace address and an app password, with a limit of 2,000 messages per day.\\n\\nMicrosoft 365 is the one to avoid for new setups. Casdoor\'s SMTP provider signs in with a username and password, which is Basic authentication for SMTP AUTH. Microsoft has announced plans to retire Basic authentication for SMTP AUTH in Exchange Online, and has already turned SMTP AUTH off in tenants that weren\'t using it. Check the latest timeline in the announcement below before you build on it.\\n\\n## Brevo SMTP\\n\\nBrevo sends transactional mail through a standard SMTP relay, so it uses the `Default` type. Most setup problems come from the Brevo SMTP login:\\n\\n- The server is `smtp-relay.brevo.com`, on port 587, 465 or 2525. Port 465 needs SSL, which `Auto` handles.\\n- The SMTP login is not always your account email. Depending on when the account was created, it is either your login email or a generated address like `[ID]@smtp-brevo.com`, and it is shown on the **SMTP** page in Brevo. Changing your account email doesn\'t change it.\\n- The password is an **SMTP key**, not an API key.\\n\\nIn Casdoor, set **Type** to `Default` and put those values in **Host**, **Port**, **Username** and **Password**. The [Brevo guide](/docs/provider/email/brevo) has screenshots. For the domain, Brevo asks for its own TXT verification record, DKIM and DMARC; SPF is only needed with a dedicated IP.\\n\\n## Resend\\n\\nThe `Resend` type needs only an API key in **Secret key**, plus a **From address** on a verified domain. Leave **Host**, **Port** and **Username** empty. Casdoor formats the sender as `Name <address>` from **From name**, or from the organization\'s display name when **From name** is empty.\\n\\nTwo things to know before you test. First, the shared `onboarding@resend.dev` sender can only send to the email address of your own Resend account; any other recipient gets a 403. Second, verifying your own domain means adding the SPF and DKIM records Resend generates, and Resend recommends sending from a subdomain to keep its reputation separate. The free plan covers 3,000 emails a month with a cap of 100 per day; the Pro plan starts at $20 a month for 50,000. See the [Resend guide](/docs/provider/email/resend).\\n\\n## SendGrid\\n\\nThe `SendGrid` type uses an API key. Leave **Host** and **Endpoint** empty and Casdoor uses SendGrid\'s standard send API; fill both only if you need a different endpoint. Casdoor treats anything but `202 Accepted` as a failure and shows the error messages SendGrid returns, which helps when a sender isn\'t verified yet.\\n\\nSendGrid\'s pricing page lists a 60-day free trial at 100 emails a day, then Essentials from $19.95 a month. Domain authentication with automated security adds a CNAME for SPF and DKIM and a TXT record for DMARC. See the [SendGrid guide](/docs/provider/email/sendgrid).\\n\\n## ACS Email (Azure Communication Services)\\n\\nThe `Azure ACS` type takes the resource endpoint as **Host** and the access key as **Secret key**; Casdoor signs each request with that key. Azure gives you a one-click managed subdomain under `azurecomm.net` with authentication already set up, and Microsoft suggests using it for development and switching to a custom domain, with its SPF and DKIM records, for launch. There is no free tier on the pricing page: $0.00025 per email plus $0.00012 per MB transferred. See the [Azure ACS guide](/docs/provider/email/azureACS).\\n\\n## Amazon SES\\n\\nCasdoor has no SES API type, so use the SES SMTP interface with the `Default` type. The host is the Region\'s SMTP endpoint, such as `email-smtp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com`; SES accepts STARTTLS on 25, 587 or 2587 and implicit TLS on 465 or 2465. The SMTP username and password are SMTP credentials you generate in SES. They are not your AWS access keys, and they are different in each Region.\\n\\nNew accounts start in the SES sandbox: mail goes only to verified addresses, at most 200 messages per 24 hours and one per second. Request production access before real users sign up. SES charges $0.10 per 1,000 outbound emails at its \xe0 la carte rate, with no minimum fee.\\n\\n## Custom HTTP Email\\n\\nFor a service without a native type, `Custom HTTP Email` sends `fromAddress`, `fromName`, `toAddress` (or `toAddresses` for several recipients), `subject` and `content` as a form or JSON body, with headers you define. You can rename each field to match the target API. Casdoor counts only HTTP 200 as success, so an API that answers `202 Accepted` shows up as an error; a small adapter in front fixes that. See [Send email through your own HTTP API](/docs/provider/email/customHttp).\\n\\n## Deliverability basics for auth mail\\n\\nVerification codes are useless if they land in spam. The rules that matter:\\n\\n- **SPF and DKIM.** Google requires every sender to Gmail to use SPF or DKIM. Above 5,000 messages a day it requires both, plus DMARC (a `p=none` policy is accepted). Set up all three from the start.\\n- **Alignment.** The domain in the From header must match the SPF domain or the DKIM domain, or DMARC fails. This is why each provider makes you verify the domain you send from.\\n- **A separate subdomain.** Yahoo recommends separating transactional mail from marketing mail by IP or DKIM domain, and Resend recommends subdomains. Something like `auth.example.com` keeps a bad newsletter from hurting your password resets.\\n- **TLS and spam rate.** Google requires TLS and asks you to keep the reported spam rate below 0.3%, ideally below 0.1%.\\n- **No unsubscribe header needed.** Google\'s FAQ says one-click unsubscribe applies to marketing mail, and names password reset messages as an example of transactional mail that is excluded.\\n- **Simple content.** Casdoor sends the template as one HTML body, without a separate plain-text part, for every built-in type. Keep the HTML light, put the code in plain text, and show the full link as text next to the button, as Casdoor\'s built-in magic link email does. Use your Casdoor origin directly rather than a link shortener, so the link domain matches your brand.\\n\\n## Configure and test the provider in Casdoor\\n\\n1. Go to **Identity** > **Providers**, add a provider, set **Category** to `Email` and choose the **Type** from the table above.\\n2. Set **From address** and, optionally, **From name**.\\n3. Write **Email title** and **Email content**. The content can use `%s` for the code, `%{user.friendlyName}` for the user\'s name, and `%link` inside a `<reset-link>` block for the password reset link. Magic link emails use `%link` and `%expireTime`; if your content has no `%link`, Casdoor sends its own magic link email instead. The [templates section](/docs/provider/email/overview#email-content-and-placeholders) has examples. One thing to edit: the default template says the code is valid for 5 minutes, but codes and magic links expire after `verificationCodeTimeout` in `conf/app.conf`, which defaults to 10 minutes.\\n4. Click **Test SMTP Connection** to open a connection without sending (SMTP types only), then enter an address in **Test Email** and click **Send Testing Email**. The test uses the values in the form, saved or not, with `%s` replaced by `123456` and the reset link block removed.\\n5. Add the provider to the application with a rule such as `Signup`, `Forget Password`, `Magic link` or `All`. You can use different providers for different rules. See [Add providers to an application](/docs/application/providers#provider-rules).\\n6. Set `origin` in `conf/app.conf`. Without it, unless the request comes in on a loopback host such as `localhost`, Casdoor leaves the reset link out of password reset emails and refuses to send magic links. See [Sign in with a magic link](/docs/application/signin-methods#magic-link).\\n\\nFor local development, point a `Default` provider at a fake SMTP server instead of a real account; [MailHog vs Mailpit](/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit) covers that setup.\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- Casdoor source: [`email/provider.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/email/provider.go), [`object/email.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/email.go)\\n- [Resend pricing](https://resend.com/pricing) and [the resend.dev 403 error](https://resend.com/docs/knowledge-base/403-error-resend-dev-domain)\\n- [Twilio SendGrid Email API pricing](https://www.twilio.com/en-us/products/email-api/pricing) and [domain authentication](https://www.twilio.com/docs/sendgrid/ui/account-and-settings/how-to-set-up-domain-authentication)\\n- [Brevo pricing](https://www.brevo.com/pricing/) and [Send transactional emails using Brevo SMTP](https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/7924908994450)\\n- [Azure Communication Services pricing](https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/pricing/details/communication-services/) and [email domains and sender authentication](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/communication-services/concepts/email/email-domain-and-sender-authentication)\\n- [Amazon SES pricing](https://aws.amazon.com/ses/pricing/), [SES SMTP endpoints](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/smtp-connect.html) and [moving out of the sandbox](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/request-production-access.html)\\n- [Google email sender guidelines](https://support.google.com/a/answer/81126) and [FAQ](https://support.google.com/a/answer/14229414)\\n- [Yahoo sender best practices](https://senders.yahooinc.com/best-practices/)\\n- [Google Workspace: send email from a printer, scanner, or app](https://support.google.com/a/answer/176600)\\n- [Updated Exchange Online SMTP AUTH Basic authentication deprecation timeline](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/exchange/updated-exchange-online-smtp-auth-basic-authentication-deprecation-timeline/4489835)"},{"id":"file-storage-and-cdn","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/file-storage-and-cdn","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-file-storage-and-cdn-for-casdoor.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-file-storage-and-cdn-for-casdoor.mdx","title":"How to Host Static Files on a CDN with Casdoor: S3, MinIO, Azure Blob and Other Storage Providers","description":"Where Casdoor keeps avatars and uploaded files (local disk, Amazon S3, MinIO, Azure Blob, GCS, OSS, COS, Synology) and how to serve them through a CDN.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/storage"},{"inline":true,"label":"CDN","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/cdn"},{"inline":true,"label":"Amazon S3","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/amazon-s-3"},{"inline":true,"label":"MinIO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/min-io"},{"inline":true,"label":"Azure Blob","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/azure-blob"},{"inline":true,"label":"Object Storage","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/object-storage"}],"readingTime":9.79,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"file-storage-and-cdn","title":"How to Host Static Files on a CDN with Casdoor: S3, MinIO, Azure Blob and Other Storage Providers","description":"Where Casdoor keeps avatars and uploaded files (local disk, Amazon S3, MinIO, Azure Blob, GCS, OSS, COS, Synology) and how to serve them through a CDN.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Storage","CDN","Amazon S3","MinIO","Azure Blob","Object Storage"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Email Provider for Casdoor Verification Codes: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, ACS Email and SES","permalink":"/de/blog/email-providers-for-verification-codes"},"nextItem":{"title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Testen von Verifizierungs- und Passwort-Zur\xfccksetzung-E-Mails mit Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit"}},"content":"To host static files on a CDN, put them in object storage (Amazon S3, MinIO, Azure Blob Storage, Google Cloud Storage, Alibaba Cloud OSS or Tencent Cloud COS), make the CDN pull from that bucket as its origin, and hand out URLs on the CDN\'s domain. In Casdoor this is one setting: create a storage provider for the bucket and put the CDN address in its **Domain** field. Casdoor uploads avatars and other files through the provider\'s API and saves URLs that start with that domain. Casdoor\'s own frontend can come from a CDN as well, through `frontendCdnUrl` and `staticBaseUrl` in `app.conf`.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Storage providers at a glance\\n\\nA storage provider decides where Casdoor writes uploaded files: user avatars, ID card images, terms-of-use pages, and anything uploaded on the **Resources** page. You add the provider to an application, and uploads made in that application go to it. The [storage provider overview](/docs/provider/storage/overview) lists all the types. This table covers the ones people usually ask about:\\n\\n| Storage | Casdoor provider type | Does Casdoor use the S3 API? | Self-hostable | How the public URL is set |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Local file system | `Local File System` | No, plain files on disk | Yes, it is the Casdoor server | Served by Casdoor under `/files/`. **Domain** is read-only |\\n| Amazon S3 | `AWS S3` | Yes | No | **Domain**: the bucket URL or a CloudFront domain. **Endpoint (Intranet)**: a VPC endpoint |\\n| MinIO | `MinIO` | Yes, with path-style requests | Yes | **Domain**: the MinIO URL followed by the bucket name, or a CDN domain |\\n| Alibaba Cloud OSS | `Aliyun OSS` | No, the OSS SDK | No | **Domain**: a CDN domain. Left empty, it becomes `bucket.endpoint`. **Endpoint (Intranet)**: the internal endpoint |\\n| Tencent Cloud COS | `Tencent Cloud COS` | No, the COS API | No | **Domain**: a CDN domain |\\n| Azure Blob Storage | `Azure Blob` | No, the Blob API | No | **Domain**: a custom or Front Door domain. Casdoor adds the container name to the path |\\n| Google Cloud Storage | `Google Cloud Storage` | No, Google\'s Go client | No | **Domain**: left empty, it becomes `https://storage.googleapis.com`. Casdoor adds the bucket name to the path |\\n| Synology NAS | `Synology` | No, the DSM File Station API | Yes | No **Domain** field. The URL is based on **Endpoint** |\\n\\nCasdoor also supports `Qiniu Cloud Kodo`, `CUCloud OSS`, and `Casdoor`, which stores files in another Casdoor instance. The per-provider guides for [Amazon S3](/docs/provider/storage/amazon-s3), [MinIO](/docs/provider/storage/minio), [Azure Blob](/docs/provider/storage/azure), [Google Cloud Storage](/docs/provider/storage/google-cloudstorage), [Alibaba Cloud OSS](/docs/provider/storage/aliyun-oss), [Tencent Cloud COS](/docs/provider/storage/tencentCloudCOS), [Synology NAS](/docs/provider/storage/synology-nas) and the [local file system](/docs/provider/storage/localFileSystem) show which value goes in which field.\\n\\n## Endpoint vs. Domain\\n\\nThese two fields do different jobs, and most CDN problems come from mixing them up.\\n\\n- **Endpoint** is the address Casdoor uses to talk to the storage API: upload, delete, list. The CDN is not involved.\\n- **Endpoint (Intranet)** replaces Endpoint when `isCloudIntranet = true` is set in `app.conf`. That keeps upload traffic on the provider\'s internal network when Casdoor runs in the same cloud. The field appears for AWS S3, Aliyun OSS, Azure Blob and CUCloud OSS.\\n- **Domain** is the base of the URL that Casdoor saves and that browsers request. If you have a CDN, its domain goes here.\\n- **Path prefix** is put in front of every object key. With a prefix of `casdoor`, an avatar ends up under `casdoor/avatar/...`.\\n\\nCasdoor builds the file URL from the scheme and host of Domain, then the path of Domain, then the path prefix, then the file path. A Domain without a scheme gets `https://`. The path part of Domain also becomes part of the object key, so `https://cdn.example.com/casdoor/` stores files under `casdoor/` in the bucket. For Azure Blob and Google Cloud Storage, Casdoor adds the container or bucket name as the first path segment after the host. Your CDN needs to serve that path.\\n\\nIf Domain is empty, Casdoor fills it in from the provider\'s endpoint the first time it uses the provider, and saves it. Sometimes the result works, as with OSS and GCS. Sometimes it doesn\'t: for an S3 endpoint without the bucket name, or for MinIO, where path-style requests put the bucket name in the path, the generated URL misses the bucket. Set Domain yourself, upload one test avatar, and open the URL Casdoor returns before anyone else uses it.\\n\\nThe provider that Casdoor uses for avatars it copies from social logins comes from `defaultStorageProvider` in `app.conf`. It is the name of a provider owned by `admin` (see the [configuration reference](/docs/basic/configuration)):\\n\\n```ini\\ndefaultStorageProvider = provider_storage_s3\\nisCloudIntranet = false\\n```\\n\\n## Public buckets, private buckets and signed URLs\\n\\nCasdoor saves one plain, permanent URL in the user\'s `avatar` field and in the resource record. It doesn\'t create signed or expiring URLs for these files. So whatever is at Domain has to answer an anonymous `GET`. There are two ways to do that.\\n\\n**Make the objects public.** With `AWS S3` and `MinIO`, Casdoor uploads every object with the `public-read` canned ACL. With `Aliyun OSS`, the client library does the same by default. On S3 this conflicts with the defaults for new buckets: according to AWS, new buckets use the **Bucket owner enforced** setting, which disables ACLs, and an upload that sets an ACL such as `public-read` fails with `AccessControlListNotSupported`. That is why the [Amazon S3 guide](/docs/provider/storage/amazon-s3) has you turn on ACLs and turn off **Block all public access**. On OSS, an object ACL takes precedence over the bucket ACL, so a `public-read` object can be read even in a private bucket. MinIO doesn\'t use the ACL for anonymous reads. It uses a bucket policy:\\n\\n```bash\\nmc anonymous set download myminio/casdoor\\n```\\n\\nAzure needs anonymous access allowed on the storage account and the container set to the **Blob** access level. Google Cloud Storage needs the Storage Object Viewer role (`roles/storage.objectViewer`) granted to `allUsers` on the bucket.\\n\\n**Keep the bucket private and let only the CDN read it.** The CDN signs its requests to the origin, and viewers go through the CDN without a signature. Each vendor has its own version:\\n\\n- CloudFront has origin access control (OAC). AWS requires **Bucket owner enforced** for OAC, or **Bucket owner preferred** if you need ACLs.\\n- Alibaba Cloud CDN can fetch from a private OSS bucket after you authorize it.\\n- Tencent Cloud CDN has origin-pull authentication for private COS buckets.\\n- Azure Front Door Premium can reach a storage account over Private Link.\\n- Google\'s Cloud CDN guide covers private bucket access too, where the Storage Object Viewer role goes to a service account instead of `allUsers`.\\n\\nThis hides the bucket\'s own URL, but the files are still public through the CDN. With the `AWS S3` and `Aliyun OSS` types, the objects also stay readable at their bucket URLs, because of the `public-read` ACL that Casdoor sets.\\n\\nIf avatars must not be public at all, a CDN in front of object storage is the wrong tool. Keep those files on a storage type you control, and limit who can reach that host.\\n\\n## Cache headers and the avatar trap\\n\\nOn S3 and MinIO, Casdoor sets `Content-Type` from the file extension when it uploads. It doesn\'t set `Cache-Control` on any provider. That means the CDN\'s default TTL applies. On CloudFront, objects without `Cache-Control` are cached for the default TTL, which is 24 hours when you don\'t use a cache policy. To control this per object, add `Cache-Control` metadata in the bucket. For a whole path, use a cache rule on the CDN.\\n\\nAvatars need a short TTL. When a user changes their picture, Casdoor writes the new file to the same key, `avatar/<organization>/<username>.<extension>`, and saves the same URL. A long TTL means people see the old picture until it expires or until you purge or invalidate that path. Other uploads get a numbered suffix (`-1`, `-2`, ...) when the name is already taken, so a new upload gets a new URL and can be cached for a long time.\\n\\nA layout that works:\\n\\n| Path | Suggested caching |\\n|---|---|\\n| `avatar/*` | Short TTL (minutes), or purge on change |\\n| `resource/*` and other uploads | Long TTL |\\n| Hashed frontend assets (`assets/*`) | Very long TTL. The file name changes on every build |\\n\\n## Casdoor\'s own frontend on a CDN\\n\\nSeparate from user uploads, there are three ways to move Casdoor\'s own static files off the Casdoor server.\\n\\n**`frontendCdnUrl`.** Use this if you install the frontend from the `casdoor-web` npm package. Point it at an npm CDN, for example:\\n\\n```ini\\nfrontendCdnUrl = \\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/{name}@{version}\\"\\n```\\n\\nCasdoor reads the package name and version from the `package.json` in the build folder. It rewrites the script and stylesheet links in `index.html` to that CDN and keeps the local paths as a fallback. If a file fails to load, the browser loads it from Casdoor, and a cookie keeps that browser on local files for the rest of the session. A frontend you build yourself has no such `package.json`, so the setting has no effect on it.\\n\\n**The deployment script.** For a frontend you build from source, [Host the frontend files on a CDN](/docs/deployment/deploy-cdn) explains `deployment/deploy_test.go`. It uploads `web/build/assets/` through one of your storage providers and points `index.html` at the provider\'s Domain, which must end with `/`. Vite hashes these file names, so they suit the long TTL in the table above.\\n\\n**`staticBaseUrl`.** This sets where default images such as logos, provider icons and the default avatar come from. The default is `https://cdn.casbin.org`, which serves the [casbin/static](https://github.com/casbin/static) repository. On a network without internet access, serve a copy of that repository yourself and point `staticBaseUrl` at it, as described in [Host static files on an intranet](/docs/deployment/deploy-intranet).\\n\\n## Picking one\\n\\n- **One server, small user base:** `Local File System`. Mount the `files` directory on a volume if Casdoor runs in a container. Casdoor sends `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and a sandboxing `Content-Security-Policy` with these files, so an uploaded HTML or SVG file can\'t run script on your Casdoor domain.\\n- **Self-hosted and S3-shaped:** `MinIO`. It is also the type the docs suggest for other S3-compatible stores.\\n- **Already on a cloud:** use that cloud\'s object storage and CDN, set **Endpoint (Intranet)** where Casdoor runs in the same cloud, and set Domain to the CDN.\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- [Casdoor storage providers](/docs/provider/storage/overview) and the source: [`object/storage.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/storage.go), [`storage/`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/storage), [`routers/frontend_cdn.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/routers/frontend_cdn.go)\\n- AWS: [Controlling ownership of objects and disabling ACLs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/about-object-ownership.html), [Restrict access to an Amazon S3 origin](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-restricting-access-to-s3.html), [Manage how long content stays in the cache](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/Expiration.html)\\n- MinIO: [`mc anonymous set`](https://docs.min.io/community/minio-object-store/reference/minio-mc/mc-anonymous-set.html)\\n- Microsoft: [Configure anonymous read access for containers and blobs](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/storage/blobs/anonymous-read-access-configure), [Integrate an Azure Storage account with Azure Front Door](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/frontdoor/integrate-storage-account)\\n- Google Cloud: [Making data public](https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/making-data-public), [Set up Cloud CDN with a backend bucket](https://cloud.google.com/cdn/docs/setting-up-cdn-with-bucket)\\n- Alibaba Cloud: [Object ACL](https://www.alibabacloud.com/help/en/oss/user-guide/object-acl), [Origin fetch from private OSS buckets](https://www.alibabacloud.com/help/en/cdn/user-guide/grant-alibaba-cloud-cdn-access-permissions-on-private-oss-buckets)\\n- Tencent Cloud: [Setting CDN Acceleration for COS](https://www.tencentcloud.com/document/product/436/18670)"},{"id":"mailhog-vs-mailpit","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mailpit-vs-mailhog.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mailpit-vs-mailhog.mdx","title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Testen von Verifizierungs- und Passwort-Zur\xfccksetzung-E-Mails mit Casdoor","description":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Beide lauschen auf SMTP-Port 1025 und Web-UI-Port 8025. Vergleichen Sie Wartung, Docker-Images und Test-APIs, dann richten Sie Casdoor auf einen von beiden ein.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Email","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/email"},{"inline":true,"label":"MailHog","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/mail-hog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mailpit","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/mailpit"},{"inline":true,"label":"Testing","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/testing"},{"inline":true,"label":"SMTP","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/smtp"},{"inline":true,"label":"CI","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/ci"}],"readingTime":8.74,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mailhog-vs-mailpit","title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Testen von Verifizierungs- und Passwort-Zur\xfccksetzung-E-Mails mit Casdoor","description":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Beide lauschen auf SMTP-Port 1025 und Web-UI-Port 8025. Vergleichen Sie Wartung, Docker-Images und Test-APIs, dann richten Sie Casdoor auf einen von beiden ein.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Email","MailHog","Mailpit","Testing","SMTP","CI"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Host Static Files on a CDN with Casdoor: S3, MinIO, Azure Blob and Other Storage Providers","permalink":"/de/blog/file-storage-and-cdn"},"nextItem":{"title":"MCP OAuth Redirect URIs f\xfcr Cursor, Claude und ChatGPT: Registrierung von MCP-Clients in Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris"}},"content":"MailHog und Mailpit sind beide gef\xe4lschte SMTP-Server f\xfcr die Entwicklung: Sie akzeptieren jede Nachricht, speichern sie und zeigen sie in einer Web-UI an, statt sie zuzustellen. Beide verwenden dieselben Standardports, **SMTP auf 1025 sowie Web-UI und API auf 8025**, sodass die SMTP-Adresse `smtp://127.0.0.1:1025` ist und der Posteingang sich unter `http://127.0.0.1:8025` befindet. Der Unterschied liegt in der Wartung: Die letzte Ver\xf6ffentlichung von MailHog, v1.0.1, erschien im August 2020, w\xe4hrend Mailpit v1.33.0 am 31. Oktober 2026 ver\xf6ffentlichte. Verwenden Sie Mailpit f\xfcr neue Projekte. Casdoor sendet an beide \xfcber einen einfachen SMTP-Provider, sodass Sie Registrierungscodes, Passwort-Zur\xfccksetzungs-E-Mails und Magic Links ohne ein echtes Mail-Konto testen k\xf6nnen.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## MailHog vs Mailpit auf einen Blick {#mailhog-vs-mailpit-at-a-glance}\\n\\n| | MailHog | Mailpit |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Letzte Ver\xf6ffentlichung | v1.0.1, 2020-08-11 | v1.33.0, 2026-10-31 |\\n| Letzter Commit im Standard-Branch | 2022-08-02 | 2026-10-31 |\\n| Lizenz | MIT | MIT |\\n| Standard-SMTP-Port | 1025 | 1025 |\\n| Standard-Port f\xfcr Web-UI und API | 8025 | 8025 |\\n| Docker-Image | `mailhog/mailhog`, nur amd64 | `axllent/mailpit`, amd64, arm64 und 386 |\\n| HTTP-API | v1 und v2: list, get, delete, Suche nach `from`, `to` oder `containing` | v1: list, get, delete, Suche mit Filtern wie `to:` und `subject:`, Verkn\xfcpfung zur neuesten Nachricht (`latest`) |\\n| Standard-Speicher | Im Speicher (In Memory) | Tempor\xe4re SQLite-Datenbank, wird beim Beenden gel\xf6scht |\\n| Persistenter Speicher | MongoDB oder Maildir | SQLite-Datei (`MP_DATABASE`) oder rqlite |\\n| Nachrichtenlimit | Keines dokumentiert | Beh\xe4lt standardm\xe4\xdfig die neuesten 500 Nachrichten (`MP_MAX_MESSAGES`) |\\n| HTML-Pr\xfcfung, Link-Pr\xfcfung, Spam-Pr\xfcfung | Nein | Ja |\\n| Webhook bei neuer E-Mail | Nein | Ja (`MP_WEBHOOK_URL`) |\\n| SMTP TLS | Nicht angeboten | Optional STARTTLS oder SSL/TLS |\\n| Fehlerinjektion | Jim | Chaos |\\n| Nachricht an einen echten SMTP-Server senden | Ja | Ja |\\n| POP3-Server | Nein | Ja, optional |\\n| Basic Auth f\xfcr die Web-UI | Ja | Ja |\\n\\nDie Zahlen auf Docker Hub zeigen, dass viele Leute immer noch MailHog beziehen. Das spiegelt alte Tutorials und CI-Dateien wider, nicht aber Aktivit\xe4t: Ein offenes Issue im MailHog-Repository mit dem Titel \\"THIS PROJECT IS DEAD!\\" hat eine Antwort des Autors aus dem Jahr 2023, in der er sagt, dass er keine Zeit mehr habe, es zu pflegen. Die README von Mailpit beschreibt sich selbst als inspiriert von MailHog und verweist auf denselben Kommentar.\\n\\nDie Architektur des Images ist wichtiger, als es scheint. Auf einem Apple Silicon Laptop oder einem ARM-CI-Runner l\xe4uft `mailhog/mailhog` nur unter Emulation, w\xe4hrend `axllent/mailpit` ein natives arm64-Image hat.\\n\\n## F\xfchren Sie beide mit Docker aus {#run-either-one-with-docker}\\n\\nMailHog:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailhog -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 mailhog/mailhog\\n```\\n\\nMailpit:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailpit -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 axllent/mailpit\\n```\\n\\nMailpit speichert E-Mails in einer tempor\xe4ren Datenbank, die verschwindet, wenn der Container stoppt. Um sie \xfcber Neustarts hinweg zu behalten, mounten Sie ein Volume und setzen Sie `MP_DATABASE`:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailpit \\\\\\n  -v \\"$PWD/mailpit-data:/data\\" \\\\\\n  -e MP_DATABASE=/data/mailpit.db \\\\\\n  -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 \\\\\\n  axllent/mailpit\\n```\\n\\nOhne Docker funktionieren sowohl `brew install mailhog` als auch `brew install mailpit` auf macOS, und Mailpit bietet statische Binaries f\xfcr Windows, Linux und macOS auf seiner Release-Seite an.\\n\\nF\xfchren Sie es in GitHub Actions als Service-Container neben Ihrem Test-Job aus:\\n\\n```yaml\\nservices:\\n  mailpit:\\n    image: axllent/mailpit\\n    ports:\\n      - 1025:1025\\n      - 8025:8025\\n```\\n\\n## Casdoor auf MailHog oder Mailpit konfigurieren {#point-casdoor-at-mailhog-or-mailpit}\\n\\nCasdoor hat keinen separaten Provider-Typ f\xfcr MailHog oder Mailpit, und das braucht es auch nicht. Der E-Mail-Typ `Default` sendet \xfcber SMTP, und beide Tools sind SMTP-Server.\\n\\n1. Als globaler Administrator f\xfcgen Sie einen Provider in der Organisation `admin` oder `built-in` hinzu.\\n1. Legen Sie **Category** auf `Email` und **Type** auf `Default` fest.\\n1. Setzen Sie **Host** auf den Ort, an dem das Tool ausgef\xfchrt wird, und **Port** auf `1025`.\\n1. Lassen Sie **Username** und **Password** leer.\\n1. Klicken Sie auf **Test SMTP Connection**, dann auf **Send Testing Email**, und pr\xfcfen Sie die Web-UI auf Port 8025.\\n\\nDie Schritt-f\xfcr-Schritt-Anleitungen mit Screenshots sind [Test email with MailHog](/docs/provider/email/mailhog) und [Test email with Mailpit](/docs/provider/email/mailpit).\\n\\nEinige Details entscheiden dar\xfcber, ob dies beim ersten Versuch funktioniert:\\n\\n**Lassen Sie den SSL-Modus auf Auto oder setzen Sie ihn auf Disable.** Im Modus `Auto` verwendet Casdoor implizites TLS nur auf Port 465 und f\xfchrt ein Upgrade mit STARTTLS durch, nur wenn der Server dies anbietet. Weder MailHog noch ein Standard-Mailpit bieten STARTTLS auf Port 1025 an, daher bleibt die Verbindung unverschl\xfcsselt (plain). W\xe4hlen Sie nicht `Enable`: Dies erzwingt implizites TLS, und der Handshake schl\xe4gt gegen einen Plain-Text-Server fehl. Die Tabelle zum [SSL-Modus](/docs/provider/email/overview#ssl-mode) enth\xe4lt das vollst\xe4ndige Verhalten.\\n\\n**Lassen Sie die Zugangsdaten leer.** Casdoor meldet sich nur beim SMTP-Server an, wenn **Username** gesetzt ist. Wenn Sie es ausf\xfcllen, verweigert Go\'s `PLAIN`-Authentifizierung das Senden des Passworts \xfcber eine unverschl\xfcsselte Verbindung, es sei denn, der Host ist localhost. Daher schl\xe4gt eine MailHog-Instanz unter `192.168.x.x` mit einem Authentifizierungsfehler fehl, obwohl MailHog jede Anmeldung akzeptieren w\xfcrde. Mailpit fragt standardm\xe4\xdfig \xfcberhaupt nicht nach Authentifizierung.\\n\\n**Verwenden Sie den richtigen Host.** `127.0.0.1` funktioniert nur, wenn Casdoor und der Mail-Catcher auf derselben Maschine laufen. Wenn beide in Docker Compose laufen, verwenden Sie den Service-Namen, z. B. `mailpit`, als Host.\\n\\n**Verwenden Sie einen globalen Provider.** Ein Provider, der einer normalen Organisation geh\xf6rt, darf m\xf6glicherweise nur Internet-Adressen erreichen und kann sich daher nicht mit `127.0.0.1` oder einer privaten IP verbinden. Provider in `admin` oder `built-in` sind nicht eingeschr\xe4nkt. Siehe [Which addresses a provider can reach](/docs/provider/overview#which-addresses-a-provider-can-reach).\\n\\n**Setzen Sie `origin` f\xfcr Links.** Best\xe4tigungscodes funktionieren sofort. Links zur Passwortzur\xfccksetzung und Magic Links ben\xf6tigen, dass Casdoor seine \xf6ffentliche URL kennt, entweder \xfcber `origin` in `conf/app.conf` oder eine Anfrage auf einem Loopback-Host. Andernfalls wird der Reset-Link aus der E-Mail entfernt und Magic Links schlagen fehl. Siehe [Templates](/docs/provider/email/overview#email-content-and-placeholders).\\n\\nF\xfcgen Sie abschlie\xdfend den Provider zu Ihrer Anwendung hinzu unter [Add providers to an application](/docs/application/providers), damit Registrierung und Passwortzur\xfccksetzung ihn tats\xe4chlich nutzen.\\n\\n## Best\xe4tigungscode in einem automatisierten Test lesen {#read-the-verification-code-in-an-automated-test}\\n\\nCasdoors Best\xe4tigungscodes bestehen aus sechs Ziffern, und das Standard-Template lautet \\"Here is your code: \\" gefolgt vom Code. Ein End-to-End-Test registriert einen Benutzer, bittet Casdoor, den Code zu senden, und liest ihn dann \xfcber die API des Mail-Catchers aus.\\n\\nBei Mailpit suchen Sie nach dem Empf\xe4nger und \xf6ffnen Sie den neuesten Treffer. `GET /api/v1/search` gibt Treffer absteigend nach Datum zur\xfcck, und `GET /api/v1/message/{ID}` gibt die geparsten `Text`- und `HTML`-Bodies zur\xfcck:\\n\\n```ts\\nimport { request } from \\"@playwright/test\\";\\n\\nconst MAILPIT = process.env.MAILPIT_URL ?? \\"http://127.0.0.1:8025\\";\\n\\nexport async function latestCode(to: string): Promise<string> {\\n  const api = await request.newContext({ baseURL: MAILPIT });\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {\\n    const res = await api.get(\\"/api/v1/search\\", {\\n      params: { query: `to:\\"${to}\\"`, limit: \\"1\\" },\\n    });\\n    const { messages } = await res.json();\\n    if (messages.length > 0) {\\n      const msg = await (await api.get(`/api/v1/message/${messages[0].ID}`)).json();\\n      const match = (msg.Text || msg.HTML).match(/code: (\\\\d{6})/);\\n      if (match) return match[1];\\n    }\\n    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 500));\\n  }\\n  throw new Error(`no verification email for ${to}`);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWenn nur ein Test gleichzeitig E-Mails sendet, \xfcberspringt `GET /api/v1/message/latest` den Suchschritt. Vor jedem Test leert `DELETE /api/v1/messages` ohne Body das Postfach.\\n\\nBei MailHog ist der n\xe4chste Aufruf `GET /api/v2/search?kind=to&query=<address>`. Die Nachrichten werden in `items` beginnend mit der neuesten zur\xfcckgegeben, und der Inhalt befindet sich in `items[0].Content.Body`:\\n\\n```ts\\nconst res = await api.get(\\"/api/v2/search\\", {\\n  params: { kind: \\"to\\", query: to, limit: \\"1\\" },\\n});\\nconst { items } = await res.json();\\nconst body: string = items[0]?.Content.Body ?? \\"\\";\\n```\\n\\n`Content.Body` ist der rohe MIME-Body. Casdoor sendet im quoted-printable-Format, daher wird `=` zu `=3D`, und lange Zeilen erhalten weiche Umbr\xfcche, die mit `=` enden. Dekodieren Sie dies zuerst oder halten Sie den Code am Anfang einer kurzen Vorlage. Leeren Sie MailHog mit `DELETE /api/v1/messages`.\\n\\n## Migration von MailHog zu Mailpit {#migrating-from-mailhog-to-mailpit}\\n\\nDa die Ports \xfcbereinstimmen, sind die meisten Migrationen eine Einzeilige \xc4nderung in `docker-compose.yml`: Ersetzen Sie `mailhog/mailhog` durch `axllent/mailpit`. Der Provider von Casdoor beh\xe4lt denselben Host und Port bei. Was sich \xe4ndert:\\n\\n- **Einstellungen.** MailHog liest `MH_*` Variablen; Mailpit liest `MP_*`. Wenn Sie `MH_STORAGE=maildir` oder MongoDB verwendet haben, wechseln Sie zu `MP_DATABASE`.\\n- **Test-Helferprogramme.** Mailpit hat keine `/api/v2` Endpunkte. Schreiben Sie Aufrufe an `/api/v2/messages` und `/api/v2/search` f\xfcr `/api/v1/messages` und `/api/v1/search` um und lesen Sie `messages[].ID` sowie `Text` oder `HTML` anstelle von `items[].Content.Body`. Das quoted-printable-Dekodieren entf\xe4llt.\\n- **Suchsyntax.** `kind=to&query=a@example.com` wird zu `query=to:\\"a@example.com\\"`.\\n- **Fehlerinjektion.** Jim hat in Mailpit keine Flags; verwenden Sie Chaos.\\n- **Authentifizierung.** Wenn eine App darauf besteht, sich \xfcber unverschl\xfcsseltes SMTP anzumelden, starten Sie Mailpit mit `MP_SMTP_AUTH_ACCEPT_ANY=1` und `MP_SMTP_AUTH_ALLOW_INSECURE=1`. F\xfcr Casdoor ist es einfacher, die Anmeldedaten leer zu lassen.\\n- **Aufbewahrungsdauer.** Mailpit l\xf6scht standardm\xe4\xdfig auf die neuesten 500 Nachrichten. Erh\xf6hen Sie `MP_MAX_MESSAGES`, wenn ein langer Testlauf mehr ben\xf6tigt.\\n\\n## \xdcbergang zur Produktion {#going-to-production}\\n\\nMailHog und Mailpit liefern niemals E-Mails an echte Postf\xe4cher aus, es sei denn, Sie geben Nachrichten manuell frei, daher ben\xf6tigt die Produktion einen echten Provider. In Casdoor tauschen Sie den Provider aus, nicht die Vorlagen. Die E-Mail-Vorlagen, der `%s` Code-Platzhalter und der `<reset-link>` Block bleiben gleich:\\n\\n- Jeder SMTP-Server mit dem Typ `Default`, beschrieben in [E-Mail-Provider](/docs/provider/email/overview)\\n- [SendGrid](/docs/provider/email/sendgrid) und [Resend](/docs/provider/email/resend) \xfcber ihre APIs\\n- [Brevo](/docs/provider/email/brevo) \xfcber seinen SMTP-Relay\\n- [Azure Communication Services](/docs/provider/email/azureACS)\\n- Ihr eigener HTTP-Endpunkt mit [Custom HTTP Email](/docs/provider/email/customHttp)\\n\\nEin h\xe4ufiges Setup h\xe4lt Mailpit in Entwicklung und CI und den echten Provider in der Produktion. Jede Umgebung zeigt ihre Anwendung auf einen anderen Provider, sodass der Anmeldeprozess, den Sie testen, auch der ist, der ausgeliefert wird.\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- MailHog Repository und README: https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog\\n- MailHog Konfiguration und API v1/v2 Dokumentation: https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog/tree/master/docs\\n- MailHog Wartungskommentar: https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog/issues/442#issuecomment-1493415258\\n- Mailpit Repository und README: https://github.com/axllent/mailpit\\n- Mailpit Dokumentation (Docker, Speicher, SMTP, Webhook, Suchfilter): https://mailpit.axllent.org/docs/\\n- Mailpit API v1 Referenz: https://mailpit.axllent.org/docs/api-v1/\\n- Casdoor E-Mail-Provider Code: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/email"},{"id":"mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mcp-oauth-redirect-uris.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mcp-oauth-redirect-uris.mdx","title":"MCP OAuth Redirect URIs f\xfcr Cursor, Claude und ChatGPT: Registrierung von MCP-Clients in Casdoor","description":"Die OAuth-R\xfcckruf-URLs, die Cursor, Claude und ChatGPT f\xfcr MCP-Server verwenden, wo sich claude_desktop_config.json befindet und wie jeder Client in Casdoor registriert wird.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"MCP","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/mcp"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cursor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/cursor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Claude","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/claude"},{"inline":true,"label":"ChatGPT","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/chat-gpt"},{"inline":true,"label":"Dynamic Client Registration","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/dynamic-client-registration"}],"readingTime":8.06,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","title":"MCP OAuth Redirect URIs f\xfcr Cursor, Claude und ChatGPT: Registrierung von MCP-Clients in Casdoor","description":"Die OAuth-R\xfcckruf-URLs, die Cursor, Claude und ChatGPT f\xfcr MCP-Server verwenden, wo sich claude_desktop_config.json befindet und wie jeder Client in Casdoor registriert wird.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","MCP","OAuth","Cursor","Claude","ChatGPT","Dynamic Client Registration"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Testen von Verifizierungs- und Passwort-Zur\xfccksetzung-E-Mails mit Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit"},"nextItem":{"title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Sch\xfctzen Sie jede App hinter Nginx mit Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/nginx-sso-authentication"}},"content":"Jeder MCP-Client sendet seine eigene Redirect URI, wenn er sich bei einem OAuth-gesch\xfctzten MCP-Server anmeldet. Cursor Desktop verwendet `http://localhost:8787/callback` (Versionen vor Mitte 2026 verwendeten `cursor://anysphere.cursor-mcp/oauth/callback`), Cursor im Web und Cursor Cloud Agents verwenden `https://www.cursor.com/agents/mcp/oauth/callback`, claude.ai, Claude Desktop Connectors, Claude Mobile und Cowork verwenden `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`, und ChatGPT verwendet `https://chatgpt.com/connector_platform_oauth_redirect` oder eine pro Verbindung `https://chatgpt.com/connector/oauth/<callback-id>`. Wenn Sie die dynamische Client-Registrierung in Casdoor aktivieren, geben Sie keine dieser URLs ein: Die Clients registrieren sich selbst oder \xfcbergeben Casdoor ein Client-ID-Metadaten-Dokument. Sie f\xfcgen sie zu **Redirect URLs** hinzu, nur wenn Sie eine Casdoor-Anwendung f\xfcr einen Client manuell erstellen.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Redirect URIs nach Client {#redirect-uris-by-client}\\n\\n| Client | Redirect URI | Wie er eine Client-ID von Casdoor erh\xe4lt | Hinweise |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Cursor desktop (aktuell) | `http://localhost:8787/callback` | DCR, oder `auth.CLIENT_ID` in `mcp.json` | Fester Port 8787 |\\n| Cursor web und Cloud Agents | `https://www.cursor.com/agents/mcp/oauth/callback` | DCR, oder `auth.CLIENT_ID` | Mit DCR registriert die Desktop-App diese URI und die localhost-URI |\\n| Cursor desktop (\xe4ltere Versionen) | `cursor://anysphere.cursor-mcp/oauth/callback` | DCR, oder `auth.CLIENT_ID` | Cursor machte die Loopback-URI im Juli 2026 zum Standard |\\n| claude.ai, Claude Desktop connectors, Claude mobile, Cowork | `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback` | CIMD, dann DCR, oder eine Client-ID, die im Dialog f\xfcr benutzerdefinierte Connector eingegeben wird | Anthropics Server stellen die Anfragen |\\n| Claude Code | `http://localhost:<port>/callback` und `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback` | CIMD | Der Port \xe4ndert sich pro Sitzung |\\n| Claude Desktop \xfcber `mcp-remote` | `http://localhost:<port>/oauth/callback` | DCR | Der Port wird aus der Server-URL abgeleitet |\\n| ChatGPT | `https://chatgpt.com/connector_platform_oauth_redirect`, oder `https://chatgpt.com/connector/oauth/<callback-id>` | CIMD, DCR, oder ein vordefinierter Client | Kopieren Sie den exakten Wert von ChatGPT |\\n\\nDiese Werte stammen aus der Dokumentation der Anbieter vom 2. November 2026 (siehe [Referenzen](#references)). Clients \xe4ndern sie. Wenn eine Anmeldung fehlschl\xe4gt, lesen Sie den Parameter `redirect_uri` der Autorisierungsanfrage in der Adressleiste des Browsers und vergleichen Sie ihn mit dem, was Casdoor hat.\\n\\nDie Tabelle gilt f\xfcr zwei Szenarien: den [in Casdoor integrierten MCP-Server](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/overview) unter `/api/mcp` und Ihren eigenen MCP-Server, der [Casdoor als Autorisierungsserver verwendet](/docs/mcp-auth/overview). In beiden F\xe4llen findet der Client Casdoor \xfcber die Metadaten der gesch\xfctzten Ressource ([RFC 9728](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9728)) und erh\xe4lt seine Client-ID von Casdoor.\\n\\n## Option 1: Clients lassen sich selbst registrieren {#option-1-let-clients-register-themselves}\\n\\nCasdoor unterst\xfctzt die dynamische Client-Registrierung ([RFC 7591](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7591)) unter `/api/oauth/register` sowie Client-ID-Metadatendokumente (CIMD), bei denen die `client_id` eine HTTPS-URL eines JSON-Dokuments ist, das die Redirect-URIs des Clients auflistet. Ein Schalter steuert beides: **Enable dynamic client registration** in der Organisation. Er ist standardm\xe4\xdfig deaktiviert, f\xfcr jede Organisation einschlie\xdflich `built-in`.\\n\\nWenn der Schalter f\xfcr `built-in` aktiviert ist, zeigt das Discovery-Dokument, wonach die Clients suchen:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -s https://your-casdoor.com/.well-known/openid-configuration | jq \'{\\n  registration_endpoint,\\n  client_id_metadata_document_supported,\\n  code_challenge_methods_supported,\\n  token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported,\\n  authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported\\n}\'\\n```\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"registration_endpoint\\": \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/oauth/register\\",\\n  \\"client_id_metadata_document_supported\\": true,\\n  \\"code_challenge_methods_supported\\": [\\"S256\\"],\\n  \\"token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported\\": [\\"client_secret_basic\\", \\"client_secret_post\\", \\"private_key_jwt\\", \\"none\\"],\\n  \\"authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported\\": true\\n}\\n```\\n\\nJeder Client w\xe4hlt dann seine Methode:\\n\\n- **Claude** verwendet CIMD nur, wenn die Metadaten sowohl `client_id_metadata_document_supported: true` als auch `none` in `token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported` enthalten. Casdoor bietet beides an, sobald der Schalter aktiviert ist, daher identifiziert sich Claude mit einer Dokument-URL und Casdoor erstellt eine Anwendung namens `cimd_...`. Andernfalls greift Claude auf DCR zur\xfcck.\\n- **ChatGPT** bevorzugt CIMD mit der `client_id` `https://chatgpt.com/oauth/client.json` (oder einer Variante pro Callback) und verwendet DCR, wenn CIMD nicht verf\xfcgbar ist.\\n- **Cursor** registriert sich \xfcber DCR als \xf6ffentlicher Client ohne Secret. Die Dokumentation von Cursor erw\xe4hnt CIMD nicht.\\n- **`mcp-remote`** registriert sich \xfcber DCR.\\n\\nDie Redirect-URIs stammen aus der Registrierungsanfrage oder dem Metadatendokument, sodass nichts manuell eingegeben werden muss. Registrierte Clients laufen unter einer eingeschr\xe4nkten Rolle und ihre Tokens k\xf6nnen die Management-API nicht aufrufen. Siehe [Dynamic client registration](/docs/application/dynamic-client-registration) f\xfcr die vollst\xe4ndigen Regeln und die Organisation, in der ein Client landet.\\n\\n## Option 2: Eine Anwendung f\xfcr den Client erstellen {#option-2-create-an-application-for-the-client}\\n\\nWenn Sie die Registrierung deaktiviert lassen, erstellen Sie eine Anwendung pro Client, damit Sie diese einzeln widerrufen k\xf6nnen:\\n\\n1. Gehen Sie in der Casdoor Admin-Console zu **Identity** > **Applications** und f\xfcgen Sie eine Anwendung hinzu, beispielsweise `cursor-mcp`.\\n1. F\xfcgen Sie die Redirect-URIs des Clients aus der Tabelle zu **Redirect URLs** hinzu. F\xfcr Cursor f\xfcgen Sie alle drei hinzu, da verschiedene Cursor-Installationen unterschiedliche senden.\\n1. Setzen Sie **Category** auf `Agent`. Siehe [Set up Casdoor for your MCP server](/docs/mcp-auth/setup) f\xfcr Scopes und Grant-Typen.\\n1. Geben Sie die **Client ID** an den Client weiter.\\n\\nEin Client-Secret ist f\xfcr diese Clients optional. Casdoor akzeptiert einen Austausch des Authorization Codes ohne Secret, wenn die Anfrage PKCE verwendet hat, und Cursor, Claude und ChatGPT verwenden alle PKCE mit `S256`. Wenn ein Client dennoch ein Secret sendet, muss es korrekt sein.\\n\\nWo die Client-ID eingetragen wird:\\n\\n- **Cursor**: Ein `auth`-Objekt in `~/.cursor/mcp.json` oder `.cursor/mcp.json`. Mit einer statischen `CLIENT_ID` sendet die Cursor-Desktop-App nur ihre localhost-Redirect-URI.\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\n    \\"mcpServers\\": {\\n      \\"casdoor\\": {\\n        \\"url\\": \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/mcp\\",\\n        \\"auth\\": {\\n          \\"CLIENT_ID\\": \\"${env:CASDOOR_MCP_CLIENT_ID}\\",\\n          \\"scopes\\": [\\"application:read\\", \\"user:read\\"]\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  ```\\n\\n- **Claude**: Das OAuth-Client-ID-Feld im Dialog f\xfcr benutzerdefinierte Konnektoren. Registrieren Sie `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`.\\n- **ChatGPT**: ChatGPT funktioniert auch mit vordefinierten OAuth-Clients. Kopieren Sie die Redirect-URI von der Verwaltungsseite des MCP-Servers in ChatGPT, da davon abh\xe4ngt, welche der beiden Formen verwendet wird, welcher Autorisierungsserver genutzt wird.\\n\\nSchritt-f\xfcr-Schritt-Anleitungen: [Cursor](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-cursor), [Claude Desktop](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-claude-desktop), [ChatGPT](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-chatgpt).\\n\\n## Wie Casdoor Redirect-URIs abgleicht {#how-casdoor-matches-redirect-uris}\\n\\nCasdoor vergleicht die `redirect_uri` einer Anfrage mit jedem Eintrag in **Redirect URLs**:\\n\\n- Schema, Host und Pfad m\xfcssen \xfcbereinstimmen. Die Query String wird nicht verglichen.\\n- Der Port muss \xfcbereinstimmen, au\xdfer bei Loopback-Hosts. Wenn beide URIs denselben Loopback-Host verwenden (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1` oder `::1`), ignoriert Casdoor den Port, wie es [RFC 8252](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8252#section-7.3) f\xfcr native Apps vorsieht. `localhost` und `127.0.0.1` sind unterschiedliche Hosts, daher ben\xf6tigt Claude Code sowohl `http://localhost/callback` als auch `http://127.0.0.1/callback`.\\n- Eine Redirect-URI auf einer Subdomain eines registrierten Hosts stimmt ebenfalls \xfcberein, sodass `https://example.com/cb` `https://app.example.com/cb` akzeptiert. Registrieren Sie den spezifischsten Host, den Sie k\xf6nnen.\\n- Benutzerdefinierte Schemata wie `cursor://` stimmen nach denselben Regeln \xfcberein. `javascript:`-, `data:`- und `vbscript:`-URIs werden immer abgelehnt.\\n\\nDa der Loopback-Port ignoriert wird, funktioniert eine Registrierung von `mcp-remote` oder Claude Code weiterhin, wenn sich der lokale Port zwischen Sitzungen \xe4ndert.\\n\\n## Speicherort der claude_desktop_config.json {#where-claude_desktop_configjson-lives}\\n\\nDie [MCP-Dokumentation](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/connect-local-servers) gibt diese Pfade an. In Claude Desktop \xf6ffnet **Settings** > **Developer** > **Edit Config** die Datei und erstellt sie, falls sie fehlt.\\n\\n| Operating system | Path                                                              |\\n|---|---|\\n| macOS            | `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` |\\n| Windows          | `%APPDATA%\\\\Claude\\\\claude_desktop_config.json`                   |\\n| Linux            | `~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json`                     |\\n\\nClaude Desktop erreicht einen Remote-MCP-Server auf eine von zwei Arten, und sie verwenden unterschiedliche Redirect-URIs:\\n\\n- **Als Konnektor**, hinzugef\xfcgt in den Connectoreinstellungen von Claude. Die Server von Anthropic f\xfchren den OAuth-Flow aus, die Redirect-URI ist `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`, und Casdoor muss vom Internet aus erreichbar sein.\\n- **\xdcber `claude_desktop_config.json`**, mit `mcp-remote` als lokaler Br\xfccke. Der OAuth-Flow l\xe4uft auf Ihrem Computer, und die Redirect-URI ist `http://localhost:<port>/oauth/callback`:\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\n    \\"mcpServers\\": {\\n      \\"casdoor\\": {\\n        \\"command\\": \\"npx\\",\\n        \\"args\\": [\\"mcp-remote\\", \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/mcp\\"]\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  ```\\n\\nBeenden Sie Claude Desktop vollst\xe4ndig und starten Sie es neu, nachdem Sie die Datei bearbeitet haben.\\n\\n## Hosting-Clients ben\xf6tigen ein \xf6ffentliches Casdoor {#hosted-clients-need-a-public-casdoor}\\n\\nChatGPT- und Claude-Konnektoren rufen Ihren MCP-Server und Casdoor von den Servern ihrer Anbieter aus auf, nicht von Ihrem Computer. Casdoor muss \xfcber \xf6ffentliches HTTPS mit einem g\xfcltigen Zertifikat erreichbar sein. Anfragen von Anthropic kommen aus `160.79.104.0/21`, falls Sie nach IP filtern. Cursor Desktop, Claude Code und `mcp-remote` f\xfchren den Flow lokal aus, daher funktioniert ein Casdoor in Ihrem Netzwerk f\xfcr sie.\\n\\nZwei weitere Dinge, die Hosting-Clients pr\xfcfen, was Casdoor bereits tut:\\n\\n- **Issuer in der Antwort** ([RFC 9207](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9207)): ChatGPT lehnt eine Autorisierungsantwort ohne das richtige `iss` ab und verwendet seine stabile Redirect-URI nur mit Autorisierungsservern, die die Anforderungen von RFC 9207 erf\xfcllen. Casdoor wirbt diese Unterst\xfctzung in seinen Metadaten an und f\xfcgt `iss` zur Weiterleitung hinzu.\\n- **Resource indicators** ([RFC 8707](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8707)): ChatGPT sendet `resource` mit der MCP-Server-URL bei den Autorisierungs- und Token-Anfragen. Casdoor setzt diesen Wert im `aud`-Claim des Access-Tokens, und Ihr MCP-Server sollte dies \xfcberpr\xfcfen. Siehe [MCP authentication](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/authentication) f\xfcr den Discovery-Flow.\\n\\nVerwandte Artikel auf www.casdoor.com: [OAuth for AI agents](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/oauth-for-ai-agents) und [authorization servers for MCP](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/mcp-authorization-servers).\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- Cursor: [Model Context Protocol (MCP)](https://cursor.com/docs/mcp), Abschnitt \\"Static OAuth for remote servers\\"\\n- Cursor Forum: [OAuth redirect URI changed from cursor:// to http://localhost](https://forum.cursor.com/t/oauth-redirect-uri-changed-from-cursor-to-http-localhost-for-streamable-http-mcp/165019)\\n- Anthropic: [Authentication for connectors](https://claude.com/docs/connectors/building/authentication)\\n- Model Context Protocol: [Connect to local MCP servers](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/connect-local-servers)\\n- OpenAI: [Authenticate users](https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/auth)\\n- `mcp-remote`: [README](https://github.com/geelen/mcp-remote)\\n- Casdoor: [Dynamic client registration](/docs/application/dynamic-client-registration), [Set up Casdoor for your MCP server](/docs/mcp-auth/setup)"},{"id":"nginx-sso-authentication","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/nginx-sso-authentication","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-nginx-sso-authentication.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-nginx-sso-authentication.mdx","title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Sch\xfctzen Sie jede App hinter Nginx mit Casdoor","description":"F\xfcgen Sie vor jeder App hinter nginx einen Login mit CAS, OIDC oder auth_request ein, indem Sie Casdoor verwenden: Forward Auth, OAuth2 Proxy, ein nginx-Modul, CAS-Clients und MFA im Vergleich.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"nginx","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/nginx"},{"inline":true,"label":"CAS","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/cas"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"MFA","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/mfa"},{"inline":true,"label":"forward auth","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/forward-auth"}],"readingTime":10.56,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"nginx-sso-authentication","title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Sch\xfctzen Sie jede App hinter Nginx mit Casdoor","description":"F\xfcgen Sie vor jeder App hinter nginx einen Login mit CAS, OIDC oder auth_request ein, indem Sie Casdoor verwenden: Forward Auth, OAuth2 Proxy, ein nginx-Modul, CAS-Clients und MFA im Vergleich.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","nginx","CAS","SSO","OIDC","MFA","forward auth"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"MCP OAuth Redirect URIs f\xfcr Cursor, Claude und ChatGPT: Registrierung von MCP-Clients in Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris"},"nextItem":{"title":"RP-Initiated Logout and SSO Logout in OpenID Connect: How Casdoor Signs Users Out","permalink":"/de/blog/oidc-rp-initiated-logout"}},"content":"Open-Source-nginx hat kein CAS-Modul, daher bedeutet \\"nginx CAS authentication\\" in der Praxis eines von zwei Setups. Entweder fragt nginx jeden Request \xfcber sein `auth_request`-Modul bei einem Authentifizierungsdienst ab, und Casdoor antwortet (direkt unter `/api/forward-auth`, oder \xfcber casdoor-forward-auth oder OAuth2 Proxy), oder die App hinter nginx ist selbst ein CAS-Client und kommuniziert mit den CAS-Server-Endpunkten von Casdoor. Wenn Sie nginx neu kompilieren k\xf6nnen, f\xfchrt das Casdoor-nginx-Modul den Login innerhalb von nginx aus, und wenn Sie nginx gar nicht betreiben m\xf6chten, kann der Site-Proxy von Casdoor vor der App sitzen. In jedem Fall ist die Login-Seite die von Casdoor, sodass eine SSO-Sitzung, ein Satz Berechtigungen und eine MFA-Richtlinie alle Apps abdecken.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Die Optionen auf einen Blick {#the-options-at-a-glance}\\n\\n| Option | Login-Logik l\xe4uft in | Was nginx ben\xf6tigt | Protokoll zu Casdoor | Wer erh\xe4lt Zugriff | W\xe4hlen Sie es, wenn |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Casdoor forward auth | Casdoor, `/api/forward-auth` | `auth_request` | Casdoor-Sitzungscookie auf der Domain der App | Anwendungs-Berechtigungen, mindestens einmal pro Minute erneut \xfcberpr\xfcft | nginx steht bereits davor und Sie m\xf6chten keinen zus\xe4tzlichen Dienst |\\n| casdoor-forward-auth | Ein kleiner Go-Dienst | `auth_request` | OIDC Authorization Code | Casdoor-Rollen oder -Gruppen, pro Location | Ein Sitzungscookie, das von vielen Subdomains geteilt wird |\\n| OAuth2 Proxy | OAuth2 Proxy | `auth_request` | OIDC | `allowed_groups`, E-Mail-Domains | Sie betreiben bereits OAuth2 Proxy |\\n| casdoor-nginx-module | nginx selbst | Ein kompiliertes Modul | OIDC mit PKCE, Tokens lokal verifiziert | Jeder angemeldete Benutzer der App | Kein zus\xe4tzlicher Prozess, plus Bearer-Tokens f\xfcr APIs |\\n| CAS-Client in der App | Die App | Nichts, sie proxyt nur | CAS 1.0, 2.0, 3.0 | Was auch immer die App mit den Attributen macht | Die App unterst\xfctzt bereits CAS |\\n| Casdoor site proxy | Casdoor auf Ports 80 und 443 | Kein nginx | Casdoor-Sitzung | Anwendungs-Berechtigungen | Nichts anderes steht vor der App |\\n\\nDie ersten drei unterscheiden sich nur darin, wer die Subrequest von nginx beantwortet.\\n\\n## Warum es kein \\"nginx CAS module\\" gibt {#why-there-is-no-nginx-cas-module}\\n\\nCAS ist eine Unterhaltung zwischen einer Anwendung und dem CAS-Server: Die App leitet den Browser zum `/login` des Servers mit einer `service`-URL weiter, erh\xe4lt ein Service-Ticket auf dieser URL zur\xfcck und validiert das Ticket mit einem Server-zu-Server-Aufruf. Apache httpd hat Apereos [mod_auth_cas](https://github.com/apereo/mod_auth_cas), um dies am Webserver durchzuf\xfchren, aber nginx hat kein CAS-Modul unter seinen offiziellen Modulen. F\xfcr nginx ist der Standard-Baustein [`ngx_http_auth_request_module`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_auth_request_module.html): Bevor nginx einen Request bedient, sendet es eine Subrequest an eine von Ihnen gew\xe4hlte URL. Eine 2xx-Antwort l\xe4sst den Request durch, 401 oder 403 verweigern ihn mit diesem Code, und jeder andere Code ist ein Fehler.\\n\\nF\xfcr den Benutzer sehen ein CAS-Login und ein OIDC-Login gleich aus: eine Weiterleitung zur Casdoor-Login-Seite und zur\xfcck. Daher ist die praktische Antwort f\xfcr eine App ohne CAS-Unterst\xfctzung OIDC auf der nginx-Ebene. Hintergrund zum Protokoll: [What is CAS](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-cas).\\n\\nDas auth_request-Modul ist standardm\xe4\xdfig nicht eingebaut: F\xfcgen Sie `--with-http_auth_request_module` beim Kompilieren hinzu, oder pr\xfcfen Sie `nginx -V` bei einem gepackten nginx.\\n\\n## Option 1: Casdoors eigenes Forward Auth {#option-1-casdoors-own-forward-auth}\\n\\nCasdoor kann die Subrequest von nginx selbst beantworten. Sie f\xfcgen eine **Site** in Casdoor hinzu, deren **Domain** die Domain der App ist und deren **Casdoor app** die Anwendung ist, in die sich Benutzer einloggen, und f\xfcgen `https://app.example.com/caswaf-handler` zu den **Redirect URLs** dieser Anwendung hinzu. Host und Port k\xf6nnen leer bleiben, da der Traffic nicht durch Casdoor geht.\\n\\nDa `auth_request` keine Weiterleitungen folgen kann, antwortet Casdoor auf eine unauthentifizierte Subrequest mit `401` und setzt die Login-URL in `Location`. nginx wandelt dies in einen 302 um:\\n\\n```nginx\\nserver {\\n    server_name app.example.com;\\n\\n    proxy_buffer_size 16k;\\n    proxy_buffers 8 16k;\\n\\n    location = /caswaf-handler {\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor:8000/api/forward-auth;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $http_host;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Uri $request_uri;\\n    }\\n\\n    location = /casdoor-auth {\\n        internal;\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor:8000/api/forward-auth;\\n        proxy_pass_request_body off;\\n        proxy_set_header Content-Length \\"\\";\\n        proxy_set_header X-Original-URL $scheme://$http_host$request_uri;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Method $request_method;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\\n    }\\n\\n    location / {\\n        auth_request /casdoor-auth;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_redirect $upstream_http_location;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_user $upstream_http_x_forwarded_user;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_email $upstream_http_x_forwarded_email;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_groups $upstream_http_x_forwarded_groups;\\n        error_page 401 =302 $casdoor_redirect;\\n\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-User $casdoor_user;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Email $casdoor_email;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Groups $casdoor_groups;\\n        proxy_pass http://app:3000;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nDas Backend empf\xe4ngt den Benutzer in `X-Forwarded-User`, `X-Forwarded-Email` und `X-Forwarded-Groups`. Der Zugriff folgt einer [permission](/docs/permission/permission-configuration) auf der Anwendung, die mindestens einmal pro Minute erneut \xfcberpr\xfcft wird, sodass ein deaktivierter Benutzer schnell den Zugriff verliert. Die **Public paths** der Site (Webhooks, Health Checks) werden ohne Login passiert.\\n\\nSiehe [Verwenden Ihres eigenen Reverse Proxys](/docs/site/overview#using-your-own-reverse-proxy-forward-auth) f\xfcr die Referenz und [Login zu einer App hinzuf\xfcgen ohne OIDC](/docs/site/protect-app) f\xfcr eine Grafana-Anleitung. Die App darf nur \xfcber nginx erreichbar sein, da sonst jeder `X-Forwarded-User` selbst senden kann.\\n\\n## Option 2: casdoor-forward-auth oder OAuth2 Proxy {#option-2-casdoor-forward-auth-or-oauth2-proxy}\\n\\nWenn Sie nicht f\xfcr jede App eine Casdoor-Site erstellen m\xf6chten, f\xfchren Sie einen kleinen Dienst aus, der den OIDC-Flow gegen Casdoor durchf\xfchrt und die Unteranfrage beantwortet.\\n\\n[casdoor-forward-auth](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-forward-auth) ist Casdoors eigenst\xe4ndiger Dienst daf\xfcr (er hat traefik-casdoor-auth ersetzt). Er speichert den Benutzer in einem signierten Cookie ohne Server-State. F\xfcr nginx stellt es `/verify` bereit, das Identit\xe4ts-Header mit 200 oder 401 beantwortet, und nginx leitet bei 401 auf sein `/login` um:\\n\\n```nginx\\nserver {\\n    server_name app.example.com;\\n\\n    location = /_casdoor_verify {\\n        internal;\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor-forward-auth:9999/verify?groups=built-in/dev;\\n        proxy_pass_request_body off;\\n        proxy_set_header Content-Length \\"\\";\\n    }\\n\\n    location @casdoor_login {\\n        return 302 https://auth.example.com/login?rd=$scheme://$http_host$request_uri;\\n    }\\n\\n    location / {\\n        auth_request /_casdoor_verify;\\n        error_page 401 = @casdoor_login;\\n\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_user $upstream_http_x_forwarded_user;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_roles $upstream_http_x_forwarded_roles;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-User $casdoor_user;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Roles $casdoor_roles;\\n        proxy_pass http://app:8080;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nDie Query-Parameter `roles` und `groups` in der Verify-URL beschr\xe4nken jede App auf Inhaber einer Casdoor-Rolle oder Mitglieder einer Gruppe; angemeldete Benutzer ohne diese erhalten 403. Rollen und Gruppen werden beim Login gelesen und f\xfcr die Sitzung beibehalten, sodass \xc4nderungen erst nach erneuter Anmeldung wirksam werden. Das Setzen von `COOKIE_DOMAIN=example.com` erm\xf6glicht es, dass ein Login alle Subdomains abdeckt. Der [Traefik-Leitfaden](/docs/integration/go/traefik) behandelt denselben Dienst f\xfcr Traefik.\\n\\n[OAuth2 Proxy](https://oauth2-proxy.github.io/oauth2-proxy/configuration/integrations/nginx) funktioniert auf dieselbe Weise mit `provider = \\"oidc\\"` und `oidc_issuer_url`, das auf Ihre Casdoor-URL gesetzt ist. Sein Endpunkt `/oauth2/auth` gibt 202 oder 401 zur\xfcck, User- und Email-Header ben\xf6tigen `--set-xauthrequest`, und die Behandlung von Forwarded-Headers im Nginx-Beispiel erfordert `--reverse-proxy`. Zwei Casdoor-Einstellungen sind wichtig: Verwenden Sie das Token-Format `JWT-Standard`, andernfalls werden Benutzer mit unverifizierter E-Mail abgelehnt, und behalten Sie den Scope `profile` bei, damit Gruppen und Rollen zur\xfcckgegeben werden. Siehe [OAuth2 Proxy mit Casdoor](/docs/integration/go/oauth2-proxy) und den [Nginx Community Edition Leitfaden](/docs/integration/C++/NginxCommunityVersion).\\n\\n## Option 3: Das Casdoor nginx-Modul {#option-3-the-casdoor-nginx-module}\\n\\n[casdoor-nginx-module](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-nginx-module) f\xfchrt den Login innerhalb von nginx durch, ohne separaten Prozess. Browser ohne Sitzung gehen zur Casdoor-Login-Seite; das Modul tauscht den Code mit PKCE aus, verifiziert die Signatur des Access Tokens, `exp`, `aud` und Typ gegen das Casdoor-Zertifikat und speichert den Benutzer in einem signierten Cookie. API-Clients k\xf6nnen `Authorization: Bearer` mit einem Casdoor Access Token senden, das lokal ohne Aufruf an Casdoor verifiziert wird.\\n\\nEs ben\xf6tigt nginx 1.22 oder neuer und OpenSSL 1.1.1 oder neuer. Sie k\xf6nnen es als dynamisches Modul gegen das bereits ausgef\xfchrte nginx bauen:\\n\\n```bash\\n./configure --with-compat --with-http_ssl_module --add-dynamic-module=/path/to/casdoor-nginx-module\\nmake modules\\ncp objs/ngx_http_casdoor_module.so /etc/nginx/modules/\\n```\\n\\n```nginx\\nload_module modules/ngx_http_casdoor_module.so;\\n\\nevents {}\\n\\nhttp {\\n    server {\\n        listen 443 ssl;\\n        server_name app.example.com;\\n        ssl_certificate     /etc/nginx/app.crt;\\n        ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/app.key;\\n\\n        casdoor_endpoint        https://door.example.com;\\n        casdoor_client_id       <client-id>;\\n        casdoor_client_secret   <client-secret>;\\n        casdoor_certificate     /etc/nginx/casdoor.pem;\\n        casdoor_redirect_uri    https://app.example.com/casdoor/callback;\\n        casdoor_token_location  /_casdoor/token;\\n\\n        location = /_casdoor/token {\\n            internal;\\n            proxy_pass https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token;\\n            proxy_ssl_server_name on;\\n        }\\n\\n        location = /casdoor/callback {\\n            casdoor_callback;\\n        }\\n\\n        location / {\\n            casdoor on;\\n            proxy_set_header X-User $casdoor_user;\\n            proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nErsetzen Sie die Platzhalter durch die Werte der Anwendung. Die Sitzung wird nicht erneut mit Casdoor \xfcberpr\xfcft, bis sie abl\xe4uft, daher wirkt sich das Deaktivieren eines Benutzers erst aus, wenn die Sitzung endet; `casdoor_session_timeout` verk\xfcrzt dieses Zeitfenster. Das Modul hat keine Rollen- oder Gruppen\xfcberpr\xfcfungen. Bei NGINX Plus siehe stattdessen [den NGINX Plus Leitfaden](/docs/integration/C++/Nginx).\\n\\n## Option 4: Die App ist ein CAS-Client {#option-4-the-app-is-a-cas-client}\\n\\nWenn die App bereits CAS spricht, z. B. eine Java-App mit dem Apereo CAS Client, proxyt nginx nur und Casdoor ist der CAS-Server. Jede Casdoor-Anwendung hat CAS-Endpunkte unter `/cas/<organization>/<application>`: `/login` und `/logout`, `/validate` f\xfcr CAS 1.0, `/serviceValidate`, `/proxyValidate` und `/proxy` f\xfcr 2.0, `/p3/serviceValidate` und `/p3/proxyValidate` f\xfcr 3.0 sowie `/samlValidate` f\xfcr SAML 1.1 Validierung.\\n\\nDie App sendet den Browser an `/login?service=...`, Casdoor leitet mit `ticket=ST-...` zur\xfcck, und die App validiert es:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl \\"https://door.example.com/cas/my-org/my-app/p3/serviceValidate?ticket=ST-1234567890&service=https%3A%2F%2Fapp.example.com%2F\\"\\n```\\n\\nEine erfolgreiche Antwort ist ein `cas:serviceResponse` mit `cas:user` und Attributen wie E-Mail, Anzeigename und `memberOf`; f\xfcgen Sie `format=json` f\xfcr JSON hinzu. H\xe4ufige Fehlerquellen:\\n\\n- Jede `service` URL muss in den **Redirect URLs** der Anwendung aufgef\xfchrt sein, und der `service` bei der Validierung muss exakt mit dem \xfcbereinstimmen, f\xfcr den das Ticket ausgestellt wurde.\\n- Tickets funktionieren einmal und laufen nach 5 Minuten ab.\\n- Tickets werden im Speicher gehalten, daher muss die Validierung bei mehreren Casdoor-Replikas die Replika erreichen, die das Ticket ausgestellt hat (Sticky Sessions).\\n- Hinter nginx geben Sie `Host` und `X-Forwarded-Proto` weiter, damit die App dieselbe `service` URL aufbaut, die der Browser gesehen hat.\\n\\nDas Setup, einschlie\xdflich der Filter des Java-Clients f\xfcr jede CAS-Version, finden Sie unter [Einen CAS-Client verbinden](/docs/how-to-connect/cas).\\n\\n## Option 5: Lassen Sie Casdoor den Reverse Proxy \xfcbernehmen {#option-5-let-casdoor-be-the-reverse-proxy}\\n\\nWenn nichts anderes vor der App sitzt, kann Casdoor sie direkt bedienen: Sein Site-Proxy lauscht auf `gatewayHttpPort` und `gatewayHttpsPort` (standardm\xe4\xdfig 80 und 443), sobald eine Site existiert. Setzen Sie den **Host** der Site auf das Backend, w\xe4hlen Sie ein Zertifikat, setzen Sie **Casdoor app** und leiten Sie DNS auf Casdoor. Login, Berechtigungen und Header verhalten sich wie in Option 1. Siehe [Sites](/docs/site/overview).\\n\\n## MFA hinzuf\xfcgen {#adding-mfa}\\n\\nMFA erfordert keine \xc4nderung in nginx oder in der App. Casdoor fragt den zweiten Faktor auf seiner eigenen Login-Seite ab, bevor es einen OAuth-Autorisierungscode oder ein CAS Service Ticket ausstellt, daher erh\xe4lt jede oben genannte Option dies. Benutzer k\xf6nnen eine TOTP-App, SMS, E-Mail oder RADIUS verwenden, mit Wiederherstellungscodes, und Administratoren k\xf6nnen MFA f\xfcr eine Organisation mit [MFA items](/docs/organization/mfa-items) erzwingen. Dies beantwortet auch \\"CAS 2FA\\": Mit Casdoor als CAS-Server ist der zweite Faktor Teil des CAS-Logins.\\n\\nMFA wird beim Login erzwungen, nicht pro Anfrage: Eine Sitzung, die nginx oder ein Forward-Auth-Dienst bereits h\xe4lt, bleibt g\xfcltig, bis sie abl\xe4uft, und die **MFA remember time** der Organisation erlaubt es Benutzern, den zweiten Faktor auf einem vertrauensw\xfcrdigen Ger\xe4t f\xfcr eine Weile zu \xfcberspringen. Siehe den [MFA Leitfaden](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication) und [Was ist MFA](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-mfa).\\n\\nSobald Benutzer bei Casdoor angemeldet sind, erreichen sie andere Apps, die sie an Casdoor senden, ohne neue Passwortabfrage; mit **Auto signin** \xfcberspringt Casdoor auch die Kontoauswahl. Siehe [Single Sign-On](/docs/session/single-sign-on) und [Single Logout](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/single-logout).\\n\\n## Welche Option w\xe4hlen? {#which-one-to-pick}\\n\\n- Kein Login in der App und nginx bereits davor: Casdoors Forward Auth.\\n- Ein Login \xfcber viele Subdomains oder Rollenpr\xfcfungen pro Standort: casdoor-forward-auth (oder OAuth2 Proxy, falls Sie diesen bereits betreiben).\\n- Sie bauen nginx selbst und sch\xfctzen APIs zus\xe4tzlich mit Bearer-Tokens: casdoor-nginx-module.\\n- Die App unterst\xfctzt CAS: Casdoors CAS-Endpunkte, nginx als einfacher Proxy.\\n- Noch kein Proxy vorhanden: Casdoors Site-Proxy.\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- nginx: [ngx_http_auth_request_module](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_auth_request_module.html)\\n- OAuth2 Proxy: [nginx integration](https://oauth2-proxy.github.io/oauth2-proxy/configuration/integrations/nginx)\\n- Apereo: [CAS Protocol Specification](https://apereo.github.io/cas/7.1.x/protocol/CAS-Protocol-Specification.html), [mod_auth_cas](https://github.com/apereo/mod_auth_cas)\\n- Casdoor: [casdoor-forward-auth](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-forward-auth), [casdoor-nginx-module](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-nginx-module)\\n- Casdoor docs: [Sites](/docs/site/overview), [Connect a CAS client](/docs/how-to-connect/cas), [MFA](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication)"},{"id":"oidc-rp-initiated-logout","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/oidc-rp-initiated-logout","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-oidc-rp-initiated-logout.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-oidc-rp-initiated-logout.mdx","title":"RP-Initiated Logout and SSO Logout in OpenID Connect: How Casdoor Signs Users Out","description":"How OIDC RP-initiated logout, back-channel logout and token revocation work in Casdoor, what each one clears, and how single sign-out spans several apps.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"Logout","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/logout"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Session","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/session"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/o-auth"}],"readingTime":10.53,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"oidc-rp-initiated-logout","title":"RP-Initiated Logout and SSO Logout in OpenID Connect: How Casdoor Signs Users Out","description":"How OIDC RP-initiated logout, back-channel logout and token revocation work in Casdoor, what each one clears, and how single sign-out spans several apps.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","OIDC","Logout","SSO","Session","OAuth"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Sch\xfctzen Sie jede App hinter Nginx mit Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/nginx-sso-authentication"},"nextItem":{"title":"RADIUS Multi-Factor Authentication for VPN and Wi-Fi: TOTP 2FA with the Casdoor RADIUS Server","permalink":"/de/blog/radius-mfa-vpn-wifi"}},"content":"Logging a user out of an OpenID Connect app takes more than deleting your own session cookie. The user is still signed in at the identity provider, so the next \\"Sign in\\" click goes straight through without a password, and the other apps that share that identity provider session stay signed in too. A proper logout has three parts: clear your app\'s local session, send the browser to the provider\'s `end_session_endpoint` (RP-initiated logout) so the provider session ends, and let the provider tell the other apps through back-channel logout. With Casdoor, the end-session endpoint is `/api/logout`, back-channel logout is configured per application, and refresh tokens can be revoked at `/api/login/oauth/revoke`.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Logout mechanisms at a glance\\n\\n| Mechanism | Who triggers it | What it clears | Casdoor support |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Local app logout | Your app | Your app\'s session cookie and stored tokens only | Your code |\\n| RP-initiated logout | Your app, by redirecting the browser | The Casdoor browser session and the token of the `id_token_hint` | Yes, `GET` or `POST /api/logout`, announced as `end_session_endpoint` |\\n| Back-channel logout | Casdoor, server to server | The sessions of other apps, each app ends its own | Yes, per application **Backchannel logout URL** |\\n| Front-channel logout | The provider, through hidden iframes | The sessions of other apps, through the browser | No, Casdoor doesn\'t announce `frontchannel_logout_supported` |\\n| Token revocation (RFC 7009) | Your app, server or client | One access or refresh token, and the tokens that share its refresh token | Yes, `POST /api/login/oauth/revoke`, announced as `revocation_endpoint` |\\n| Casdoor SSO logout | Your app, with the user\'s token or cookie | All sessions and tokens of the user, or the current session only | Yes, `/api/sso-logout`, Casdoor-specific |\\n| SAML single logout | Casdoor or a SAML service provider | Sessions in SAML apps | Yes, signed `LogoutRequest` |\\n\\n## Local session vs. identity provider session\\n\\nThere are at least two sessions involved in every OIDC sign-in, and often three:\\n\\n1. **Your app\'s session.** Usually a cookie that maps to the user and the tokens you got from the token endpoint.\\n2. **The Casdoor session.** A cookie on the Casdoor domain. As long as it is valid, Casdoor signs the user in to any app of the organization without a password, after a one-click account picker or silently with automatic sign-in. That is what makes [single sign-on](/docs/session/single-sign-on) work.\\n3. **An upstream identity provider session**, when the user signed in to Casdoor through Google, GitHub, an enterprise IdP and so on.\\n\\nAuth0 describes the same three layers in its logout documentation (application, Auth0 and identity provider session layers), and the model applies to any OIDC provider.\\n\\n\\"Localized application session termination\\" ends only the first one: your app forgets the user, the Casdoor session lives on. That is what happens when you just clear a cookie. \\"Centralized single sign-out\\" ends the Casdoor session and tells every app of the user to end its own. Most users who click \\"Sign out\\" expect the second, especially on a shared computer.\\n\\nFor the third layer, Casdoor calls the logout URL of a Custom OAuth provider when the application signs users in through one and the provider has a logout URL set. It doesn\'t sign the user out of Google or GitHub.\\n\\n## RP-initiated logout with the end-session endpoint\\n\\n[OpenID Connect RP-Initiated Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-rpinitiated-1_0.html) defines how a relying party (your app) asks the provider to end its session. The provider publishes the endpoint as `end_session_endpoint` in its discovery document, and the request carries these parameters:\\n\\n- `id_token_hint`: the ID token the provider issued to your app. The spec marks it RECOMMENDED. Casdoor uses it to find and expire that exact token.\\n- `post_logout_redirect_uri`: where the browser goes afterwards. The spec requires it to be registered in advance. Casdoor checks it against the application\'s **Redirect URLs** and refuses to redirect to anything else.\\n- `state`: an opaque value that Casdoor appends to the redirect, so your app can tell its own logout round trip from a forged one.\\n- `client_id`: identifies the app when you can\'t send an ID token.\\n\\nThe spec also defines `logout_hint` and `ui_locales`. Casdoor doesn\'t read them.\\n\\nCasdoor\'s discovery document at `/.well-known/openid-configuration` announces `end_session_endpoint` as `https://<your-casdoor>/api/logout`, so OIDC libraries that support RP-initiated logout pick it up without configuration. The endpoint accepts `GET` and `POST`, as the spec requires.\\n\\nWhat Casdoor does with the request, briefly (the [RP-initiated logout reference](/docs/session/rp-initiated-logout) has every branch):\\n\\n- With `id_token_hint`, Casdoor looks up the token, sends back-channel logout tokens to the user\'s applications, expires the token (its refresh token stops working too), clears the browser\'s Casdoor session and redirects to `post_logout_redirect_uri` with `state`.\\n- Without `id_token_hint`, Casdoor signs out the current browser session and expires the tokens issued in it. If the browser has no session left, it still redirects when it knows the app, from the session or from `client_id`.\\n- Without `post_logout_redirect_uri`, the endpoint returns a JSON response instead of a redirect.\\n\\n### Example: sign-out button in a browser app\\n\\nA single-page app that signed in with the authorization code flow and PKCE keeps the ID token after the token exchange. The sign-out handler clears local state first, then hands the browser to Casdoor:\\n\\n```js\\nconst ISSUER = \\"https://door.example.com\\";\\nconst CLIENT_ID = \\"your-client-id\\";\\n\\nasync function signOut() {\\n  const idToken = sessionStorage.getItem(\\"id_token\\");\\n\\n  const discovery = await fetch(`${ISSUER}/.well-known/openid-configuration`)\\n    .then((r) => r.json());\\n\\n  // 1. Forget the user locally, whatever happens next\\n  sessionStorage.clear();\\n\\n  // 2. Remember a state value to check when Casdoor sends the browser back\\n  const state = crypto.randomUUID();\\n  sessionStorage.setItem(\\"logout_state\\", state);\\n\\n  // 3. End the Casdoor session\\n  const url = new URL(discovery.end_session_endpoint);\\n  if (idToken) {\\n    url.searchParams.set(\\"id_token_hint\\", idToken);\\n  } else {\\n    url.searchParams.set(\\"client_id\\", CLIENT_ID);\\n  }\\n  url.searchParams.set(\\"post_logout_redirect_uri\\", \\"https://app.example.com/signed-out\\");\\n  url.searchParams.set(\\"state\\", state);\\n  window.location.assign(url.toString());\\n}\\n\\n// On https://app.example.com/signed-out\\nconst returned = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get(\\"state\\");\\nif (returned !== sessionStorage.getItem(\\"logout_state\\")) {\\n  console.warn(\\"Unexpected logout redirect\\");\\n}\\nsessionStorage.removeItem(\\"logout_state\\");\\n```\\n\\nAdd `https://app.example.com/signed-out` to the application\'s **Redirect URLs** in Casdoor, or Casdoor returns an error instead of redirecting. A server-rendered app does the same from its logout route: destroy the server session, then answer with a 302 to the end-session URL. For Spring Security, see the [Spring Boot SSO post](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc).\\n\\n## Back-channel logout: telling the other apps\\n\\nEnding the Casdoor session stops new silent sign-ins, but app B still has its own session cookie from an hour ago. Something has to tell app B. [OpenID Connect Back-Channel Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-backchannel-1_0.html) does this server to server: the provider sends a `POST` with a form parameter `logout_token`, a signed JWT that names the user, to each app\'s registered back-channel URI.\\n\\nIn Casdoor, set **Backchannel logout URL** on the application\'s edit page. When a user signs out through `/api/logout`, Casdoor sends a logout token to every application that holds an active token for that user and has this URL set. The token is signed with the application\'s certificate, the same key as its ID tokens, and contains:\\n\\n- `iss` and `aud`: the same issuer and client ID as the app\'s ID tokens\\n- `sub`: the user\'s ID, the same value as `sub` in the ID token\\n- `iat` and `jti`\\n- `events` with the member `http://schemas.openid.net/event/backchannel-logout`\\n- `sid` only for some logouts, such as [SSO logout](/docs/session/single-sign-out), and not for `/api/logout`\\n\\nCasdoor\'s ID tokens don\'t carry a `sid` claim, so key your local sessions by `sub` and end all of them for that user. Casdoor\'s logout token also has no `exp` claim, which the final spec lists as required, so check freshness with `iat` and don\'t make `exp` mandatory in your validator. Casdoor sends each token once, with a 30-second timeout, and doesn\'t retry. For applications outside the `built-in` organization, it refuses to call loopback and link-local addresses, so `http://localhost` won\'t receive logout tokens during development.\\n\\n### Example: a back-channel logout receiver\\n\\nThis Express handler follows the validation steps of the spec, using the [`jose`](https://github.com/panva/jose) library:\\n\\n```js\\nimport express from \\"express\\";\\nimport { createRemoteJWKSet, jwtVerify } from \\"jose\\";\\n\\n// Take both values from the discovery document of your Casdoor\\nconst ISSUER = \\"https://door.example.com\\";\\nconst JWKS = createRemoteJWKSet(new URL(`${ISSUER}/.well-known/jwks`));\\nconst CLIENT_ID = process.env.CASDOOR_CLIENT_ID;\\nconst EVENT = \\"http://schemas.openid.net/event/backchannel-logout\\";\\n\\nconst seenJti = new Map();\\nconst app = express();\\n\\napp.post(\\n  \\"/oidc/backchannel-logout\\",\\n  express.urlencoded({ extended: false }),\\n  async (req, res) => {\\n    res.set(\\"Cache-Control\\", \\"no-store\\");\\n    try {\\n      const { payload } = await jwtVerify(req.body.logout_token, JWKS, {\\n        issuer: ISSUER,\\n        audience: CLIENT_ID,\\n        maxTokenAge: \\"2m\\",\\n      });\\n\\n      if (!payload.events || typeof payload.events[EVENT] !== \\"object\\") {\\n        throw new Error(\\"not a logout token\\");\\n      }\\n      if (!payload.sub && !payload.sid) {\\n        throw new Error(\\"no sub or sid\\");\\n      }\\n      if (\\"nonce\\" in payload) {\\n        throw new Error(\\"nonce is not allowed\\");\\n      }\\n      if (seenJti.has(payload.jti)) {\\n        throw new Error(\\"replayed token\\");\\n      }\\n      seenJti.set(payload.jti, Date.now());\\n\\n      // Your own helper: delete every session row that belongs to this user\\n      await destroySessionsForUser(payload.sub);\\n      res.sendStatus(200);\\n    } catch (err) {\\n      res.status(400).json({ error: \\"invalid_request\\", error_description: err.message });\\n    }\\n  }\\n);\\n```\\n\\n`jwtVerify` checks the signature, `iss`, `aud`, and with `maxTokenAge` the `iat` claim. To find sessions by user, store `sub` from the ID token in each session record at sign-in. Prune `seenJti` periodically, or keep it in Redis.\\n\\n## Revoking refresh tokens\\n\\nA refresh token outlives the session that created it. If your app keeps one, a logout that never touches it leaves a credential that can mint new access tokens for days.\\n\\nThe end-session call with `id_token_hint` already expires that token, refresh token included. Revoke explicitly when the logout doesn\'t go through the browser, for example in a CLI, a desktop or mobile app, or when the user leaves only your app and keeps the Casdoor session. Casdoor implements [RFC 7009](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7009) at the `revocation_endpoint` of its discovery document:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -X POST https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/revoke \\\\\\n  -u \\"$CLIENT_ID:$CLIENT_SECRET\\" \\\\\\n  -d token=\\"$REFRESH_TOKEN\\" \\\\\\n  -d token_type_hint=refresh_token\\n```\\n\\nA public client that used PKCE can send `client_id` in the body without a secret. Revoking a refresh token also expires the access tokens issued with it. After that, the refresh endpoint answers `invalid_grant` and introspection returns `\\"active\\": false`; [When Casdoor revokes tokens](/docs/token/overview#when-casdoor-revokes-tokens) lists every event that does this.\\n\\nOne limit applies to every provider that issues JWT access tokens: an API that only checks the signature and `exp` keeps accepting a revoked access token until it expires. Keep access tokens short-lived, or have the API call [token introspection](/docs/how-to-connect/oauth#how-to-verify-access-token) when it must notice a logout at once.\\n\\n## Single sign-out across several apps\\n\\nWith the pieces above, single sign-out works like this when the user clicks \\"Sign out\\" in app A:\\n\\n1. App A clears its session and redirects to `/api/logout` with `id_token_hint`.\\n2. Casdoor sends logout tokens to the back-channel URLs of the user\'s other apps, expires the token, and ends the browser\'s Casdoor session.\\n3. Apps B and C receive the logout token and delete their sessions for that `sub`.\\n4. The browser lands on app A\'s signed-out page. Opening app B now shows the Casdoor sign-in page.\\n\\nAn app without a back-channel URL stays signed in until its own session or access token expires. If you can\'t add a receiver to an app, keep its sessions short or make it validate the token on each request.\\n\\nFor \\"sign me out everywhere\\" after a lost laptop, use Casdoor\'s [SSO logout endpoint](/docs/session/single-sign-out) with `logoutAll=true`: it expires every token and deletes every session of the user, and also sends signed notifications through notification providers to apps that don\'t speak OIDC. Administrators can end single sessions on the [Sessions page](/docs/session/management). For SAML apps, see [SAML single logout](/docs/how-to-connect/saml/overview#single-logout). Background reading: [single logout](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/single-logout) and [SAML single logout](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/saml-single-logout) on www.casdoor.com.\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- [OpenID Connect RP-Initiated Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-rpinitiated-1_0.html)\\n- [OpenID Connect Back-Channel Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-backchannel-1_0.html)\\n- [OpenID Connect Front-Channel Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-frontchannel-1_0.html)\\n- [RFC 7009: OAuth 2.0 Token Revocation](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7009)\\n- [Auth0: Logout](https://auth0.com/docs/authenticate/login/logout)\\n- [Casdoor: RP-initiated logout](/docs/session/rp-initiated-logout)\\n- [Casdoor: Sign users out of all applications](/docs/session/single-sign-out)\\n- [Casdoor: Connect a standard OIDC client](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client)"},{"id":"radius-mfa-vpn-wifi","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/radius-mfa-vpn-wifi","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-radius-mfa-vpn-wifi.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-radius-mfa-vpn-wifi.mdx","title":"RADIUS Multi-Factor Authentication for VPN and Wi-Fi: TOTP 2FA with the Casdoor RADIUS Server","description":"Add TOTP 2FA to VPN logins over RADIUS with Casdoor: why only PAP works, how Access-Challenge asks for the code, which VPN clients support it, and Wi-Fi limits.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"RADIUS","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/radius"},{"inline":true,"label":"MFA","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/mfa"},{"inline":true,"label":"2FA","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/2-fa"},{"inline":true,"label":"TOTP","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/totp"},{"inline":true,"label":"VPN","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/vpn"},{"inline":true,"label":"Wi-Fi","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/wi-fi"},{"inline":true,"label":"802.1X","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/802-1-x"}],"readingTime":10.04,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"radius-mfa-vpn-wifi","title":"RADIUS Multi-Factor Authentication for VPN and Wi-Fi: TOTP 2FA with the Casdoor RADIUS Server","description":"Add TOTP 2FA to VPN logins over RADIUS with Casdoor: why only PAP works, how Access-Challenge asks for the code, which VPN clients support it, and Wi-Fi limits.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","RADIUS","MFA","2FA","TOTP","VPN","Wi-Fi","802.1X"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"RP-Initiated Logout and SSO Logout in OpenID Connect: How Casdoor Signs Users Out","permalink":"/de/blog/oidc-rp-initiated-logout"},"nextItem":{"title":"Spring Boot SSO mit OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server und Spring Cloud Gateway mit Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc"}},"content":"To put multi-factor authentication in front of a VPN with RADIUS, the VPN gateway sends the username and password to the RADIUS server with PAP, the server checks the password and answers with an `Access-Challenge`, the client shows a prompt, and the user types the 2FA code from an authenticator app into a second request. Casdoor ships a RADIUS server that does exactly this for users who have TOTP set up, so a gateway that can show an `Access-Challenge` prompt (FortiGate SSL VPN is one whose vendor documents it) gets a password plus a 2FA code without any extra software. Wi-Fi with WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise is different: 802.1X runs over EAP, which has no place for a typed code, so Casdoor has a switch to skip the code for those clients.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nIf you want the background on RADIUS itself and how it compares with LDAP for network devices, read [RADIUS vs LDAP](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/radius-vs-ldap) first. This post is about the MFA part: what goes over the wire, which clients can carry a second factor, and how to turn it on in Casdoor.\\n\\n## Which clients can do RADIUS MFA with Casdoor\\n\\nThe table lists what each vendor documents. \\"Challenge support\\" means the client shows the `Access-Challenge` prompt to the user and sends the answer back. Where the vendor page we checked doesn\'t say, the table says so instead of guessing.\\n\\n| Client | RADIUS auth method to use with Casdoor | Access-Challenge support | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| FortiGate SSL VPN | PAP (set the method to PAP, or leave Default, which tries PAP first) | Yes, per Fortinet | Fortinet says SSL VPN forwards the challenge to the client and waits for the answer |\\n| FortiGate IPsec VPN (including FortiClient) | PAP | Not forwarded by default | Fortinet\'s fix requires MS-CHAP on the RADIUS server, which Casdoor doesn\'t speak; use SSL VPN or skip MFA |\\n| Cisco ASA (AnyConnect / Secure Client) | PAP, the ASA default for all connection types | Not stated in the ASA RADIUS configuration guide | Don\'t enable `password-management` on the tunnel group: it switches the ASA to MS-CHAPv2 |\\n| OpenVPN Access Server | PAP (it also offers CHAP and MS-CHAPv2) | Not stated on the RADIUS settings page | Accounting is a separate toggle, default port 1813 |\\n| pfSense (RADIUS auth server, used by its OpenVPN) | PAP (also MD5-CHAP, MS-CHAPv1, MS-CHAPv2) | Not stated in the Netgate docs | Netgate\'s OTP examples put the code inside the password field, which Casdoor doesn\'t accept |\\n| WireGuard | None | Not applicable | Peers authenticate with public keys only, so there is no login for RADIUS to check |\\n| Wi-Fi, WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise (802.1X) | EAP (PEAP, EAP-TTLS and so on) | No typed-code prompt | Casdoor doesn\'t implement EAP; see the Wi-Fi section |\\n\\nWhere a cell says \\"not stated\\", test with the real VPN client before you roll it out, and check that the prompt appears and the code goes through.\\n\\n## Why only PAP can carry the password Casdoor checks\\n\\nRADIUS has several ways to send a password, and they decide what the server is able to verify.\\n\\nWith **PAP**, the client puts the password into the `User-Password` attribute, hidden with an MD5 hash of the shared secret and the request authenticator (RFC 2865, section 5.2). The server reverses that with the same secret, gets the cleartext password, and can check it against a stored hash or an LDAP bind. Casdoor reads `User-Password` and runs the same password check as its sign-in page.\\n\\nWith **CHAP**, the client never sends the password. It sends a hash of the password and a random challenge. RFC 2865 says plainly that CHAP needs the user\'s password in cleartext on the server to recompute that hash. **MS-CHAPv2** has the same shape with an NT hash. An identity provider that stores passwords as one-way hashes (Casdoor\'s built-in organization uses bcrypt) can\'t verify either one. The Casdoor RADIUS server only reads `User-Password`, so a CHAP or MS-CHAPv2 request reaches it with no password and is rejected.\\n\\nBecause the PAP hiding is only MD5 with the shared secret, use a long random secret, keep RADIUS on a private network or a VPN between the gateway and Casdoor, and turn on the `Message-Authenticator` attribute on the client. Casdoor verifies `Message-Authenticator` when a request carries it and signs its reply in that case, which is the mitigation for Blast-RADIUS (CVE-2024-3596). The details are in the [RADIUS server docs](/docs/radius/overview#message-authenticator).\\n\\n## Access-Challenge vs appending the code to the password\\n\\nThere are two common ways to get a 2FA code through RADIUS.\\n\\n1. **Access-Challenge.** The server checks the password, then replies with `Access-Challenge`, a `State` attribute and a `Reply-Message`. The client shows the message, the user types the code, and the client sends a new `Access-Request` with the code in `User-Password` and the same `State`. RFC 2865 section 4.4 also says what happens on a client without challenge support: it MUST treat the `Access-Challenge` as an `Access-Reject`.\\n2. **Code appended to the password.** The user types `password123456` into the one password field, and the server splits off the last six digits. This works with any client, which is why products built for older gateways offer it.\\n\\nCasdoor implements only the first. Here is the flow in its RADIUS server (`radius/server.go`):\\n\\n- The first `Access-Request` must carry the correct password. A user with MFA turned on gets `Access-Challenge`, a random `State`, and the `Reply-Message` `please enter OTP`.\\n- The `State` is valid for 120 seconds and can be used once. It is tied to the organization and username of the first request.\\n- The second `Access-Request` must carry that `State` and the six-digit TOTP code from the user\'s authenticator app in `User-Password`. Casdoor checks the code against the user\'s TOTP secret and answers `Access-Accept` or `Access-Reject`.\\n- Wrong codes count toward the failed sign-in limit of the user\'s application (5 attempts and a 15-minute freeze by default), separately from wrong passwords.\\n\\nTwo consequences follow. A client that can only do \\"password plus code in one field\\" will fail, because Casdoor checks the whole string as the password. And only TOTP works over RADIUS: SMS and email codes need Casdoor to send a message after the password step, which the RADIUS server doesn\'t do. A user whose only MFA method is SMS or email gets the challenge and then a reject, so have RADIUS users set up an authenticator app.\\n\\n## Wi-Fi: 802.1X and WPA-Enterprise\\n\\nOn Wi-Fi with WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3-Enterprise, the access point doesn\'t collect a password itself. It relays an EAP conversation between the device (the supplicant) and the RADIUS server, and RADIUS carries the EAP packets in `EAP-Message` attributes. In that setup `Access-Challenge` is already in use: every round trip of the EAP exchange is an `Access-Request` and `Access-Challenge` pair (RFC 3579). RFC 3579 also says a message carrying `EAP-Message` must not include `Reply-Message`, so there is no field for \\"please enter OTP\\" and no dialog on the phone or laptop to type a code into.\\n\\nTwo practical points for Casdoor:\\n\\n- Casdoor\'s RADIUS server doesn\'t implement EAP. An access point can\'t talk to it directly for WPA-Enterprise. You need an EAP server in front that terminates the TLS tunnel and forwards a plain PAP request. FreeRADIUS can do this with EAP-TTLS and PAP as the inner method, proxying the inner request to another RADIUS server, and the devices must be set to TTLS with PAP inside. PEAP with MS-CHAPv2, the default on many devices, won\'t work against Casdoor for the reason in the PAP section.\\n- For those users, turn on **Skip MFA for RADIUS** on the organization. Casdoor then accepts users who have MFA after the password check alone. MFA on the web sign-in page is not affected.\\n\\n## Ports, shared secret and accounting\\n\\nRFC 2865 assigns UDP port 1812 to RADIUS authentication, and RFC 2866 assigns 1813 to accounting. Casdoor listens on one UDP port, `radiusServerPort`, and handles both `Access-Request` and `Accounting-Request` there. So point the gateway\'s accounting at the same port as authentication (1812 by default), not at 1813, or accounting packets will go nowhere. If Casdoor runs in a container, publish the port as UDP, for example `-p 1812:1812/udp`.\\n\\nThe shared secret is one value for all clients, `radiusSecret`. Every gateway that talks to Casdoor uses it.\\n\\nFor accounting, Casdoor records `Start`, `Interim-Update` and `Stop` messages in its database (table `radius_accounting`), with the session ID, session time, NAS address, framed IP and byte and packet counters. It only does that for organizations with RADIUS turned on.\\n\\n## Casdoor konfigurieren {#configure-casdoor}\\n\\n**1. Set the RADIUS options.** In `conf/app.conf`, or as environment variables with the same names:\\n\\n```ini\\nradiusServerPort = 1812\\nradiusDefaultOrganization = \\"my-company\\"\\nradiusSecret = \\"a-long-random-string-shared-with-the-gateway\\"\\n```\\n\\n`radiusDefaultOrganization` is the organization Casdoor uses when a request has no `Class` attribute. If your gateway can\'t add a `Class` attribute to its requests, this setting decides which organization\'s users sign in over RADIUS. All keys are listed in the [configuration reference](/docs/basic/configuration).\\n\\n**2. Turn on RADIUS for the organization.** On the organization edit page, turn on **Enable RADIUS**. It is off by default and only global administrators can change it. While it is off, Casdoor rejects every `Access-Request` for that organization and ignores its accounting.\\n\\n**3. Decide on MFA for RADIUS.** Leave **Skip MFA for RADIUS** off for VPN gateways that support `Access-Challenge`. Turn it on for an organization whose RADIUS clients can\'t answer a challenge, such as WPA-Enterprise Wi-Fi.\\n\\n**4. Get users onto TOTP.** Users set up an authenticator app from their profile page, as described in [multi-factor authentication](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication). To make it mandatory, add the TOTP item with the **Required** rule under the organization\'s [MFA items](/docs/organization/mfa-items). Note the order: a user who must still set up required MFA, or must change the password first, is rejected over RADIUS until they finish that on the Casdoor sign-in page.\\n\\n**5. Test from the command line.** FreeRADIUS\'s `radclient` sends PAP requests and prints the reply:\\n\\n```bash\\necho \'User-Name = \\"alice\\", User-Password = \\"her-password\\"\' \\\\\\n  | radclient -x casdoor.example.com:1812 auth a-long-random-string-shared-with-the-gateway\\n```\\n\\nFor a user with TOTP you should see `Access-Challenge` with a `State` and the `Reply-Message`. Send the code back with that `State` within 120 seconds:\\n\\n```bash\\necho \'User-Name = \\"alice\\", User-Password = \\"123456\\", State = 0x...\' \\\\\\n  | radclient -x casdoor.example.com:1812 auth a-long-random-string-shared-with-the-gateway\\n```\\n\\nPaste the `State` value from the first reply in place of `0x...`. An `Access-Accept` means the setup is right, and the gateway is next.\\n\\nIf you also want Casdoor to use an outside RADIUS server as a second factor for its own web sign-in, that is the opposite direction and is covered by the [RADIUS MFA provider](/docs/provider/mfa/radius).\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- [RFC 2865: Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS)](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2865)\\n- [RFC 2866: RADIUS Accounting](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2866)\\n- [RFC 3579: RADIUS Support for EAP](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3579)\\n- [Fortinet: Difference in RADIUS Access-Challenge handling between SSL VPN and IPsec VPN](https://community.fortinet.com/t5/FortiGate/Technical-Tip-Difference-in-RADIUS-Access-Challenge-for/ta-p/406069)\\n- [FortiOS 7.6.5: Configuring a RADIUS server](https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/7.6.5/administration-guide/759080/configuring-a-radius-server)\\n- [Cisco ASA 9.22: RADIUS servers for AAA](https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/security/asa/asa922/configuration/general/asa-922-general-config/aaa-radius.html)\\n- [OpenVPN Access Server: Authentication](https://openvpn.net/as-docs/authentication-78640/)\\n- [pfSense: RADIUS authentication servers](https://docs.netgate.com/pfsense/en/latest/usermanager/radius.html)\\n- [WireGuard](https://www.wireguard.com/)\\n- [FreeRADIUS `inner-tunnel` virtual server](https://github.com/FreeRADIUS/freeradius-server/blob/v3.2.x/raddb/sites-available/inner-tunnel)\\n- [Casdoor RADIUS server](/docs/radius/overview)"},{"id":"spring-boot-sso-oidc","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-spring-boot-sso-oidc.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-spring-boot-sso-oidc.mdx","title":"Spring Boot SSO mit OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server und Spring Cloud Gateway mit Casdoor","description":"F\xfcgen Sie Single Sign-On zu Spring Boot mit OpenID Connect und Casdoor hinzu: oauth2Login Web-Apps, JWT Resource Server und Spring Cloud Gateway TokenRelay, jeweils mit YAML.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Boot","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/spring-boot"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Security","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/spring-security"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Cloud Gateway","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/spring-cloud-gateway"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth 2.0","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"JWT","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/jwt"}],"readingTime":12.28,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"spring-boot-sso-oidc","title":"Spring Boot SSO mit OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server und Spring Cloud Gateway mit Casdoor","description":"F\xfcgen Sie Single Sign-On zu Spring Boot mit OpenID Connect und Casdoor hinzu: oauth2Login Web-Apps, JWT Resource Server und Spring Cloud Gateway TokenRelay, jeweils mit YAML.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Spring Boot","Spring Security","Spring Cloud Gateway","OIDC","OAuth 2.0","SSO","JWT"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"RADIUS Multi-Factor Authentication for VPN and Wi-Fi: TOTP 2FA with the Casdoor RADIUS Server","permalink":"/de/blog/radius-mfa-vpn-wifi"},"nextItem":{"title":"DolphinScheduler, IoTDB and Other Apache Data Platforms: SSO with Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/sso-for-apache-data-platforms"}},"content":"Um Single Sign-On zu Spring Boot hinzuzuf\xfcgen, registrieren Sie jede App als Anwendung bei einem OpenID Connect-Anbieter wie Casdoor, verweisen Sie Spring Security auf die Issuer-URL des Anbieters und w\xe4hlen Sie das Spring-Modul aus, das zur App passt: `oauth2Login()` f\xfcr eine serverseitig gerenderte Web-App, `oauth2ResourceServer().jwt()` f\xfcr eine REST-API, die Bearer-Tokens empf\xe4ngt, und Spring Cloud Gateway mit dem `TokenRelay`-Filter, wenn ein Gateway Benutzer f\xfcr einen Satz von Microservices authentifiziert. Benutzer erhalten SSO, weil jede App sie an denselben Casdoor sendet, der sich die Sitzung merkt: Die zweite App leitet um, Casdoor kennt den Benutzer bereits, und er kehrt direkt ohne Login-Formular zur\xfcck. Dieser Beitrag richtet alle drei Varianten gegen einen Casdoor ein, jeweils mit einer funktionierenden `application.yml` und einer angepassten `SecurityFilterChain`.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Die drei Varianten im \xdcberblick {#the-three-shapes-at-a-glance}\\n\\n| Variante | Spring-Abh\xe4ngigkeit | Wer h\xe4lt die Anmeldung | Verwendete Casdoor-Endpunkte | Filterkette |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Web-App | `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` | Die App (HTTP-Sitzung) | discovery, authorize, token, userinfo, JWKS, logout | `SecurityFilterChain` mit `oauth2Login()` |\\n| REST API | `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server` | Niemand, jeder\u8BF7\u6C42 tr\xe4gt ein Bearer-Token | JWKS (und discovery, wenn Sie `issuer-uri` verwenden) | `SecurityFilterChain` mit `oauth2ResourceServer()` |\\n| Gateway + Microservices | `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux` + `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` | Das Gateway (WebFlux-Sitzung) | Wie die Web-App; dahinterliegende Services nutzen JWKS | `SecurityWebFilterChain` mit `oauth2Login()`, `TokenRelay` auf Routen |\\n\\nVersionsannahmen: Spring Boot 3.5 mit Spring Security 6.5, Java 17 und Spring Cloud 2025.0 (Spring Cloud Gateway 4.3). In Spring Boot 4 hei\xdfen die beiden Security-Starter `spring-boot-starter-security-oauth2-client` und `spring-boot-starter-security-oauth2-resource-server`. Gateway 4.3 und 5.0 verwenden beide das unten verwendete Pr\xe4fix `spring.cloud.gateway.server.webflux.*`; das \xe4ltere Pr\xe4fix `spring.cloud.gateway.*` und das Artefakt `spring-cloud-starter-gateway` sind seit Spring Cloud 2025.0 veraltet.\\n\\n## Was Casdoor Spring bietet {#what-casdoor-gives-spring}\\n\\nErstellen Sie in der Casdoor-Admin-Konsole eine Anwendung (oder nutzen Sie eine vorhandene) und kopieren Sie deren **Client ID** und **Client secret**. F\xfcgen Sie jeden Callback, den die Spring-Apps verwenden werden, zu **Redirect URLs** hinzu. Der Standardwert von Spring Security ist `{baseUrl}/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}`, also lautet die URL bei der Registrierungs-ID `casdoor` und einer App auf Port 8080 `http://localhost:8080/login/oauth2/code/casdoor`. Wenn Sie OIDC-Logout verwenden, f\xfcgen Sie auch die Seite hinzu, auf der die Benutzer danach landen, z. B. `http://localhost:8080/`; Casdoor lehnt eine `post_logout_redirect_uri` ab, die nicht in der Liste steht.\\n\\nCasdoor ver\xf6ffentlicht ein standardm\xe4\xdfiges Discovery-Dokument unter `/.well-known/openid-configuration`, sodass Spring nur den Issuer ben\xf6tigt. Dies sind die beworbenen Endpunkte, entnommen aus dem Casdoor-Quellcode:\\n\\n| Metadatenfeld | Casdoor-Pfad |\\n|---|---|\\n| `issuer` | Der Backend-Origin, z. B. `https://door.example.com` (kein abschlie\xdfender Schr\xe4gstrich) |\\n| `authorization_endpoint` | `/login/oauth/authorize` |\\n| `token_endpoint` | `/api/login/oauth/access_token` |\\n| `userinfo_endpoint` | `/api/userinfo` |\\n| `jwks_uri` | `/.well-known/jwks` |\\n| `end_session_endpoint` | `/api/logout` |\\n\\nUnterst\xfctzte Scopes sind `openid`, `email`, `profile`, `address`, `phone` und `offline_access`, sowie alle benutzerdefinierten Scopes, die f\xfcr die Anwendung definiert sind. Tokens werden standardm\xe4\xdfig mit RS256 signiert und enthalten den Zertifikatsnamen als `kid`. Jede Anwendung hat auch ihr eigenes Discovery-Dokument unter `/.well-known/<application>/openid-configuration`; siehe [Connect a standard OIDC client](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) f\xfcr Details und die vollst\xe4ndige Liste der [OAuth 2.0 endpoints](/docs/how-to-connect/oauth).\\n\\nZwei Dinge in Casdoors Tokens sind f\xfcr Spring wichtig:\\n\\n- **Der Issuer muss exakt \xfcbereinstimmen.** Spring vergleicht den `issuer` im Discovery-Dokument mit der konfigurierten `issuer-uri` und sp\xe4ter mit dem `iss`-Claim jedes Tokens. Casdoor leitet den Issuer aus der `origin`-Einstellung in `app.conf` ab oder vom Host-Header, wenn `origin` leer ist. Wenn Ihre Spring-Apps Casdoor \xfcber einen internen Hostnamen erreichen (z. B. `http://casdoor:8000` innerhalb von Docker), w\xe4hrend Browser einen \xf6ffentlichen verwenden, setzen Sie `origin` auf die \xf6ffentliche URL, damit jedes Token denselben `iss` erh\xe4lt. Siehe [Configuration](/docs/basic/configuration).\\n- **Rollen kommen in zwei Formen vor.** `/api/userinfo` gibt `roles` als Liste von Rollennamen zur\xfcck, wenn der Scope `profile` gew\xe4hrt wurde. Das Access-Token im Standard-`JWT` [token format](/docs/token/overview) enth\xe4lt `roles` als Liste von Rollenobjekten, jeweils mit einem Feld `name`. Der Code unten behandelt beide F\xe4lle. Casdoor Access-Tokens enthalten au\xdferdem `scope` als durch Leerzeichen getrennte Zeichenkette, die Spring standardm\xe4\xdfig in `SCOPE_...` Authorities umwandelt.\\n\\nWenn Sie Hintergrundwissen zum Protokoll ben\xf6tigen, lesen Sie [Was ist OpenID Connect](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-openid-connect) und [ID-Token vs. Access-Token](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/id-token-vs-access-token).\\n\\n## 1. Spring Boot Web-App mit oauth2Login() {#1-spring-boot-web-app-with-oauth2login}\\n\\nF\xfcgen Sie `spring-boot-starter-web`, `spring-boot-starter-security` und `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` hinzu. Konfigurieren Sie dann den Anbieter \xfcber den Issuer:\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 8080\\n\\nspring:\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      client:\\n        registration:\\n          casdoor:\\n            client-id: <client-id>\\n            client-secret: <client-secret>\\n            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code\\n            scope: openid,profile,email\\n        provider:\\n          casdoor:\\n            issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n            user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n```\\n\\nBeim Start holt sich Spring Boot `https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration` und f\xfcllt die URIs f\xfcr Autorisierung, Token, Userinfo und JWKS auf. Spring validiert anschlie\xdfend die Signatur, den Issuer, die Audience und den Nonce des ID-Tokens, ruft `/api/userinfo` auf, da `profile` und `email` im Scope enthalten sind, und f\xfchrt die Claims zusammen. `preferred_username` ist der Casdoor-Benutzername; `name` in der Userinfo ist der Anzeigename, und das Standard-`sub` ist die UUID des Benutzers.\\n\\nOhne eigene Konfiguration erfordert Spring Boot eine Anmeldung f\xfcr jede URL. Die untenstehende angepasste `SecurityFilterChain` \xf6ffnet die Startseite, bildet Casdoor-Rollen auf `ROLE_`-Berechtigungen ab und meldet Benutzer sowohl von Casdoor als auch von der App ab:\\n\\n```java title=\\"SecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebSecurity\\npublic class SecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http,\\n            ClientRegistrationRepository clientRegistrations) throws Exception {\\n        OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler oidcLogout =\\n                new OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler(clientRegistrations);\\n        oidcLogout.setPostLogoutRedirectUri(\\"{baseUrl}/\\");\\n\\n        http\\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/\\", \\"/public/**\\", \\"/error\\").permitAll()\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/admin/**\\").hasRole(\\"admin\\")\\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2Login(login -> login\\n                .userInfoEndpoint(userInfo -> userInfo\\n                    .userAuthoritiesMapper(casdoorRoles())))\\n            .logout(logout -> logout\\n                .logoutSuccessHandler(oidcLogout));\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n\\n    private GrantedAuthoritiesMapper casdoorRoles() {\\n        return authorities -> {\\n            Set<GrantedAuthority> mapped = new HashSet<>(authorities);\\n            for (GrantedAuthority authority : authorities) {\\n                if (authority instanceof OidcUserAuthority oidc && oidc.getUserInfo() != null) {\\n                    List<String> roles = oidc.getUserInfo().getClaimAsStringList(\\"roles\\");\\n                    if (roles != null) {\\n                        roles.forEach(role -> mapped.add(new SimpleGrantedAuthority(\\"ROLE_\\" + role)));\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n            return mapped;\\n        };\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`hasRole(\\"admin\\")` entspricht nun Benutzern, die die Casdoor-Rolle `admin` haben. `OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler` liest `end_session_endpoint` aus dem Discovery-Dokument und leitet zu Casdoors `/api/logout` weiter mit `id_token_hint` und `post_logout_redirect_uri`, sodass auch die Casdoor-Sitzung beendet wird. Ohne diesen Handler l\xf6scht `/logout` nur die Spring-Sitzung, und die n\xe4chste Anmeldung erfolgt stillschweigend.\\n\\nIn einem Controller ist der angemeldete Benutzer ein `OidcUser`:\\n\\n```java\\n@GetMapping(\\"/me\\")\\nMap<String, Object> me(@AuthenticationPrincipal OidcUser user) {\\n    return Map.of(\\"name\\", user.getName(), \\"email\\", user.getEmail(),\\n            \\"authorities\\", user.getAuthorities().toString());\\n}\\n```\\n\\nDie \xe4ltere Schritt-f\xfcr-Schritt-Seite f\xfcr diese Einrichtung finden Sie unter [Spring Security OAuth](/docs/integration/java/spring-security/spring-security-oauth).\\n\\n## 2. Spring Boot API als OAuth2 Resource Server {#2-spring-boot-api-as-an-oauth2-resource-server}\\n\\nEine REST-API sollte niemanden umleiten. Sie akzeptiert `Authorization: Bearer <access token>`, pr\xfcft die JWT-Signatur gegen Casdoors JWKS und \xfcberpr\xfcft die Claims. F\xfcgen Sie `spring-boot-starter-web`, `spring-boot-starter-security` und `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server` hinzu:\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 9000\\n\\nspring:\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      resourceserver:\\n        jwt:\\n          issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n          jwk-set-uri: https://door.example.com/.well-known/jwks\\n          audiences: <client-id>\\n```\\n\\nMit nur dieser Datei erstellt Spring Boot einen `JwtDecoder`, der die Signatur, das Ablaufdatum, `iss` und `aud` pr\xfcft. Wenn `jwk-set-uri` neben `issuer-uri` gesetzt wird, werden die Schl\xfcssel direkt abgerufen statt \xfcber Discovery, aber der Issuer wird weiterhin validiert. Die Audience ist die Client-ID der Casdoor-Anwendung, die das Token ausgestellt hat: Wenn eine Single-Page-App oder das Gateway aus Abschnitt 3 das Token erh\xe4lt, verwenden Sie die Client-ID dieser Anwendung.\\n\\nEs gibt eine casdoor-spezifische Pr\xfcfung, die es wert ist, hinzugef\xfcgt zu werden. Casdoor signiert ID-Tokens und Access-Tokens mit demselben Schl\xfcssel und weist beiden die Client-ID als Audience zu, sodass die Standard-Validatoren auch ein ID-Token akzeptieren w\xfcrden, das als Bearer-Token gesendet wird. Jedes Casdoor-Token enth\xe4lt einen `tokenType`-Claim (`access-token`, `id-token` oder `refresh-token`), was die Pr\xfcfung vereinfacht. Wenn Sie einen `JwtDecoder`-Bean definieren, ersetzt er den von Boot bereitgestellten, daher f\xfcgt der Bean die Pr\xfcfungen f\xfcr Issuer und Audience wieder hinzu:\\n\\n```java title=\\"ApiSecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebSecurity\\npublic class ApiSecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    JwtDecoder jwtDecoder(\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri}\\") String issuer,\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.jwk-set-uri}\\") String jwkSetUri,\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.audiences}\\") String clientId) {\\n        NimbusJwtDecoder decoder = NimbusJwtDecoder.withJwkSetUri(jwkSetUri).build();\\n        decoder.setJwtValidator(new DelegatingOAuth2TokenValidator<>(\\n                JwtValidators.createDefaultWithIssuer(issuer),\\n                new JwtClaimValidator<List<String>>(JwtClaimNames.AUD,\\n                        aud -> aud != null && aud.contains(clientId)),\\n                new JwtClaimValidator<String>(\\"tokenType\\", \\"access-token\\"::equals)));\\n        return decoder;\\n    }\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityFilterChain apiFilterChain(HttpSecurity http, JwtDecoder jwtDecoder) throws Exception {\\n        http\\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/actuator/health\\").permitAll()\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/api/admin/**\\").hasRole(\\"admin\\")\\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2ResourceServer(server -> server\\n                .jwt(jwt -> jwt\\n                    .decoder(jwtDecoder)\\n                    .jwtAuthenticationConverter(casdoorJwtConverter())))\\n            .sessionManagement(session -> session\\n                .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))\\n            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable());\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n\\n    private JwtAuthenticationConverter casdoorJwtConverter() {\\n        JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter scopes = new JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter();\\n        JwtAuthenticationConverter converter = new JwtAuthenticationConverter();\\n        converter.setPrincipalClaimName(\\"name\\");\\n        converter.setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter(jwt -> {\\n            Collection<GrantedAuthority> authorities = new ArrayList<>(scopes.convert(jwt));\\n            if (jwt.getClaims().get(\\"roles\\") instanceof Collection<?> roles) {\\n                for (Object role : roles) {\\n                    Object name = role instanceof Map<?, ?> map ? map.get(\\"name\\") : role;\\n                    if (name instanceof String roleName && !roleName.isEmpty()) {\\n                        authorities.add(new SimpleGrantedAuthority(\\"ROLE_\\" + roleName));\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n            return authorities;\\n        });\\n        return converter;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nDer Converter beh\xe4lt die `SCOPE_`-Berechtigungen bei, f\xfcgt `ROLE_`-Berechtigungen aus dem `roles`-Claim hinzu (unabh\xe4ngig davon, ob er Namen oder Objekte enth\xe4lt) und verwendet den `name`-Claim (den Casdoor-Benutzernamen) als Principal. Wenn die Anwendung das `JWT-Standard`-Tokenformat verwendet, verwenden Sie stattdessen `preferred_username` als Principal-Claim. Das Deaktivieren von CSRF und Sitzungen ist hier richtig, da die API niemals Browser-Cookies sieht; kopieren Sie diese beiden Zeilen nicht in die Web-App.\\n\\nUm dies ohne Browser zu testen, holen Sie sich ein Machine-Token mit dem Client Credentials Grant und rufen Sie die API auf:\\n\\n```shell\\nTOKEN=$(curl -s -u \'<client-id>:<client-secret>\' \\\\\\n  -d grant_type=client_credentials \\\\\\n  https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token | jq -r .access_token)\\n\\ncurl -H \\"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\\" http://localhost:9000/api/hello\\n```\\n\\nEin Client-Credentials-Token repr\xe4sentiert die Anwendung, nicht einen Benutzer, daher hat es keine Rollen. Weitere Informationen dazu, welche Claims gepr\xfcft werden sollten und warum, finden Sie unter [JWT-Validierung](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/jwt-validation) und [OAuth-Scopes](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/oauth-scopes).\\n\\n## 3. Spring Cloud Gateway mit TokenRelay {#3-spring-cloud-gateway-with-tokenrelay}\\n\\nBei Microservices ist ein \xfcbliches Layout, dass das Gateway die Anmeldung \xfcbernimmt und die Services als Resource Server fungieren. Der Browser kommuniziert nur mit dem Gateway und h\xe4lt ein Sitzungscookie; das Gateway speichert das Access-Token des Benutzers, und der `TokenRelay`-Filter f\xfcgt es jedem proxied Request als `Authorization: Bearer ...` hinzu. Die Services aus Abschnitt 2 validieren es wie \xfcblich.\\n\\nF\xfcgen Sie `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux` und `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` hinzu, zusammen mit dem Spring Cloud 2025.0 BOM:\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 8080\\n\\nspring:\\n  application:\\n    name: gateway\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      client:\\n        registration:\\n          casdoor:\\n            client-id: <client-id>\\n            client-secret: <client-secret>\\n            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code\\n            scope: openid,profile,email\\n        provider:\\n          casdoor:\\n            issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n            user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n  cloud:\\n    gateway:\\n      server:\\n        webflux:\\n          routes:\\n            - id: orders\\n              uri: http://localhost:9000\\n              predicates:\\n                - Path=/api/orders/**\\n              filters:\\n                - TokenRelay=\\n            - id: users\\n              uri: http://localhost:9001\\n              predicates:\\n                - Path=/api/users/**\\n              filters:\\n                - TokenRelay=\\n```\\n\\n`TokenRelay=` ohne Argument relayed das Token des angemeldeten Benutzers. Die `TokenRelay`-Filter-Fabrik wird nur erstellt, wenn `spring.security.oauth2.client.*` konfiguriert ist, und der Standard-Authorized-Client-Speicher ist im Speicher, daher ben\xf6tigt ein Gateway mit mehreren Replikas einen gemeinsamen Speicher oder Sticky Sessions. Die dahinterliegenden Services setzen `audiences` auf die Client-ID des Gateways.\\n\\nSpring Cloud Gateway l\xe4uft auf WebFlux, daher ist seine Sicherheitskonfiguration eine `SecurityWebFilterChain`, die aus `ServerHttpSecurity` erstellt wird, nicht die Servlet-`SecurityFilterChain`:\\n\\n```java title=\\"GatewaySecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebFluxSecurity\\npublic class GatewaySecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityWebFilterChain gatewayFilterChain(ServerHttpSecurity http,\\n            ReactiveClientRegistrationRepository clientRegistrations) {\\n        OidcClientInitiatedServerLogoutSuccessHandler oidcLogout =\\n                new OidcClientInitiatedServerLogoutSuccessHandler(clientRegistrations);\\n        oidcLogout.setPostLogoutRedirectUri(\\"{baseUrl}/\\");\\n\\n        http\\n            .authorizeExchange(exchanges -> exchanges\\n                .pathMatchers(\\"/\\", \\"/actuator/health\\").permitAll()\\n                .anyExchange().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2Login(Customizer.withDefaults())\\n            .logout(logout -> logout.logoutSuccessHandler(oidcLogout));\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nBehalten Sie Autorisierungsentscheidungen in den Services, wo die Rollen im Token enthalten sind; das Gateway stellt nur sicher, dass ein angemeldeter Benutzer vorhanden ist. Der CSRF-Schutz bleibt im Gateway aktiv, da er Cookie-basiert ist.\\n\\nDie vorhandenen Seiten [Spring Cloud Gateway](/docs/integration/java/spring-cloud-gateway) und [Spring Cloud](/docs/integration/java/spring-cloud) zeigen ein anderes Design, bei dem ein benutzerdefinierter `GlobalFilter` einen in der Gateway-Sitzung gespeicherten Casdoor-Benutzer pr\xfcft.\\n\\n## Alternative: der Casdoor Spring Boot Starter {#alternative-the-casdoor-spring-boot-starter}\\n\\nWenn Sie lieber kein YAML schreiben m\xf6chten, nimmt [casdoor-spring-boot-starter](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-spring-boot-starter) 2.x (Spring Boot 3 und 4, Java 17+) `casdoor.endpoint`, `casdoor.client-id`, `casdoor.client-secret`, `casdoor.organization-name` und `casdoor.application-name` entgegen und setzt die oben genannten `spring.security.oauth2.*`-Eigenschaften f\xfcr whichever Security Starter sich auf dem Classpath befindet. Es registriert den Client als `casdoor`, bildet Casdoor-Rollen auf `ROLE_`-Berechtigungen ab f\xfcr sowohl OAuth2 Login als auch den Servlet Resource Server und stellt die Dienste des [Casdoor Java SDK](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-java-sdk) als Beans zur Verf\xfcgung. Seine Eigenschaften haben die niedrigste Priorit\xe4t, sodass Sie sie weiterhin \xfcberschreiben k\xf6nnen. Siehe [Spring Boot](/docs/integration/java/spring-boot) f\xfcr die Einrichtung.\\n\\n## H\xe4ufige Fehler {#common-errors}\\n\\n- **\\"The Issuer ... provided in the configuration metadata did not match the requested issuer\\"**: Die `issuer-uri` unterscheidet sich von dem, was Casdoor meldet. Entfernen Sie einen abschlie\xdfenden Schr\xe4gstrich und setzen Sie `origin` in Casdoor, falls die Hostnamen unterschiedlich sind.\\n- **`redirect_uri` von Casdoor abgelehnt**: Der Callback ist nicht in den **Redirect URLs** der Anwendung enthalten. \xdcberpr\xfcfen Sie das Schema und den Port, insbesondere hinter einem Reverse Proxy.\\n- **\\"Missing attribute \'preferred_username\'\\"**: Der `profile`-Scope wurde nicht angefordert oder die Token-Felder der Anwendung lassen `Name` aus.\\n- **401 mit einem `invalid_token`-Fehler bei der API**: Das Token stammt von einer anderen Anwendung (falsche `aud`), es ist abgelaufen oder es handelt sich um ein ID-Token.\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- Casdoor Discovery-Dokument und Endpunkte: [`object/wellknown_oidc_discovery.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/wellknown_oidc_discovery.go)\\n- Casdoor-Token-Anspr\xfcche: [`object/token_jwt.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/token_jwt.go)\\n- [Spring Boot 3.5 Referenz: Spring Security, OAuth2 Client und Resource Server](https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/3.5/reference/web/spring-security.html)\\n- [Spring Security: OIDC client-initiated logout](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/login/logout.html)\\n- [Spring Security: OAuth 2.0 Resource Server JWT](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/resource-server/jwt.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud Gateway: TokenRelay GatewayFilter Factory](https://docs.spring.io/spring-cloud-gateway/reference/spring-cloud-gateway-server-webflux/gatewayfilter-factories/tokenrelay-factory.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud Gateway: Starter `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux`](https://docs.spring.io/spring-cloud-gateway/reference/spring-cloud-gateway-server-webflux/starter.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud 2025.0 Release Notes](https://github.com/spring-cloud/spring-cloud-release/wiki/Spring-Cloud-2025.0-Release-Notes)\\n- [casdoor-spring-boot-starter](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-spring-boot-starter)"},{"id":"sso-for-apache-data-platforms","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/sso-for-apache-data-platforms","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-sso-for-apache-data-platforms.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-sso-for-apache-data-platforms.mdx","title":"DolphinScheduler, IoTDB and Other Apache Data Platforms: SSO with Casdoor","description":"Which Apache data projects support Casdoor SSO, since which version and with which config keys: DolphinScheduler, IoTDB, ShenYu, Pulsar Manager, ElasticJob UI.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"DolphinScheduler","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/dolphin-scheduler"},{"inline":true,"label":"IoTDB","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/io-tdb"},{"inline":true,"label":"ShenYu","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/shen-yu"},{"inline":true,"label":"Pulsar","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/pulsar"},{"inline":true,"label":"ShardingSphere","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sharding-sphere"},{"inline":true,"label":"RuoYi","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/ruo-yi"}],"readingTime":8.91,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"sso-for-apache-data-platforms","title":"DolphinScheduler, IoTDB and Other Apache Data Platforms: SSO with Casdoor","description":"Which Apache data projects support Casdoor SSO, since which version and with which config keys: DolphinScheduler, IoTDB, ShenYu, Pulsar Manager, ElasticJob UI.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","OIDC","DolphinScheduler","IoTDB","ShenYu","Pulsar","ShardingSphere","RuoYi"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Spring Boot SSO mit OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server und Spring Cloud Gateway mit Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc"},"nextItem":{"title":"Steam OAuth and Telegram OAuth with Casdoor: Adding Non-Standard Social Logins Through OIDC","permalink":"/de/blog/steam-telegram-login"}},"content":"Several Apache data and middleware projects ship a Casdoor login of their own: Apache DolphinScheduler has an authentication type called `CASDOOR_SSO` (since 3.2.0) and a generic OIDC mode (since 3.4.0), Apache ShenYu has a Casdoor plugin (since 2.5.1), Apache Pulsar Manager has a \\"Log in with Casdoor\\" button (since 0.3.0), and Apache APISIX has the `authz-casdoor` plugin (since 2.14.0). Apache IoTDB is the exception: the database itself authenticates with its own users and passwords, and only the now archived IoTDB Web Workbench talks to Casdoor. The table below lists every project, the version, and where the settings live; the second half of the post sets up user authentication in IoTDB step by step.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Which Apache projects support Casdoor\\n\\nAll facts in this table come from each project\'s source tree and release tags, checked on 2026-11-02.\\n\\n| Project | How it connects to Casdoor | First release | Settings |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Apache DolphinScheduler | Built-in `CASDOOR_SSO` authentication type (Casdoor Java SDK, OAuth 2.0 code flow) | 3.2.0 | `security.authentication.type`, `casdoor.*` in `dolphinscheduler-api/src/main/resources/application.yaml` |\\n| Apache DolphinScheduler | Generic OIDC login (Spring Security) | 3.4.0 | `security.authentication.oidc.*` in the same file |\\n| Apache IoTDB Web Workbench | Casdoor login in the web console | no tagged release, repository archived | `casdoor.*` in `backend/src/main/resources/application.properties`, `VUE_APP_CASDOOR` in `frontend/.env` |\\n| Apache IoTDB (server) | Native users and roles; an `OpenIdAuthorizer` existed up to 2.0.8 and was removed | n/a | see the IoTDB section below |\\n| Apache ShenYu | Casdoor plugin on the gateway | 2.5.1 | plugin config `endpoint`, `client_id`, `client_secrect`, `certificate`, `organization-name`, `application-name` |\\n| Apache Pulsar Manager | \\"Log in with Casdoor\\" button next to the password form | 0.3.0 | `casdoor.*` in `src/main/resources/application.properties`; front-end config in `front-end/src/main.js` since 0.4.0 |\\n| Apache ShardingSphere ElasticJob UI | \\"Login with Casdoor\\" button | master only, after 3.0.2 | `casdoor.*` in the backend `application.properties` |\\n| Apache APISIX | `authz-casdoor` plugin, or `openid-connect` | 2.14.0 | `endpoint_addr`, `client_id`, `client_secret`, `callback_url` |\\n| RuoYi-Cloud (not an Apache project) | Code changes with the Casdoor Spring Boot SDK | n/a | your own login controller |\\n\\nEvery SDK-based integration in the table follows the same pattern. You create an application in Casdoor, copy its client ID, client secret and the public certificate of its token cert, and paste them into the project\'s config together with the organization and application names. The project redirects the browser to Casdoor, receives an authorization code, exchanges it for a JWT, and verifies the JWT with the certificate. The certificate is the one under **Certs** in the Casdoor admin console; see [certificates](/docs/cert/overview) and the **Token cert** field in the [application settings reference](/docs/application/terminology).\\n\\n## Apache DolphinScheduler\\n\\nDolphinScheduler gives you two ways in.\\n\\n**`CASDOOR_SSO`** has been there since 3.2.0. Set the type and fill the top-level `casdoor` block of `dolphinscheduler-api/src/main/resources/application.yaml`:\\n\\n```yaml\\nsecurity:\\n  authentication:\\n    type: CASDOOR_SSO\\n    casdoor:\\n      user:\\n        admin: \\"alice\\"   # this Casdoor user becomes a DolphinScheduler admin\\ncasdoor:\\n  endpoint: https://door.example.com\\n  client-id: <client id>\\n  client-secret: <client secret>\\n  certificate: |-\\n    -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\\n    ...\\n    -----END CERTIFICATE-----\\n  organization-name: my-org\\n  application-name: app-dolphinscheduler\\n  redirect-url: http://localhost:5173/login\\n```\\n\\n`redirect-url` is the DolphinScheduler UI login page, and it must also be in the **Redirect URLs** of the Casdoor application. On the first login, DolphinScheduler creates a local user with the Casdoor user name and email. The user named in `security.authentication.casdoor.user.admin` gets the admin type; everyone else becomes a general user. Our [DolphinScheduler guide](/docs/integration/java/dolphinscheduler) has the screenshots.\\n\\n**OIDC** arrived in 3.4.0. It is generic, so Casdoor works as an ordinary issuer. Spring Security\'s discovery requires the configured issuer to match the `issuer` in the discovery document, and Casdoor\'s issuer is the origin of its backend, so `issuer-uri` is just the Casdoor URL:\\n\\n```yaml\\napi:\\n  base-url: https://ds.example.com/dolphinscheduler\\n  ui-url: https://ds.example.com\\nsecurity:\\n  authentication:\\n    type: OIDC\\n    oidc:\\n      enable: true\\n      providers:\\n        casdoor:\\n          display-name: \\"Login with Casdoor\\"\\n          issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n          client-id: <client id>\\n          client-secret: <client secret>\\n          scope: openid, profile, email\\n          user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n          groups-claim: groups\\n      user:\\n        auto-create: true\\n        default-tenant-code: \\"default\\"\\n        default-queue: \\"default\\"\\n        admin-group-mapping:\\n          - my-org/ds-admins\\n```\\n\\nThe redirect URL to register in Casdoor is `https://ds.example.com/dolphinscheduler/login/oauth2/code/casdoor`, that is, `api.base-url` plus `/login/oauth2/code/` plus the provider key. With the `profile` scope, Casdoor\'s UserInfo endpoint returns `preferred_username` and `groups`. By default groups arrive as `my-org/ds-admins`. Set **Token group format** to `Name (group)` in the application if you would rather write `ds-admins` in `admin-group-mapping`. If `auto-create` is `false` (the upstream default), only users that already exist in DolphinScheduler can sign in. The [standard OIDC client guide](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) lists what the discovery document and UserInfo return.\\n\\n## Apache ShenYu\\n\\nThe Casdoor plugin has shipped with the ShenYu gateway since 2.5.1. You turn it on in the ShenYu admin console and fill in the plugin data. Two details come from the source rather than the docs. First, the secret key is spelled `client_secrect`. Second, the certificate field is a single line, so you write `\\\\n` where each line break goes. The plugin accepts a JWT from the `Authorization` header, or a `code` and `state` on the query string. It then passes the user downstream in the headers `name`, `id` and `organization`. Requests that carry neither are rejected with a token error, not redirected. On ShenYu\'s master branch (October 2026) the plugin moved out of the main repository into the ShenYu plugin store. The plugin name, fields and headers stayed the same, and you add the starter `shenyu-spring-boot-starter-plugin-casdoor` yourself. See [Using Casdoor in ShenYu](/docs/integration/java/shenyu).\\n\\n## Apache Pulsar Manager and ShardingSphere ElasticJob UI\\n\\nPulsar Manager added Casdoor in 0.3.0. The back end reads `casdoor.endpoint`, `casdoor.clientId`, `casdoor.clientSecret`, `casdoor.certificate`, `casdoor.organizationName` and `casdoor.applicationName` from `src/main/resources/application.properties`. Since 0.4.0 the front end uses `casdoor-vue-sdk`, configured in `front-end/src/main.js` with `serverUrl`, `clientId`, `organizationName`, `appName` and `redirectPath`. A first-time Casdoor user gets a local Pulsar Manager account. See [Pulsar Manager](/docs/integration/java/Pulsar-manager).\\n\\nElasticJob UI, the console of Apache ShardingSphere ElasticJob, merged Casdoor login in November 2022. That was after its last release, 3.0.2, so you need to build master. The keys use dashes: `casdoor.endpoint`, `casdoor.client-id`, `casdoor.client-secret`, `casdoor.certificate`, `casdoor.organization-name`, `casdoor.application-name`. See [ShardingSphere](/docs/integration/java/ShardingSphere).\\n\\nFor Apache APISIX, both plugins are covered in [APISIX](/docs/integration/lua/apisix).\\n\\n## Step by step user authentication setup in Apache IoTDB\\n\\nIoTDB authenticates every session (CLI, JDBC, Session API) with a user name and password stored by the database. Since there is no Casdoor login on the server side, the setup is IoTDB\'s native user model. The statements below are for the tree model. The table model of IoTDB 2.x has its own syntax in the [table model guide](https://iotdb.apache.org/UserGuide/latest-Table/User-Manual/Authority-Management_apache.html).\\n\\n**Step 1. Change the root password.** `root` is the only administrator and cannot be deleted. Log in with the CLI and set a strong password:\\n\\n```sql\\nALTER USER root SET PASSWORD \'Str0ng_Passw0rd!\';\\n```\\n\\n**Step 2. Create users.** User names and passwords are 4 to 32 characters long. They can contain letters, digits and `!@#$%^&*()_+-=`, and from V2.0.7 a password cannot equal the user name. TimechoDB, Timecho\'s commercial IoTDB distribution, requires 12 to 32 characters with upper and lower case, a digit and a symbol, and it has a different default root password. Change it the same way.\\n\\n```sql\\nCREATE USER ln_reader \'Read_Passw0rd!\';\\nCREATE USER ops_admin \'Ops_Passw0rd!\';\\n```\\n\\n**Step 3. Group privileges in roles.** New users have no privileges. Series privileges (`READ_DATA`, `WRITE_DATA`, `READ_SCHEMA`, `WRITE_SCHEMA`, or the aliases `READ`, `WRITE`, `ALL`) are granted on path patterns:\\n\\n```sql\\nCREATE ROLE ln_readers;\\nGRANT READ ON root.ln.** TO ROLE ln_readers;\\nGRANT ROLE ln_readers TO ln_reader;\\n```\\n\\n**Step 4. Delegate user management.** This is where versions differ. From V2.0.7, global privileges are `SYSTEM` and `SECURITY`. Before that, they were split into `MANAGE_USER`, `MANAGE_ROLE`, `MANAGE_DATABASE` and others. Global privileges are always granted on `root.**`:\\n\\n```sql\\n-- IoTDB V2.0.7 and later, TimechoDB\\nGRANT SECURITY ON root.** TO USER ops_admin;\\n-- before V2.0.7\\nGRANT MANAGE_USER, MANAGE_ROLE ON root.** TO USER ops_admin;\\n```\\n\\n**Step 5. Connect and verify.**\\n\\n```bash\\nsbin/start-cli.sh -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6667 -u ln_reader -pw \'Read_Passw0rd!\'\\n```\\n\\nThen run `LIST PRIVILEGES OF USER ln_reader;`. A user\'s effective privileges are the union of their own and those of their roles. To take a privilege away, revoke it from both the user and every role that grants it.\\n\\n### What about OIDC in IoTDB?\\n\\nOlder IoTDB versions had `authorizer_provider_class=org.apache.iotdb.commons.auth.authorizer.OpenIdAuthorizer` with `openID_url`. The client passed an access token as the user name with an empty password. That authorizer was built around Keycloak. It treats a user as an administrator only if the token\'s `realm_access.roles` contains `iotdb_admin`, and every privilege check reduces to that test. Its token parser also sets a near-infinite clock skew, which in practice ignores token expiry. Casdoor does not emit `realm_access`, so this was never a fit. IoTDB removed it in March 2026 ([apache/iotdb#17320](https://github.com/apache/iotdb/pull/17320)), and it is absent from 2.0.10 and 2.0.11.\\n\\nIf you want Casdoor accounts in front of IoTDB, the option that exists is the [IoTDB Web Workbench](/docs/integration/java/iotdb) integration. Its repository is archived on GitHub, so treat it as a starting point rather than a maintained product. The database connection itself still uses an IoTDB user from the steps above.\\n\\n## RuoYi\\n\\nRuoYi-Cloud has no Casdoor switch. Our [RuoYi guide](/docs/integration/java/RuoYi) replaces its password login with the Casdoor authorization code flow through the Casdoor Spring Boot SDK. It syncs the existing RuoYi users into Casdoor with a syncer, using bcrypt so their passwords keep working. For a plain Spring Boot app, start with [Spring Boot SSO with OIDC](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc).\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- Apache DolphinScheduler, [authentication types](https://github.com/apache/dolphinscheduler/blob/dev/docs/docs/en/guide/security/authentication-type.md) and [`application.yaml`](https://github.com/apache/dolphinscheduler/blob/dev/dolphinscheduler-api/src/main/resources/application.yaml)\\n- Apache IoTDB, [Authority Management (from V2.0.7)](https://iotdb.apache.org/UserGuide/latest/User-Manual/Authority-Management-Upgrade_apache.html), [Authority Management (before V2.0.7)](https://iotdb.apache.org/UserGuide/latest/User-Manual/Authority-Management_apache.html), [CLI](https://iotdb.apache.org/UserGuide/latest/Tools-System/CLI_apache.html)\\n- Timecho, [TimechoDB Authority Management](https://www.timecho.com/docs/UserGuide/latest/User-Manual/Authority-Management-Upgrade_timecho.html)\\n- [apache/iotdb-web-workbench](https://github.com/apache/iotdb-web-workbench)\\n- Apache ShenYu, [Casdoor plugin](https://shenyu.apache.org/docs/plugin-center/security/casdoor/) and [plugin store cutover](https://github.com/apache/shenyu/blob/master/docs/plugin-store-casdoor.md)\\n- [apache/pulsar-manager](https://github.com/apache/pulsar-manager) and [apache/shardingsphere-elasticjob-ui](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere-elasticjob-ui)\\n- Apache APISIX, [authz-casdoor](https://apisix.apache.org/docs/apisix/plugins/authz-casdoor/)"},{"id":"steam-telegram-login","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/steam-telegram-login","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-steam-telegram-login.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-steam-telegram-login.mdx","title":"Steam OAuth and Telegram OAuth with Casdoor: Adding Non-Standard Social Logins Through OIDC","description":"Steam uses OpenID 2.0 and Telegram signs widget data with HMAC. Here is how Casdoor adds Steam, Telegram, X and Facebook login and hands your app plain OIDC.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"Steam","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/steam"},{"inline":true,"label":"Telegram","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/telegram"},{"inline":true,"label":"Social Login","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/social-login"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":9.21,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"steam-telegram-login","title":"Steam OAuth and Telegram OAuth with Casdoor: Adding Non-Standard Social Logins Through OIDC","description":"Steam uses OpenID 2.0 and Telegram signs widget data with HMAC. Here is how Casdoor adds Steam, Telegram, X and Facebook login and hands your app plain OIDC.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","OAuth","OIDC","Steam","Telegram","Social Login","SSO"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"DolphinScheduler, IoTDB and Other Apache Data Platforms: SSO with Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/sso-for-apache-data-platforms"},"nextItem":{"title":"Dummy Payment Gateway for Testing: Casdoor\'s Dummy Provider, Stripe Test Mode and PayPal Sandbox","permalink":"/de/blog/test-payments-without-a-gateway"}},"content":"There is no \\"Steam OAuth\\": Steam signs users in with OpenID 2.0 and gives you a 64-bit SteamID, nothing more. Telegram\'s classic Login Widget isn\'t OAuth either: it hands your page a set of user fields plus an HMAC-SHA256 hash that you check with your bot token. Telegram now also runs a real OpenID Connect provider at `oauth.telegram.org`. If you put Casdoor between your app and these providers, Casdoor does the protocol-specific checks and your app only ever speaks OpenID Connect to Casdoor, the same way it would for Google or GitHub.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The providers at a glance\\n\\n| Provider | Protocol | What identifies the user | Email available? | Casdoor provider type |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Steam | OpenID 2.0 at `https://steamcommunity.com/openid/login`, plus the Steam Web API for the profile | SteamID64, taken from the claimed ID `https://steamcommunity.com/openid/id/<steamid>` | No | `Steam` |\\n| Telegram (Login Widget) | Signed widget data, HMAC-SHA256 keyed with the SHA-256 of the bot token | Telegram user `id` | No | `Telegram` |\\n| Telegram (OpenID Connect) | OIDC authorization code flow with PKCE at `https://oauth.telegram.org` | `sub` claim of the ID token | No email claim; phone with the `phone` scope | `Custom` |\\n| X (Twitter) | OAuth 2.0 authorization code with PKCE | X user ID from `/2/users/me` | Not with Casdoor\'s default scopes | `Twitter` |\\n| Facebook | OAuth 2.0 authorization code | App-scoped user ID | Only if the profile has a valid primary email | `Facebook` |\\n\\nThe rest of this post goes through what each protocol actually returns and what Casdoor does with it.\\n\\n## Steam: OpenID 2.0, no OAuth\\n\\nSteam\'s developer pages describe Steam as an OpenID provider: your site sends the user to Steam, and Steam sends back a claimed ID of the form `https://steamcommunity.com/openid/id/<steamid>`. The number at the end is the user\'s 64-bit SteamID. There is no authorization code, no access token, no scopes, and no email: neither the OpenID response nor the Web API profile contains one.\\n\\nThat is why searches for \\"steam oauth\\" lead nowhere useful: the protocol is OpenID 2.0, the predecessor of OpenID Connect, so an OAuth 2.0 or OIDC client library can\'t talk to Steam directly.\\n\\nWhat Casdoor does for a Steam sign-in:\\n\\n1. The Casdoor sign-in page builds an OpenID 2.0 `checkid_setup` request to `https://steamcommunity.com/openid/login`, with `openid.realm` set to the Casdoor origin and `openid.return_to` set to Casdoor\'s `/callback` page.\\n2. When Steam redirects back, Casdoor posts the signed fields back to Steam with `openid.mode=check_authentication`. Only if Steam answers `is_valid:true` does it continue. It also checks that `openid.claimed_id` matches the `steamcommunity.com/openid/id/<digits>` pattern and extracts the SteamID from it.\\n3. With the Steam Web API key that you enter as the provider\'s **Client secret**, Casdoor calls `ISteamUser/GetPlayerSummaries` to get the persona name and the avatar.\\n\\nThe resulting Casdoor user has the SteamID64 as its username, the Steam persona name as its display name and the Steam avatar. The email field stays empty. Casdoor deliberately drops the placeholder text that the underlying library puts there. If your app needs an email address, collect it after sign-up.\\n\\nSetup takes two minutes: register a Web API key for your Casdoor domain on Steam\'s key page, create a provider of type `Steam` and paste the key as the client secret. The [Steam provider guide](/docs/provider/oauth/Steam) has the steps.\\n\\n## Telegram: the Login Widget and its HMAC check\\n\\nThe classic Telegram login is a JavaScript widget. You create a bot with @BotFather, link your domain to it with `/setdomain`, and embed the widget. When the user confirms in Telegram, the widget either redirects to the URL in `data-auth-url` or calls a JavaScript function, passing `id`, `first_name`, `last_name`, `username`, `photo_url`, `auth_date` and `hash`.\\n\\nNothing in that payload is a token. Anyone can type those fields into a URL, so the server must check the `hash`. Telegram\'s documented check works like this:\\n\\n```text\\ndata_check_string = all received fields except hash,\\n                    as key=value, sorted by key, joined with \\"\\\\n\\"\\nsecret_key        = SHA256(bot_token)\\nvalid             = hex(HMAC_SHA256(data_check_string, secret_key)) == hash\\n```\\n\\nTelegram also tells you to check `auth_date` so that old data can\'t be replayed, but it leaves the window up to you.\\n\\nIn Casdoor, the `Telegram` provider type takes the bot username (without `@`) as the **Client ID** and the bot token as the **Client secret**. When a user clicks the Telegram button, Casdoor opens its own `/telegram-login` page, which loads `telegram-widget.js` from telegram.org with your bot username and points `data-auth-url` back at Casdoor\'s `/callback`. The callback collects the fields and the Casdoor backend then:\\n\\n- rebuilds the data-check string from every field except `hash`, sorts it, and compares the HMAC-SHA256 against `hash` using `SHA256(bot token)` as the key;\\n- rejects the data if `auth_date` is more than 24 hours old;\\n- uses the numeric Telegram `id` as the identity, the `username` as the username, the first and last name as the display name, and `photo_url` as the avatar.\\n\\nTelegram usernames are optional. When a user has none, Casdoor generates `telegram_<id>`, which stays the same at every sign-in. The widget never sends an email address or a phone number, so a Telegram-only user in Casdoor has neither. See the [Telegram provider guide](/docs/provider/oauth/telegram) for the BotFather steps.\\n\\n## Telegram now offers OpenID Connect too\\n\\nTelegram\'s \\"Log In With Telegram\\" page now documents a standard OpenID Connect flow, and the old iframe widget documentation has moved to an archive page. The facts from Telegram\'s docs:\\n\\n- Discovery document: `https://oauth.telegram.org/.well-known/openid-configuration`. Authorization endpoint `https://oauth.telegram.org/auth`, token endpoint `https://oauth.telegram.org/token`, keys at `https://oauth.telegram.org/.well-known/jwks.json`.\\n- In @BotFather you open your bot\'s **Login Widget** settings, register the allowed URLs (origins and exact redirect URIs), and get a client ID and client secret.\\n- Authorization code flow, PKCE with `S256` recommended. The token request uses HTTP Basic authentication with the client ID and secret.\\n- Scopes: `openid` is required; `profile` adds the name, `preferred_username` and `picture`; `phone` adds `phone_number` with the user\'s consent. There is no email claim.\\n- There is no UserInfo endpoint. All user data is in the ID token, whose `iss` is `https://oauth.telegram.org` and whose `aud` is your bot ID.\\n\\nYou can connect this flow with a Casdoor [custom OAuth provider](/docs/provider/oauth/CustomProvider). Set the **Auth URL** and **Token URL** to the endpoints above, set **Scope** to `openid profile` (add `phone` if you need it), turn on **Enable PKCE**, and leave **UserInfo URL** empty. Register Casdoor\'s `https://<your-casdoor-host>/callback` as an allowed URL in BotFather. With no UserInfo URL, Casdoor takes the user from the ID token in the token response: it requires `sub`, rejects an expired token, and checks that `aud` contains the client ID. Without a user mapping, `sub` becomes the user ID, `preferred_username` the username, `name` the display name and `picture` the avatar, and `phone_number` fills the phone. The Casdoor sign-in page recognizes a custom provider whose auth URL points at `oauth.telegram.org` and keeps the OAuth `state` short for it.\\n\\nNote that a Telegram user signed in through the widget and the same person signed in through OIDC are not automatically the same Casdoor user. The widget identity is the numeric `id`, while the OIDC identity is `sub`, and Telegram\'s own examples show different values for the two. Pick one method per application.\\n\\n## X (Twitter) and Facebook\\n\\nThese two are ordinary OAuth 2.0, but each has a detail worth knowing.\\n\\n**X** requires a PKCE challenge on its OAuth 2.0 authorize URL. Casdoor\'s `Twitter` type generates a new code verifier for every sign-in, sends the `S256` challenge, and passes the verifier in the token exchange, so there is nothing to configure beyond the client ID and secret. Casdoor requests the `users.read tweet.read` scopes and reads the user from `/2/users/me`, which gives the user ID, the handle and the display name. It doesn\'t request an email field, so plan for X users without an email address. The [Twitter provider guide](/docs/provider/oauth/Twitter) covers the app settings.\\n\\n**Facebook** returns an app-scoped user ID: the same person has a different ID in each Facebook app, so moving to a new Facebook app means your users won\'t match their old records unless you link them by email. Casdoor asks for `email,public_profile` and reads `id`, `name`, `picture` and `email` from the Graph API. Facebook omits `email` when the profile has no valid address. See the [Facebook provider guide](/docs/provider/oauth/facebook).\\n\\n## How your app sees all of this: plain OIDC\\n\\nYour application never talks to Steam, Telegram, X or Facebook. It is an OIDC client of Casdoor, configured once with the Casdoor issuer, `https://<your-casdoor-host>/.well-known/openid-configuration`. You add the social providers to the Casdoor application on its **Providers** tab, and their buttons appear on the Casdoor sign-in page.\\n\\nAfter any of these sign-ins, Casdoor finds or creates a Casdoor user and finishes the normal authorization code flow with your app. The ID token your app receives is signed by Casdoor, and its `sub` is the Casdoor user ID, not the SteamID or the Telegram ID. So your app needs no OpenID 2.0 library, no HMAC code, no PKCE quirks per provider, and no special case for users without an email. The original provider ID is stored on the Casdoor user, for example in the `steam` or `telegram` field, if you ever need it.\\n\\nA few things to plan for:\\n\\n- **Account linking.** Casdoor links a social sign-in to an existing user by verified email, verified phone, or username, in the order the application\'s binding rule sets. Steam and widget-based Telegram provide neither email nor phone, so they create a separate user unless the user links the provider from an account that is already signed in. See [how Casdoor links accounts](/docs/provider/oauth/overview#automatic-account-linking).\\n- **Extra profile fields.** Providers that pass on raw data, such as the Goth-based Steam type and custom providers, can fill more user fields through [user mapping](/docs/provider/oauth/user-mapping).\\n- **The client side.** Any standard library works against Casdoor. [Connect a standard OIDC client](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) lists the endpoints, and the [Spring Boot SSO with OIDC](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc) post shows a complete app.\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- Steam Web API and OpenID: [steamcommunity.com/dev](https://steamcommunity.com/dev)\\n- Steamworks, User Authentication and Ownership: [partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/auth](https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/auth)\\n- Telegram, Log In With Telegram (OpenID Connect): [core.telegram.org/bots/telegram-login](https://core.telegram.org/bots/telegram-login)\\n- Telegram, Login Widget (legacy): [core.telegram.org/widgets/login-legacy](https://core.telegram.org/widgets/login-legacy)\\n- X, OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow with PKCE: [docs.x.com](https://docs.x.com/fundamentals/authentication/oauth-2-0/authorization-code)\\n- Meta, Graph API User reference: [developers.facebook.com/docs/graph-api/reference/user](https://developers.facebook.com/docs/graph-api/reference/user/)\\n- Casdoor source: [`idp/telegram.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/idp/telegram.go), [`idp/goth.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/idp/goth.go), [`idp/custom.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/idp/custom.go)"},{"id":"test-payments-without-a-gateway","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/test-payments-without-a-gateway","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-test-payments-without-a-gateway.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-test-payments-without-a-gateway.mdx","title":"Dummy Payment Gateway for Testing: Casdoor\'s Dummy Provider, Stripe Test Mode and PayPal Sandbox","description":"A dummy payment gateway completes checkout without moving money. How the Casdoor Dummy provider works, and when to switch to Stripe test mode or PayPal sandbox.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Payment","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/payment"},{"inline":true,"label":"Testing","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/testing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Stripe","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/stripe"},{"inline":true,"label":"PayPal","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/pay-pal"},{"inline":true,"label":"Subscription","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/subscription"}],"readingTime":8.82,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"test-payments-without-a-gateway","title":"Dummy Payment Gateway for Testing: Casdoor\'s Dummy Provider, Stripe Test Mode and PayPal Sandbox","description":"A dummy payment gateway completes checkout without moving money. How the Casdoor Dummy provider works, and when to switch to Stripe test mode or PayPal sandbox.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Payment","Testing","Stripe","PayPal","Subscription"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Steam OAuth and Telegram OAuth with Casdoor: Adding Non-Standard Social Logins Through OIDC","permalink":"/de/blog/steam-telegram-login"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor v4: Eine neue Konsole, ein Marktplatz und OAuth f\xfcr MCP-Server","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-v4"}},"content":"A dummy payment gateway is a payment provider that accepts every checkout as paid without contacting a bank or card network, so you can test orders, subscriptions and fulfillment with no money and no gateway account. Casdoor ships one: the [Dummy payment provider](/docs/provider/payment/dummy). Add it to a product, buy the product, and Casdoor records the payment and the order as `Paid`, starts the subscription and writes the transaction, all without leaving your server. Use Dummy in development, and switch to a vendor sandbox (Stripe test mode, PayPal sandbox, Paddle sandbox) in staging, where you also need declined cards, 3-D Secure and real redirects.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Dummy, Balance and vendor sandboxes at a glance\\n\\n| Provider | Test environment | How Casdoor picks test or live | Casdoor provider type |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Dummy | None needed, every payment succeeds | Always simulated | `Dummy` |\\n| Balance | Internal, funded by a recharge product | Always internal | `Balance` |\\n| Stripe | Sandbox with `sk_test_` / `pk_test_` keys | By the keys you enter | `Stripe` |\\n| PayPal | Sandbox apps and sandbox accounts | `runmode` other than `prod` uses the sandbox | `PayPal` |\\n| Paddle | Separate sandbox account at `sandbox-vendors.paddle.com` | `runmode` other than `prod` uses `sandbox-api.paddle.com` | `Paddle` |\\n| Adyen | Test environment (`checkout-test.adyen.com`) | `runmode` other than `prod` uses the test environment | `Adyen` |\\n| AirWallex | Separate sandbox account | `runmode` other than `prod` calls `api-demo.airwallex.com` | `AirWallex` |\\n| Lemon Squeezy | Store test mode with test-mode API keys | By the API key you enter | `Lemon Squeezy` |\\n| FastSpring | Test storefront with `.test.` in the host | By the **Host** you enter | `FastSpring` |\\n| Polar | Sandbox at `sandbox.polar.sh` | Not supported, Casdoor always calls `api.polar.sh` | `Polar` |\\n\\n`runmode` is the option in `conf/app.conf` (or the `runmode` environment variable) described in the [configuration reference](/docs/basic/configuration). Its default is `dev`, so a fresh Casdoor talks to the sandboxes of PayPal, Paddle, Adyen and AirWallex until you set it to `prod`.\\n\\n## What a dummy transaction means\\n\\nA dummy transaction runs through the same records as a real purchase (order, payment, subscription, ledger entry) but moves no money. It is different from a sandbox transaction. A sandbox still calls the vendor\'s API, shows the vendor\'s checkout page and sends webhooks; only the money is fake. A dummy transaction never leaves your system.\\n\\nThat difference decides what each one can test. A dummy gateway is good for your own logic: does the plan grant the right role, does the order show up, does the coupon count as used, does the user land on the right page. A sandbox is good for the integration: credentials, redirects, card declines, authentication challenges and webhook delivery. You need both, at different stages.\\n\\n## How the Casdoor Dummy provider works\\n\\nThe Dummy provider has no settings and no payment page. Here is the full flow on Casdoor master:\\n\\n1. When the user pays, the provider\'s `Pay` call returns the Casdoor result page as the payment URL. It packs the price and currency into the order ID instead of creating anything at a vendor.\\n1. Casdoor stores the payment in state `Created` and sends the browser straight to the result page. There is no card form, fake or otherwise.\\n1. The result page sees a `Created` payment and calls `POST /api/notify-payment` about a second later.\\n1. The provider\'s `Notify` call always answers `Paid`, with the price it packed earlier, so the price check passes.\\n1. Casdoor then runs the normal success path: the payment and the order become `Paid`, the subscriptions created for the payment are updated, a `Purchase` transaction is written, product stock is reduced and a coupon on the order is marked as used. If the product is a recharge product, a `Recharge` transaction also adds the amount to the user\'s balance.\\n\\nSo yes, Dummy marks the order as paid right away, and the user sees the success page with no step in between.\\n\\nTwo limits follow from that design. Dummy can\'t fail: there is no way to make it return `Canceled`, `Timeout` or `Error`, so failure paths need a sandbox. And Dummy can\'t renew: Casdoor only allows auto renewal for Balance and Stripe, so a subscription bought with Dummy always has auto renewal off.\\n\\nOn a new database, Casdoor creates a built-in Dummy provider named `provider_payment_dummy` in the `admin` organization, next to a built-in Balance provider. You can also add your own: in **Identity** > **Providers**, set **Category** to `Payment` and **Type** to `Dummy`.\\n\\n### Keep Dummy out of production\\n\\n`/api/notify-payment` is open to anonymous callers, because real gateways call it without a Casdoor session. For a real provider that is safe, since `Notify` checks the payment with the vendor. Dummy checks nothing and reports `Paid`. Any product that lists Dummy in its **Payment providers** shows it at checkout, and anyone who picks it gets the product for free. Remove Dummy from every product before you go live, and do not put it on products in an organization that real users can reach.\\n\\n## Balance: the internal wallet you can fund with Dummy\\n\\nThe [Balance provider](/docs/provider/payment/Balance) lets users pay from their account balance. Casdoor checks the balance first, deducts the price, and marks the payment and order `Paid` in the same request. Balance is a real production feature, not a test tool, but it pairs well with Dummy: put Dummy on a recharge product, \\"top up\\" a test user, and the balance is credited with no money involved.\\n\\nThis matters for subscriptions, because Balance is one of the two providers that support auto renewal. With Dummy plus Balance you can test the whole renewal loop on a laptop with no gateway account.\\n\\n## Vendor sandboxes for staging\\n\\n**Stripe.** Stripe separates test and live data by keys: sandbox keys start with `pk_test_` and `sk_test_`, live keys with `pk_live_` and `sk_live_`, and objects in one mode are not visible in the other. Casdoor uses whatever keys you put in the [Stripe provider](/docs/provider/payment/stripe); `runmode` does not change anything for Stripe. Stripe\'s test cards cover the paths Dummy can\'t: `4242 4242 4242 4242` succeeds, `4000 0000 0000 0002` is declined, and `4000 0000 0000 3220` requires 3-D Secure. Use any future expiry and any CVC. Stripe\'s terms forbid testing in live mode with real card details.\\n\\n**PayPal.** The PayPal sandbox is a separate environment with its own apps and test accounts. PayPal creates a default personal and a default business sandbox account when you first sign in to the developer dashboard; you pay with the personal one. Create the app in sandbox mode, put its credentials in the [PayPal provider](/docs/provider/payment/paypal), and keep `runmode` at `dev`.\\n\\n**Paddle.** The sandbox is a separate account at `sandbox-vendors.paddle.com` with its own keys and products; nothing is shared with live. Paddle\'s test cards include `4242 4242 4242 4242` (succeeds) and `4000 0000 0000 0002` (declined).\\n\\n**Lemon Squeezy** has a test mode per store, and API keys created in test mode only see test data. **FastSpring** test orders go through a test storefront: insert `.test.` into the store host, for example `yourstore.test.onfastspring.com`, and enter that as the provider\'s **Host**. **Polar** has a sandbox with its own tokens, but the Casdoor Polar provider does not select it, so a Polar provider in Casdoor always works against live Polar. **Alipay** in Casdoor is also hard-wired to production.\\n\\n## A test plan for Casdoor products, plans and subscriptions\\n\\n### Development, with Dummy\\n\\n1. Create a [product](/docs/products/product) and add Dummy to its **Payment providers**.\\n1. Create a [plan](/docs/pricing/plan) for that product and a pricing page that lists it (see the [pricing overview](/docs/pricing/overview)).\\n1. As a test user, open the pricing page and buy the plan. Check that the payment and the order are `Paid`, the subscription is `Active`, and a `Purchase` transaction appears on the user.\\n1. Sign in as the user and check that the plan\'s role took effect in your application.\\n1. If the plan has **Is exclusive** on, buy it again and expect the error that the user already has an active subscription.\\n1. Apply a coupon at checkout and check that its usage is recorded.\\n1. Create a recharge product with Dummy, top up the test user, then buy the plan with Balance and **Auto renew** on.\\n1. To test renewal without waiting a month, edit the subscription\'s **End time** on its edit page so it ends within 24 hours. Casdoor checks subscriptions once an hour; expect **Renewal state** `Renewed` and a new subscription for the next period. Then buy the plan again with a balance too low for the next period, move its end time the same way, and expect `Failed` with the reason in **Renewal message**. The [subscription docs](/docs/pricing/subscription#auto-renewal) describe the exact rules.\\n\\n### Staging, with a sandbox\\n\\n1. Run staging with `runmode = dev` and sandbox or test credentials only.\\n1. Replace Dummy with the provider you will use in production, configured with its sandbox keys.\\n1. Repeat the purchase and check that the browser goes to the vendor\'s checkout and comes back to the Casdoor result page.\\n1. With Stripe, pay once with the decline card and once with the 3-D Secure card, and check the payment and order states for each.\\n1. With Stripe and **Auto renew** on, Casdoor saves the card at checkout and charges it itself at renewal, so the end-time trick above works in the sandbox too.\\n\\n### Going live\\n\\nRemove Dummy from all products, set `runmode = prod` if you use PayPal, Paddle, Adyen or AirWallex, and swap in live keys. Check the [payment records](/docs/products/payment) of the first real orders.\\n\\nIf you also test sign-up email in the same environment, a local mail catcher keeps those messages off real inboxes; see [MailHog vs Mailpit](/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit).\\n\\n## Referenzen {#references}\\n\\n- Casdoor source: [`pp/dummy.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/pp/dummy.go), [`pp/balance.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/pp/balance.go), [`object/order_pay.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/order_pay.go), [`object/payment.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/payment.go), [`object/subscription_renew.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/subscription_renew.go)\\n- Casdoor docs: [Payment providers](/docs/provider/payment/overview), [Dummy](/docs/provider/payment/dummy), [Balance](/docs/provider/payment/Balance), [Subscription](/docs/pricing/subscription)\\n- Stripe: [Testing](https://docs.stripe.com/testing), [API keys](https://docs.stripe.com/keys)\\n- PayPal: [Sandbox](https://developer.paypal.com/tools/sandbox/), [Sandbox accounts](https://developer.paypal.com/tools/sandbox/accounts/)\\n- Paddle: [Sandbox](https://developer.paddle.com/build/tools/sandbox)\\n- Lemon Squeezy: [Test mode](https://docs.lemonsqueezy.com/help/getting-started/test-mode)\\n- FastSpring: [Test orders](https://developer.fastspring.com/docs/test-orders)\\n- Polar: [Sandbox](https://polar.sh/docs/integrate/sandbox)\\n- Adyen: [Live endpoints](https://docs.adyen.com/development-resources/live-endpoints)"},{"id":"casdoor-v4","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-v4","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-30-casdoor-v4.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-30-casdoor-v4.mdx","title":"Casdoor v4: Eine neue Konsole, ein Marktplatz und OAuth f\xfcr MCP-Server","description":"Casdoor v4.0 erschien am 1. September. Zwei Monate und 25 Releases sp\xe4ter sind wir bei v4.25, daher ist dies ein guter Zeitpunkt, um zur\xfcckzublicken, was sich ge\xe4ndert hat. Kurz gesagt: Die Admin-Konsole wurde neu aufgebaut, das Self-Hosting ist deutlich weniger fummelig geworden, es gibt nun einen Marketplace f\xfcr Integrationen und Anmelde-Themes, und Casdoor kann als Autorisierungsserver f\xfcr MCP-Server und KI-Agenten fungieren.","date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"MCP","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/mcp"},{"inline":true,"label":"Self-hosting","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/self-hosting"}],"readingTime":4.87,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-v4","title":"Casdoor v4: Eine neue Konsole, ein Marktplatz und OAuth f\xfcr MCP-Server","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Release","IAM","SSO","OAuth","MCP","Self-hosting"],"date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Dummy Payment Gateway for Testing: Casdoor\'s Dummy Provider, Stripe Test Mode and PayPal Sandbox","permalink":"/de/blog/test-payments-without-a-gateway"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor auf Kubernetes betreiben: Offizielles Helm Chart und Configuration as Code","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"}},"content":"Casdoor v4.0 erschien am 1. September. Zwei Monate und 25 Releases sp\xe4ter sind wir bei v4.25, daher ist dies ein guter Zeitpunkt, um zur\xfcckzublicken, was sich ge\xe4ndert hat. Kurz gesagt: Die Admin-Konsole wurde neu aufgebaut, das Self-Hosting ist deutlich weniger fummelig geworden, es gibt nun einen Marketplace f\xfcr Integrationen und Anmelde-Themes, und Casdoor kann als Autorisierungsserver f\xfcr MCP-Server und KI-Agenten fungieren.\\n\\nFalls Sie es noch nicht verwendet haben: Casdoor ist eine Open-Source-Plattform f\xfcr Identit\xe4ts- und Zugriffsverwaltung, geschrieben in Go. Es ist ein vollst\xe4ndiger Identity Provider, keine Bibliothek oder ein Login-Proxy. Benutzer existieren in Casdoor, Casdoor stellt die Tokens aus, und Ihre Apps kommunizieren \xfcber OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect, SAML 2.0, CAS, LDAP oder SCIM damit. Die Autorisierung wird von [Apache Casbin](https://casbin.apache.org) gehandhabt, sodass ACL-, RBAC- und ABAC-Modelle alle funktionieren. Es steht unter der Apache-2.0-Lizenz und ist im CNCF Landscape gelistet.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Eine neu aufgebaute Konsole und Anmeldeseiten {#a-rebuilt-console-and-sign-in-pages}\\n\\nv4.0 ersetzte die Ant Design Admin-Konsole durch eine neue, die auf shadcn/ui, Tailwind und Vite (jetzt mit React 19) basiert. Sie verf\xfcgt \xfcber eine Befehlspalette, Bulk-Zeilenauswahl, Inline-Formularvalidierung, zweispaltige Formulare und Tabellen, in denen Spalten ausgeblendet werden k\xf6nnen. Der Sprachwechsel l\xe4dt die Seite nicht mehr neu.\\n\\nDie Seiten f\xfcr Anmeldung, Registrierung und Passwort-Reset wurden ebenfalls neu gestaltet. Der Dark Mode funktioniert ordnungsgem\xe4\xdf, und die Seiten sind auf dem Smartphone nutzbar.\\n\\nWenn Sie von v3 upgraden, lesen Sie zuerst den Upgrade-Leitfaden, der in der README verlinkt ist. Das Wichtigste, was Sie pr\xfcfen m\xfcssen, ist der Pfad des Frontend-Builds, wenn Sie statische Dateien \xfcber ein CDN oder einen Reverse Proxy bereitstellen.\\n\\n## Einfacheres Self-Hosting {#easier-to-self-host}\\n\\nViele der Arbeiten an v4 entstanden aus Issues und Forum-Threads von Personen, die Casdoor selbst betreiben:\\n\\n- **Kein Standard-Passwort.** Beim ersten Besuch fragt Casdoor nach dem `admin`-Passwort, oder Sie k\xf6nnen es mit `initAdminPassword` festlegen.\\n- **Bereitstellung unter Sub-Pfad.** Setzen Sie `basePath`, um Casdoor unter `https://example.com/auth/` statt auf einem eigenen Host bereitzustellen.\\n- **Forward Auth.** Casdoor hat einen Forward-Auth-Endpoint f\xfcr Traefik, Caddy und Nginx, sodass Sie Apps ohne eigenes Login dahinter schalten k\xf6nnen, mit \xf6ffentlichen Pfaden und regelspezifischen Einstellungen pro Site.\\n- **Docker-Tags.** Images werden jetzt auch mit `X`, `X.Y` und `X.Y.Z` getaggt, sodass Sie einer Major- oder Minor-Version folgen k\xf6nnen.\\n- **Helm.** Das Chart wurde in das Hauptrepository verschoben, wird bei jedem Release auf GHCR und [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) ver\xf6ffentlicht und kann Organisationen, Anwendungen und Benutzer als Konfiguration enthalten. Siehe [Casdoor auf Kubernetes ausf\xfchren](/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes).\\n- **Windows-Dienst.** Die Binary kann als Windows-Dienst ausgef\xfchrt werden.\\n- **Upgrades.** Die Systeminfo-Seite zeigt die neueste Version an und kann ein offizielles Binary mit einem Klick aktualisieren.\\n- **Betrieb.** Container-Health-Checks, Prometheus-Counter f\xfcr erfolgreiche und fehlgeschlagene Anmeldungen, Audit-Logs, die an Syslog weitergeleitet werden, sowie Redis Cluster-Support.\\n\\n## Marktplatz {#marketplace}\\n\\nDer neue [Marketplace](https://marketplace.casdoor.com) erm\xf6glicht es Administratoren, Integrationen direkt innerhalb von Casdoor zu installieren: Anwendungsvorlagen, Identity Provider und Anmelde-Themes. Bei der Installation wird die Konfiguration automatisch ausgef\xfcllt, und bei der Deinstallation wird der vorherige Zustand wiederhergestellt.\\n\\nEs gibt bisher mehr als 20 Anmelde-Themes. Sie k\xf6nnen ein Theme auf Ihrer eigenen Anmeldeseite vorinstalliert testen. Organisationsadministratoren k\xf6nnen die Anmeldeseiten au\xdferdem mit ihren eigenen Schriftarten, Bildern und HTML anpassen, innerhalb einer Allowlist, die Formulare und Skripte blockiert. Die schreibgesch\xfctzte Demo unter [door.casdoor.com](https://door.casdoor.com) wechselt zwischen den Themes, wenn Sie sie sehen m\xf6chten.\\n\\n## OAuth f\xfcr MCP-Server und KI-Agenten {#oauth-for-mcp-servers-and-ai-agents}\\n\\nDie MCP-Spezifikation verlangt, dass Remote-MCP-Server OAuth verwenden, und MCP-Clients kennen im Voraus normalerweise nicht den Autorisierungsserver, dem sie begegnen werden. Casdoor deckt nun die erforderlichen Komponenten ab:\\n\\n- OAuth 2.1 Authorization Code Flow mit PKCE und eine Consent-Seite, auf der Benutzer nur einige der angeforderten Scopes gew\xe4hren k\xf6nnen\\n- Dynamic Client Registration (RFC 7591) und Client-Management (RFC 7592)\\n- Client ID Metadata Documents: Ein MCP-Client kann eine URL als `client_id` verwenden, ohne sich vorher zu registrieren\\n- `private_key_jwt` Client-Authentifizierung f\xfcr dynamisch registrierte Clients\\n- Resource Indicators (RFC 8707) und Protected Resource Metadata (RFC 9728)\\n- Token Exchange (RFC 8693), einschlie\xdflich ID-JAGs f\xfcr unternehmensverwaltete Autorisierung, sodass der IdP einer Organisation entscheiden kann, welche Agenten auf welche MCP-Server zugreifen d\xfcrfen\\n\\nRichten Sie die gesch\xfctzten Ressourcen-Metadaten Ihres MCP-Servers auf Casdoor, und Anmeldung, Consent, Token-Ausstellung und Validierung werden f\xfcr Sie erledigt. Casdoor betreibt auch seinen eigenen MCP-Server, sodass ein Assistent wie Claude Code oder Cursor Benutzer, Anwendungen und Berechtigungen dar\xfcber verwalten kann.\\n\\n## Weitere Protokollarbeiten {#other-protocol-work}\\n\\n- SAML Single Logout und IdP-initiiertes SSO\\n- OIDC `prompt=none`, bessere Konformit\xe4t f\xfcr Hybrid-Flow, `prompt` und `max_age` sowie den RFC 9207 `iss`-Parameter\\n- Token-Widerruf (RFC 7009), DPoP und der Device Authorization Flow\\n- Magic Link-Anmeldung, Pr\xfcfung der Passwort-Historie und Passkeys, die \xfcber Geschwister-Subdomains geteilt werden\\n- Exklusive Anmeldung mit einer maximalen Anzahl von Sitzungen pro Benutzer\\n\\n## Ausprobieren {#try-it}\\n\\nDer schnellste Weg, sich umzusehen:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -p 8000:8000 casbin/casdoor-all-in-one\\n```\\n\\n\xd6ffnen Sie dann http://localhost:8000. Das Image enth\xe4lt SQLite und Beispieldaten, verwenden Sie es also zum Ausprobieren, nicht f\xfcr Produktion. Der [Installationsleitfaden](/docs/basic/server-installation) behandelt reale Bereitstellungen.\\n\\nEs gibt zwei Online-Demos: [demo.casdoor.com](https://demo.casdoor.com) ist beschreibbar und setzt sich alle f\xfcnf Minuten zur\xfcck, und [door.casdoor.com](https://door.casdoor.com) ist schreibgesch\xfctzt.\\n\\nWenn Sie es lieber nicht selbst betreiben m\xf6chten, bietet Ihnen [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing) eine dedizierte, verwaltete Instanz zu einem Festpreis ohne Geb\xfchren pro Benutzer. Neue Konten erhalten 10 $ Guthaben zum Testen.\\n\\n## Links\\n\\n- GitHub: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor\\n- Release Notes: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/releases\\n- Docs: https://casdoor.ai/docs/overview\\n- Discord: https://discord.gg/5rPsrAzK7S\\n\\nFehlerberichte und Funktionsw\xfcnsche sind auf GitHub willkommen. Wenn Casdoor f\xfcr Sie n\xfctzlich ist, hilft ein Stern anderen, es zu finden."},{"id":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","title":"Casdoor auf Kubernetes betreiben: Offizielles Helm Chart und Configuration as Code","description":"Casdoor verf\xfcgt \xfcber ein offizielles Helm Chart. Es befindet sich im Hauptrepository unter manifests/casdoor, wird bei jedem Casdoor-Release im GHCR ver\xf6ffentlicht und ist auf Artifact Hub gelistet. Ab Casdoor v4.18.0 kann das Chart auch Ihre Organisationen, Anwendungen, Benutzer und Provider als deklarative Konfiguration enthalten: \xc4ndern Sie diese in der values-Datei, f\xfchren Sie helm upgrade aus, und die laufenden Pods wenden die \xc4nderung ohne Neustart an.","date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Kubernetes","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/kubernetes"},{"inline":true,"label":"Helm","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/helm"},{"inline":true,"label":"GitOps","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/git-ops"},{"inline":true,"label":"Configuration as Code","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/configuration-as-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":5.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","title":"Casdoor auf Kubernetes betreiben: Offizielles Helm Chart und Configuration as Code","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Kubernetes","Helm","GitOps","Configuration as Code","IAM","SSO"],"date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor v4: Eine neue Konsole, ein Marktplatz und OAuth f\xfcr MCP-Server","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-v4"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor tritt der CNCF Cloud Native Landscape bei","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"}},"content":"Casdoor verf\xfcgt \xfcber ein offizielles Helm Chart. Es befindet sich im Hauptrepository unter [`manifests/casdoor`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor), wird bei jedem Casdoor-Release im GHCR ver\xf6ffentlicht und ist auf [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) gelistet. Ab Casdoor v4.18.0 kann das Chart auch Ihre Organisationen, Anwendungen, Benutzer und Provider als deklarative Konfiguration enthalten: \xc4ndern Sie diese in der values-Datei, f\xfchren Sie `helm upgrade` aus, und die laufenden Pods wenden die \xc4nderung ohne Neustart an.\\n\\nDieser Beitrag f\xfchrt durch eine Kubernetes-Bereitstellung vom ersten `helm install` bis hin zu einem Setup, das Sie in Git speichern k\xf6nnen.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Installation {#install}\\n\\nSie ben\xf6tigen Helm v3.8+ und einen Cluster:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor\\n```\\n\\nDie Chart-Version entspricht der Casdoor-Version, daher installiert `--version 4.18.0` Casdoor 4.18.0. Ohne Ingress oder Gateway ist der Dienst nur innerhalb des Clusters erreichbar. Um es auszuprobieren, leiten Sie einen Port weiter:\\n\\n```shell\\nkubectl port-forward svc/casdoor 8000:8000\\n```\\n\\n\xd6ffnen Sie dann `http://localhost:8000`. Beim ersten Besuch fragt Casdoor Sie nach dem Passwort f\xfcr `built-in/admin`; melden Sie sich dann damit an. Siehe [Anmeldung in der Admin-Konsole](/docs/basic/server-installation#sign-in).\\n\\n## Eine Produktionsumgebung {#a-production-setup}\\n\\nDie Standardeinstellungen sind f\xfcr einen schnellen Test gedacht: Casdoor speichert seine Daten in SQLite im Container, sodass sie verloren gehen, wenn der Pod ersetzt wird. F\xfcr eine echte Bereitstellung legen Sie die folgenden Einstellungen in einer `values.yaml`-Datei ab und installieren Sie mit `helm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor -f values.yaml`.\\n\\n### Externe Datenbank {#external-database}\\n\\n```yaml\\ndatabase:\\n  driver: postgres # or mysql, cockroachdb\\n  host: postgres.db.svc.cluster.local\\n  user: casdoor\\n  password: change-me\\n  databaseName: casdoor\\n  sslMode: require\\n```\\n\\nUm das Datenbankpasswort nicht in der values-Datei abzulegen, setzen Sie die gesamte `app.conf` in ein Secret und definieren Sie `configFromSecret` mit dessen Namen.\\n\\n### Casdoor verf\xfcgbar machen {#exposing-casdoor}\\n\\nDas Chart unterst\xfctzt sowohl ein klassisches Ingress als auch die Gateway API. Mit einem Ingress-Controller und cert-manager:\\n\\n```yaml\\ningress:\\n  enabled: true\\n  className: nginx\\n  annotations:\\n    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt-prod\\n  hosts:\\n    - host: door.example.com\\n      paths:\\n        - path: /\\n          pathType: Prefix\\n  tls:\\n    - secretName: casdoor-tls\\n      hosts:\\n        - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nMit der Gateway API (Istio, Envoy Gateway, Cilium, Kong, NGINX Gateway Fabric und andere) h\xe4ngen Sie eine `HTTPRoute` an ein vorhandenes Gateway an:\\n\\n```yaml\\ngatewayApi:\\n  enabled: true\\n  parentRefs:\\n    - name: my-gateway\\n      namespace: gateway-system\\n  hostnames:\\n    - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nDas Chart kann auch das Gateway selbst erstellen und eine HTTP-zu-HTTPS-Umleitung hinzuf\xfcgen, siehe den [Helm-Leitfaden](/docs/basic/try-with-helm#exposing-casdoor).\\n\\n### Mehr als eine Replik {#more-than-one-replica}\\n\\nCasdoor speichert Anmelde-Sitzungen standardm\xe4\xdfig lokal auf der Festplatte. Bevor Sie mehr als eine Replik betreiben, verweisen Sie alle auf denselben Redis, damit ein Benutzer angemeldet bleibt, unabh\xe4ngig davon, welcher Pod die Anfrage bearbeitet. Jede `app.conf`-Einstellung kann als Umgebungsvariable \xfcbergeben werden, sodass der Endpunkt aus einem Secret stammen kann:\\n\\n```yaml\\nreplicaCount: 3\\n\\nenvFromSecret:\\n  - name: redisEndpoint\\n    secretName: casdoor-redis\\n    key: endpoint # host:port, or host:port,db,password\\n```\\n\\nDerselbe Redis wird f\xfcr den Status des OAuth-Ger\xe4teflusses verwendet. Sobald Redis eingerichtet ist, f\xfcgt `autoscaling.enabled: true` einen HorizontalPodAutoscaler hinzu.\\n\\n## Configuration as Code {#configuration-as-code}\\n\\nOrganisationen und Anwendungen in der Web-Oberfl\xe4che zusammenzuklicken ist f\xfcr einen ersten Test in Ordnung, aber die meisten Teams m\xf6chten das Setup ihres Identit\xe4tsanbieters wie den Rest ihrer Infrastruktur \xfcberpr\xfcfen und versionieren. Die `initData`-Werte tun genau das:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  data:\\n    organizations:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: acme\\n        displayName: Acme\\n        passwordType: bcrypt\\n    applications:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: app-acme-portal\\n        organization: acme\\n        displayName: Acme Portal\\n        redirectUris:\\n          - https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\n    users:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: alice\\n        displayName: Alice\\n        password: change-me\\n        signupApplication: app-acme-portal\\n    roles:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: admins\\n        displayName: Admins\\n        users: [acme/alice]\\n        isEnabled: true\\n```\\n\\nDas Chart speichert die Daten in einem Secret und bindet sie in die Pods ein. Casdoor wendet sie beim Start an und pr\xfcft sie dann alle 30 Sekunden (`initData.watchInterval`). Wenn Sie die Daten \xe4ndern und `helm upgrade` ausf\xfchren, aktualisiert kubelet das eingebundene Secret, und die laufenden Pods wenden es ohne Neustart an.\\n\\nEinige Regeln machen die parallele Nutzung neben der Web-Oberfl\xe4che sicher:\\n\\n- **Nur die von Ihnen geschriebenen Felder werden verwaltet.** Ein bereits vorhandenes Objekt erh\xe4lt die in der Datei festgelegten Felder; seine anderen Felder behalten die Werte bei, die in der Benutzeroberfl\xe4che bearbeitet wurden. Sie k\xf6nnen nur die Redirect URIs einer Anwendung verwalten und den Rest der Admin-Konsole \xfcberlassen.\\n- **Passw\xf6rter sind initiale Passw\xf6rter.** Das `password` eines Benutzers wird nur bei der Erstellung des Benutzers verwendet, sodass Benutzer ihr Passwort danach \xe4ndern k\xf6nnen.\\n- **Nichts wird gel\xf6scht.** Wenn ein Objekt aus der Datei entfernt wird, wird es nicht aus Casdoor entfernt.\\n- **Fehler bringen Casdoor nicht zum Absturz.** Wenn die Anwendung eines Objekts fehlschl\xe4gt, wird der Fehler protokolliert und Casdoor bleibt im Dienst; die Daten werden bei jeder \xdcberpr\xfcfung erneut versucht, bis sie angewendet werden.\\n\\nDas Dateiformat ist dasjenige, das Casdoor seit jeher f\xfcr [Dateninitialisierung](/docs/deployment/data-initialization) verwendet: Organisationen, Anwendungen, Benutzer, Anbieter, Zertifikate, Rollen, Berechtigungen, Gruppen, LDAP-Server, Webhooks, Pl\xe4ne, Preise und mehr, mit denselben Feldnamen wie die REST-API. Ein schneller Einstiegsweg besteht darin, eine bestehende Instanz mit `casdoor -export` zu exportieren und die Objekte beizubehalten, die verwaltet werden sollen.\\n\\nUm die Daten aus der values-Datei herauszuhalten, z. B. wenn sie von Sealed Secrets oder External Secrets verwaltet wird, erstellen Sie das Secret selbst:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  existingSecret: casdoor-init-data\\n  existingSecretKey: init_data.yaml\\n```\\n\\nDieselbe Funktion funktioniert auch ohne Kubernetes. Mounten Sie eine JSON- oder YAML-Datei und setzen Sie:\\n\\n```ini\\ninitDataFile = /init-data/init_data.yaml\\ninitDataMerge = true\\ninitDataWatchInterval = 30\\n```\\n\\n## Oder verwenden Sie Terraform {#or-use-terraform}\\n\\nWenn Sie `terraform plan`, Importe vorhandener Objekte und Drift-Erkennung bevorzugen, verwaltet der offizielle [Terraform-Anbieter](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/casdoor/casdoor) dieselben Objekte \xfcber die Casdoor-API:\\n\\n```terraform\\nresource \\"casdoor_application\\" \\"portal\\" {\\n  name          = \\"app-acme-portal\\"\\n  organization  = \\"acme\\"\\n  redirect_uris = [\\"https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\"]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nVerwenden Sie Helm values, wenn die Konfiguration zusammen mit dem Deployment ausgeliefert werden soll, und Terraform, wenn Casdoor eines von vielen Systemen ist, die Sie bereits damit verwalten. Siehe den [Terraform-Leitfaden](/docs/deployment/terraform).\\n\\n## Migration vom alten Chart {#coming-from-the-old-chart}\\n\\nBis v4.15.0 wurde das Chart als `oci://registry-1.docker.io/casbin/casdoor-helm-charts` ver\xf6ffentlicht, und das separate Repository `casdoor/casdoor-helm` ist nun archiviert. Das Docker-Hub-Chart erh\xe4lt weiterhin jede Ver\xf6ffentlichung, aber neue Installationen sollten das GHCR-Chart verwenden. Die Migration einer bestehenden Release ist ein regul\xe4res Upgrade, und die Ressourcennamen bleiben gleich:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm upgrade casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor --version <version>\\n```\\n\\n## Links\\n\\n- Helm chart: [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [source](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor)\\n- Guides: [Try with Helm](/docs/basic/try-with-helm) \xb7 [Data initialization and configuration as code](/docs/deployment/data-initialization#configuration-as-code) \xb7 [Terraform](/docs/deployment/terraform)\\n- Sie m\xf6chten es nicht selbst betreiben? [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing?utm_source=casdoor.ai&utm_medium=blog&utm_content=kubernetes) bietet Ihnen eine dedizierte Casdoor-Instanz, die wir hosten und f\xfcr Sie aktuell halten."},{"id":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","title":"Casdoor tritt der CNCF Cloud Native Landscape bei","description":"Am 22. Februar 2026 ist Casdoor der CNCF Cloud Native Landscape beigetreten, in der Kategorie Security & Compliance unter Provisioning. Die Landscape vereint weit verbreitete cloud-native Projekte und Produkte; die Aufnahme hilft Nutzern und Unternehmen, L\xf6sungen f\xfcr den Aufbau cloud-nativer Systeme zu entdecken und zu bewerten.","date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CNCF","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/cncf"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cloud Native","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/cloud-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"Landscape","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/landscape"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":2.02,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","title":"Casdoor tritt der CNCF Cloud Native Landscape bei","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CNCF","Cloud Native","Landscape","IAM","SSO","Security"],"date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor auf Kubernetes betreiben: Offizielles Helm Chart und Configuration as Code","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"},"nextItem":{"title":"Verbesserung der App-Sicherheit und Benutzererfahrung mit Casdoor Face Authentifizierung SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"Am 22. Februar 2026 ist **Casdoor** der [CNCF Cloud Native Landscape](https://landscape.cncf.io/) beigetreten, in der Kategorie **Security & Compliance** unter Provisioning. Die Landscape vereint weit verbreitete cloud-native Projekte und Produkte; die Aufnahme hilft Nutzern und Unternehmen, L\xf6sungen f\xfcr den Aufbau cloud-nativer Systeme zu entdecken und zu bewerten.\\n\\n[https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<img src=\\"/img/blog/cncf-landscape-casdoor.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor on CNCF Landscape - Security and Compliance section\\" style={{maxWidth: \'100%\', height: \'auto\', border: \'1px solid #eee\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n\\n_Casdoor on the CNCF Cloud Native Landscape (Security & Compliance)_\\n\\n## \xdcber Casdoor {#about-casdoor}\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/casdoor-logo_1185x256.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nCasdoor ist eine Open-Source-, UI-first Identity and Access Management (IAM) / Single Sign-On (SSO)-Plattform mit einer Web-Benutzeroberfl\xe4che. Sie unterst\xfctzt OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML und CAS sowie viele Social Logins (Google, GitHub, WeChat usw.) und hilft Ihnen, Benutzermanagement und SSO in wenigen Schritten aufzubauen. Casdoor wird von [Casbin](https://casbin.org) in Golang und React entwickelt. Es nutzt eine Architektur mit Trennung von Frontend und Backend, unterst\xfctzt hohe Nebenl\xe4ufigkeit und wird aktiv von der Community gepflegt. Das Projekt hat \xfcber 13k GitHub-Sterne erhalten.\\n\\n## \xdcber CNCF und die Cloud Native Landscape {#about-cncf-and-the-cloud-native-landscape}\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1.5rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://www.cncf.io/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/cncf-main-site-logo.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF - Cloud Native Computing Foundation\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n<img src=\\"https://landscape.cncf.io/images/logo_header.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF Cloud Native Landscape\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nDie **Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)** ist ein Linux-Foundation-Projekt, das cloud-native Technologien f\xf6rdert. Ihre Vision ist es, cloud native allgegenw\xe4rtig zu machen. Die CNCF pflegt die **Cloud Native Landscape** ([https://landscape.cncf.io](https://landscape.cncf.io)), die Projekte und Produkte im cloud-native \xd6kosystem klassifiziert und verfolgt und als Referenzkarte f\xfcr Entwickler und Unternehmen dient.\\n\\n## Ausblick {#looking-ahead}\\n\\nDer Beitritt zur CNCF Landscape spiegelt Fortschritte in der Reife und Community von Casdoor wider. Wir werden Casdoor und seine Integration in cloud-native Workflows weiterhin verbessern und freuen uns \xfcber weitere Nutzer und Mitwirkende, die der Community beitreten.\\n\\nVerwandte Links:\\n\\n- [Casdoor](https://casdoor.org) \xb7 [GitHub](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [Dokumentation](https://casdoor.org/docs) \xb7 [CNCF Landscape \u2013 Casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?selected=casdoor)"},{"id":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","title":"Verbesserung der App-Sicherheit und Benutzererfahrung mit Casdoor Face Authentifizierung SSO","description":"In einer Zeit, in der digitales Identit\xe4tsmanagement von entscheidender Bedeutung ist, revolutionieren Single Sign-On (SSO) L\xf6sungen wie Casdoor die Interaktion zwischen Nutzern und verschiedenen Anwendungen. Das SSO von Casdoor vereinfacht den Login-Prozess, indem es Benutzern erm\xf6glicht, sich nur einmal zu authentifizieren, um auf mehrere Dienste zuzugreifen. Dies erh\xf6ht nicht nur die Benutzerfreundlichkeit, sondern erh\xf6ht auch das Engagement und die Zufriedenheit.","date":"2023-12-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Single-Sign-On","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/single-sign-on"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face Recogniztion","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/face-recogniztion"},{"inline":true,"label":"FaceID","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/face-id"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/face"},{"inline":true,"label":"Machine Learning","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/machine-learning"}],"readingTime":1.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"James Lawson","title":"Product Manager","url":"https://casdoor.org","imageURL":"https://cdn.casbin.com/casdoor/static/admin.png","key":"James Lawson","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","title":"Verbesserung der App-Sicherheit und Benutzererfahrung mit Casdoor Face Authentifizierung SSO","authors":["James Lawson"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Single-Sign-On","Face Recogniztion","FaceID","Face","Machine Learning"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor tritt der CNCF Cloud Native Landscape bei","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"},"nextItem":{"title":"Willkommen","permalink":"/de/blog/welcome"}},"content":"In einer Zeit, in der digitales Identit\xe4tsmanagement von entscheidender Bedeutung ist, revolutionieren Single Sign-On (SSO) L\xf6sungen wie Casdoor die Interaktion zwischen Nutzern und verschiedenen Anwendungen. Das SSO von Casdoor vereinfacht den Login-Prozess, indem es Benutzern erm\xf6glicht, sich nur einmal zu authentifizieren, um auf mehrere Dienste zuzugreifen. Dies erh\xf6ht nicht nur die Benutzerfreundlichkeit, sondern erh\xf6ht auch das Engagement und die Zufriedenheit.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nF\xfcr Unternehmen optimiert das SSO die Verwaltung von Benutzeranmeldungen, erh\xf6ht die Sicherheit und vereinfacht neue Benutzer an Bord. Durch die Zentralisierung der Benutzerverwaltung kann das Risiko mit schwachen oder recycelten Passw\xf6rtern gedrosselt werden.\\n\\nAngesichts wachsender Online-Bedrohungen ist jedoch die Integration robuster Sicherheitsma\xdfnahmen in SSO-Systemen wie Casdoor unerl\xe4sslich. Hier kommt die Gesichtauthentifizierungstechnologie ins Spiel, indem sie eine zus\xe4tzliche Schutzschicht bei der Aufrechterhaltung benutzerfreundlicher Interaktionen hinzuf\xfcgt.\\n\\nFace Authentifizierung verwendet einzigartige Gesichtsmerkmale, um Benutzeridentit\xe4ten zu best\xe4tigen. Es vergleicht Live-Bilder mit gespeicherten Gesichtsvorlagen und bietet eine sichere und unkomplizierte M\xf6glichkeit, Benutzer zu authentifizieren. Diese Methode zeichnet sich durch hohe Genauigkeit auch unter unterschiedlichen Bedingungen aus und wird in modernen Endger\xe4ten weit verbreitet.\\n\\nDie Integration der Gesichtserkennung in Casdoor SSO-System verspricht eine sichere, effiziente und nahtlose Benutzererfahrung. Um eine optimale Leistung zu gew\xe4hrleisten, ist es von entscheidender Bedeutung, sich auf folgende Bereiche zu konzentrieren:\\n\\n1. _Anti-Spoofing Ma\xdfnahmen_: Die Umsetzung der Lebenserkennung, wie das Aufsp\xfcren von Augenbewegungen oder zuf\xe4lligen Gesten, ist unerl\xe4sslich, um den Fehlversuchen entgegenzuwirken und die Systemintegrit\xe4t zu wahren.\\n\\n2. _Regelm\xe4\xdfige Updates zur Verbesserung der Genauigkeit_: Die Aktualisierung der Gesicht-Authentifizierungstechnologie mit den neuesten KI und biometrischen Fortschritten gew\xe4hrleistet Genauigkeit und verbessert das Nutzererlebnis. Es ist wichtig, verschiedene Datens\xe4tze f\xfcr Algorithmustraining zu verwenden, um Vorurteile zu vermeiden.\\n\\n3. _Ausgewogenheit von Sicherheit und Bequemlichkeit_: Die besten Systeme bieten eine perfekte Balance zwischen strengen Sicherheitsma\xdfnahmen und Benutzerfreundlichkeit. Dieser Ansatz ermutigt die Benutzer zu adoptieren, indem er einen einfachen Authentifizierungsprozess sicherstellt, ohne die Sicherheit zu beeintr\xe4chtigen.\\n\\nZusammenfassend l\xe4sst sich sagen, dass Unternehmen durch die Integration der Gesichtserkennung in das SSO von Casdoor ein sicheres, effizientes und benutzerfreundliches Anmeldeerlebnis anbieten k\xf6nnen. Dies st\xe4rkt nicht nur die Sicherheit, sondern passt sich auch den Erwartungen der modernen Nutzer in der digitalen Welt an."},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","title":"Willkommen","description":"Willkommen auf der offiziellen Casdoor Webseite!\u{1F44B}","date":"2021-08-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Authentication","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/authentication"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth2.0","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"Casbin","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casbin"}],"readingTime":1.52,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"ErikQQY","title":"Casbin Member","url":"https://github.com/ErikQQY","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52615090?s=120&v=4","key":"ErikQQY","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Willkommen","authors":["ErikQQY"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Authentication","OAuth2.0","Casbin"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Verbesserung der App-Sicherheit und Benutzererfahrung mit Casdoor Face Authentifizierung SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"Willkommen auf der offiziellen Casdoor Webseite!\u{1F44B}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Was ist Casdoor? {#what-is-casdoor}\\n\\nCasdoor ist eine UI-first [Identity Access Management (IAM)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Identity_management) / [Single-Sign-On (SSO)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_sign-on)-Plattform, die auf OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML und CAS basiert.\\n\\nCasdoor wird von [Casbin](https://casbin.org) mit Golang und React entwickelt.\\n\\nZiel von Casdoor ist es, eine leistungsstarke Authentifizierungsplattform f\xfcr die Benutzerverwaltung bereitzustellen.\\n\\n## Warum Casdoor? {#why-use-casdoor}\\n\\n* \u26A1 Leistungsstarke Funktion und einfache Konfiguration. With support for  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_google.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_github.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_facebook.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_twitter.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_linkedin.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_weibo.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wechat.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_qq.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_dingtalk.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>   <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitee.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wecom.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img><img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitlab.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> sign in and traditional username/password sign in, Casdoor helps you implement your users management system just in a few steps.\\n\\n* \u{1F4A5} Front-End-Back-End-Trennarchitektur. Casdoor bietet eine man\xf6vrierf\xe4hige Web-Benutzeroberfl\xe4che und unterst\xfctzt eine hohe Parallelit\xe4t.\\n\\n* \u{1F942} Aktive Open Source Community. Casdoor ist Open Source und wird aktiv gepflegt.\\n\\n* \u{1F310} Mehrsprachige Unterst\xfctzung. Casdoor unterst\xfctzt mehrere Sprachen, darunter Deutsch, Chinesisch, Englisch, Franz\xf6sisch und viele mehr.\\n\\n\\n## Wie benutze ich Casdoor? {#how-to-use-casdoor}\\n\\nSie k\xf6nnen dem detaillierten Dokument folgen, um mehr \xfcber die [Casdoor API](https://door.casdoor.com/swagger/) zu erfahren und [Ihr eigenes Casdoor einzurichten](/docs/basic/server-installation). Oder nutzen Sie die [Casdoor SDKs](/docs/how-to-connect/sdk), um Ihre eigene Anwendung zu implementieren."}]}}')}}]);